// ── THE IN-BAND RECOVERY ACCESS ROUTE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE GAP THIS MODULE CLOSES. gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_secure answers AccessDiscover through
// BmcSecure, which needs a credential -- either a managed secret we hold or the published factory
// one. gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_rotation_route rotates FROM a working credential. So a board whose
// Administrator password is unknown has no route at all: every existing arm asks, first, for the
// thing that is missing. This module models the one route that starts without it -- reach the
// machine's own host OS, write an temporary Administrator password over the in-band system interface
// (SSIF), then actuate a factory reset over the BMC's OUT-OF-BAND management interface -- and it
// terminates at a PROOF CARRIER saying that happened. It terminates there, and only there: no
// bmc_secure fold, no bmc_onboard fold and no arrival entry consumes that carrier today (git grep;
// gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route_frontier rosters the missing onboarding consumer as a
// pending frontier row). Claiming the route "hands the unit to onboarding" would name a consumer that
// does not exist; the standing is the proof that recovery happened, nothing more.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. Ruled with the cut-2 owner (gunbc.host.host_control_route /
// gunbc.host.bmc_control_binding, PR #14063): this is not a control route and not a third arm of
// HostControlRoute. The arms there answer "how do we drive this host's controller out-of-band"; this
// answers "how do we get the controller back", and it does not terminate in a binding -- it
// terminates in a BmcSecureStanding, and the ordinary join mints the binding. This module imports
// gunbc.host.bmc_control_binding nowhere, mints no binding, and mints no credential store. The
// dependency is one-way and short: this module -> BmcSecureStanding -> the control-route join.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING FACT IS THE HOST OS, NOT THE MEDIUM (DESIGN §4d). Recovery is possible only
// because a rescue OS is running on the board and can reach /dev/ipmi0. Whether that OS arrived on a
// USB stick or over the network is a realisation of that ONE fact, so nothing here is named for USB:
// the precondition is RescueHostReachability, and media are providers of it -- one executed, one
// declared. Without a reachable rescue host there is no route, and this module says so with a named
// cause rather than modelling a recovery that needs somebody to walk to the machine.

// ── THE MEASURED FIRMWARE POLICY THAT MAKES THE RESET NECESSARY ────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FACT THAT DECIDES THE ACTUATION, AND WHERE IT LIVES. Writing the published factory password
// to the Administrator account is refused by the firmware we measured -- completion code 0xCC,
// "Invalid data field in request" -- on both the 8-byte and the 20-byte form, which is the
// observation that rules password length out as the explanation. A refused Set User Password changes
// nothing, so the unit stays locked: the route cannot be "write the factory password, log in", it has
// to be a factory reset.
//
// That refusal is a property of ONE VENDOR BUILD, so it is not stated here (and not in
// extdeps.bmc.openbmc either, which would make it a rule about OpenBMC in general). It is a per-
// release row in extdeps.boards.asrock_rack, keyed by the catalog's own typed BmcFirmwareVersion
// beside the release's other capabilities, and this module only ever ASKS for the release it is
// looking at: asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_for. A release the catalog
// lacks answers NotMeasured, and NotMeasured makes the route REFUSE (DESIGN §4d -- "we never read
// this firmware" is not a licence to assert recovery is possible). The completion code arrives as a
// typed value from the IPMI specification's completion-code list (extdeps.bmc.ipmi), with the English
// as its description: a free string would let "Request data length invalid" (0xC7), a complaint about
// the request LENGTH, be cited as the policy refusal and license a destructive ResetAll on a unit
// whose firmware would have accepted the write.

fn recovery_measured_refusal_policy() -> PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy {
  asrock_altrad8ud_openbmc_3_22_00_published_factory_password_write_policy
}

// ── STEP 1: THE RESCUE MEDIUM, AS A PROVIDER OF THE REACHABLE HOST ─────────────────────────────
//
// The USB stick is not the precondition; the running rescue OS is. The medium is recorded because it
// is what makes that OS exist, and because the two ways of getting one are NOT at the same rung: the
// USB rescue has been executed and captured (an operator inserted a prepared Ubuntu live USB and the
// host took DHCP at its address); network boot has never served a real host --
// gunbc.network_boot_delivery says so of itself. Modelling both as equal would let a PXE path that
// has never delivered a kernel ground a recovery, which is exactly the fabricated-possibility class
// DESIGN §4d refuses. So a declared provider admits nothing: it can never satisfy
// RescueHostReachable, and its refusal is named SEPARATELY from "there is no rescue host", so a
// reader can tell "we have not built this provider" from "this unit has no way in".
// WHAT AN EXECUTED MEDIUM PROVES, AS A RECORD AND NOT ONLY AS AN ARM'S FIELDS. An executed rescue boot is
// witnessed by an EvidenceRef and an instant, those two and nothing else -- and RescueHostReachable.medium
// below is typed with THIS, which is why the reachability cannot carry a declared provider: the declared
// arm has no such record to hand over. It also means the reachability cannot carry evidence that disagrees
// with the medium it rests on, because there is one copy of those two fields, not three.
type ExecutedRescueMedium { evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: CapturedInstant }

type RescueHostMedium
  = UsbRescueMediumObserved { medium: ExecutedRescueMedium }
  | NetbootRescueMediumDeclared

// WHETHER A RESCUE MEDIUM HAS EVER BEEN RUN IS THE ARM IT IS, NOT A BOOL. A true/false answer would be
// asked at call sites that could each ask it differently, and the destructive gates downstream read this
// answer. The decision is made once, in rescue_host_reachable, at the edge where a caller hands a medium
// over; every value past that point has already been decided, which is why the reachability's own arm
// carries no medium question at all. `NetbootRescueMediumDeclared` has no evidence field to read, which is
// the whole enforcement: a declared provider has nothing to satisfy a reachability with.

// The driver stack the observed rescue host loads, named rather than "modules present" because each
// absence breaks the channel differently: ipmi_ssif binds the system interface (the host-to-BMC I2C
// bridge -- without it there is no in-band link at all), ipmi_msghandler is the message router,
// ipmi_devintf is what creates /dev/ipmi*, acpi_ipmi carries the ACPI-side IPMI description the
// bridge is declared through. An SSH session to a machine missing any of them reaches a Linux box
// that cannot talk to its own BMC, which is the same dead end as no session.
data recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> = [
  "ipmi_ssif",
  "ipmi_devintf",
  "ipmi_msghandler",
  "acpi_ipmi",
]

// The device the in-band channel is read through, as the TYPE the upstream plugins imply: open.c builds
// "/dev/ipmi%d" from a device NUMBER, so this is the closed set extdeps.bmc.ipmi owns. §2 rules out the
// alternative -- a string leaf hiding named parts, in a second home for a fact ipmi already carries. The
// arm is named, and the comparison below is on arms.
data recovery_ipmi_device_node: IpmiDeviceNode = IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 0 }

type InBandIpmiPresence {
  device_node: IpmiDeviceNode
  kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName>
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

// WHETHER THE IN-BAND IPMI IS THERE, AS A VERDICT THAT NAMES WHICH PART IS MISSING. A Bool here was the
// defect: it answered false for TWO different mistakes, and the refusal built from it asked a second
// function for the missing modules -- a function that returns the empty list when the DEVICE NODE is the
// problem. So a host whose /dev node was the wrong one was reported as "up, but no in-band IPMI: load the
// missing ipmi modules" with an empty list beside it: the sentence blamed drivers and named none, and the
// next action told someone to insmod nothing. One function now decides once, in the order the checks
// actually depend on (the node before the drivers, since the drivers are what create the node), and each
// mistake arrives as its own arm carrying what was seen.
type InBandIpmiPresenceVerdict
  = InBandIpmiPresenceComplete { presence: InBandIpmiPresence }
  | RecoveryDeviceNodeIsNotTheInBandNode { expected: IpmiDeviceNode, reported: IpmiDeviceNode }
  | RecoveryKernelModulesMissing { missing: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> }

fn in_band_ipmi_presence_verdict(presence: InBandIpmiPresence) -> InBandIpmiPresenceVerdict {
  if presence.device_node != recovery_ipmi_device_node {
    // BOTH SIDES ARE KEPT. "It is not the node we read the route through" is unactionable without the
    // node that WAS found: a reported /dev/imb means a different driver is loaded, which is a different
    // fix from a missing one.
    RecoveryDeviceNodeIsNotTheInBandNode {
      expected: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
      reported: presence.device_node,
    }
  } else {
    let missing = recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules
      |> filter(m => presence.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m) == false)
    if list_length(items: missing) == 0 {
      InBandIpmiPresenceComplete { presence: presence }
    } else {
      RecoveryKernelModulesMissing { missing: missing }
    }
  }
}


// THE HONEST BOUNDARY, AND WHERE "PHYSICAL-ONLY" IS STATED. With no rescue OS we can reach there is
// no route in this module, no credential at the end of it, and no pretending otherwise. The cause is
// NAMED rather than a generic refusal because the consumer is gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge:
// its AccessDiscover arm has to tell an operator "insert the rescue medium / netboot this board" from
// "the rescue host is up but has no in-band IPMI -- this unit needs different hands" from "the only
// provider we model is a declared one".
type RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS
  = NoRescueHostAddressResponded { address: Ipv4Address, ssh_port: Port }
  | RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: Ipv4Address, missing_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> }
  | RescueHostReachedAtTheWrongDeviceNode {
      address: Ipv4Address
      expected: IpmiDeviceNode
      reported: IpmiDeviceNode
    }
  // Nullary on purpose: exactly one provider of the two modelled is a declared one, so "which" is
  // answerable from the arm's name and a field would be a second, editable place to say it (§4c).
  | RescueMediumNotExecuted

// WHICH MISTAKE, DECIDED ONCE, IN THE ORDER THE FACTS DEPEND ON EACH OTHER. The medium is asked first
// because nothing below it is observable without the rescue OS the medium boots: checking drivers before
// the medium would report "no ipmi modules" for a host that is not running at all.
type RescueHostReachability
  = RescueHostReachable {
      address: Ipv4Address
      ssh_port: Port
      ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence
      // THE TYPE IS THE ENFORCEMENT. An executed record, not the medium sum: a declared netboot provider
      // has no constructor path into a reachable host, so no consumer downstream can be handed one and no
      // check has to remember to ask. Widening this to RescueHostMedium would make that value writable and
      // need a second match to catch it -- a check for a state the model was allowed to create, which is
      // §5 spent backwards. The evidence and instant are not repeated here either: they are the medium's,
      // held once, so a reachability cannot disagree with the medium it rests on.
      medium: ExecutedRescueMedium
    }
  | RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }

fn recovery_host_refusal(reachability: RescueHostReachability) -> RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS? {
  match reachability {
    // No question asked, because none can be posed: the arm carries an ExecutedRescueMedium, which is
    // what a reachability is only ever built with.
    RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _ } => none
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: c } => Present { value: c }
  }
}

// THE ONLY WAY A REACHABLE RESCUE HOST ENTERS THE MODEL. A rescue host that is "reachable" because a
// caller typed an address is the assertion this whole step exists to refuse, so nothing constructs
// RescueHostReachable except here -- and here the constructor checks its own inputs: the medium must
// be an executed one and the driver stack must actually be the one claimed. The port is NOT range-checked
// here: its type is std.types.Port, `Int where range(min: 1, max: 65535)`, which the tree already declares
// and this module already imports, so a check here would be a second representation of a constraint the
// model carries (§5). It would also misreport: an out-of-range value arriving as RescueHostUnreachable /
// NoRescueHostAddressResponded tells an operator a host "did not respond" for an address that was never
// dialed -- a fabricated observation about a network.
// The executed check happens twice by design (here, and again in recovery_host_refusal) because the
// second is the one the standing reads, and a reader of the standing must not have to know how the
// value was built.
fn rescue_host_reachable(
  address: Ipv4Address,
  ssh_port: Port,
  ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence,
  medium: RescueHostMedium,
) -> RescueHostReachability {
  // ONE DECISION PER FACT, EACH IN ITS OWN MATCH, AND THE REFUSALS CARRY WHAT WAS SEEN. The medium first:
  // a declared provider has no record to hand over, and everything else here presupposes the OS it boots.
  match medium {
    NetbootRescueMediumDeclared => RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RescueMediumNotExecuted }
    UsbRescueMediumObserved { medium: executed } => match in_band_ipmi_presence_verdict(presence: ipmi) {
      // The node check runs before the driver check, so a host reporting the wrong node is refused as the
      // wrong node, with both nodes named -- not as "modules missing", with an empty list beside it.
      RecoveryDeviceNodeIsNotTheInBandNode { expected: e, reported: r } =>
        RescueHostUnreachable {
          cause: RescueHostReachedAtTheWrongDeviceNode { address: address, expected: e, reported: r },
        }
      RecoveryKernelModulesMissing { missing: m } =>
        RescueHostUnreachable {
          cause: RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: address, missing_modules: m },
        }
      InBandIpmiPresenceComplete { presence: proven } => RescueHostReachable {
        address: address,
        ssh_port: ssh_port,
        ipmi: proven,
        medium: executed,
      }
    }
  }
}

// ── STEP 2: THE IN-BAND CHANNEL, OBSERVED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS ROUTE EXIST AT ALL: the SSIF system interface needs no credential. On the captured unit every
// call was made against /dev/ipmi0 with no -I lanplus and no user name or password on the command
// line, and it still read the Administrator account out of the controller. That is the whole reason a
// board with an unknown password is reachable: the host-to-BMC interface inside the chassis
// authenticates by PHYSICAL POSITION, not by a secret. So the fact is not a comment beside a
// function -- it is a type, and it is the type the route admits on.
// WHY THE CREDENTIAL QUESTION IS IN THE ARMS AND NOT IN A CHECK. A passwordless arm carrying a credential
// word list is a type that can hold "passwordless, with -P 0penBmc on the argv", and every consumer would
// have to remember to ask -- including the destructive ResetAll gate. The transport side of this same
// question was decided the same way, for the same reason: extdeps/bmc/ipmi.dag records that a consumer
// which forgot to ask "failed OPEN". §5: "Stronger than catching a wrong state is making it UNWRITABLE --
// correctness by construction, not validation."
//
// So the credential-bearing command line is now its OWN arm, and the argv is decided ONCE by
// in_band_channel_from_argv: words containing -U/-P (ipmitool's user-name and password options,
// src/ipmitool.c) route to the credential-bearing arm, an argv without them routes to the passwordless
// arm, which therefore has NO field a credential could be written into. The refused arms are not widened
// into the admitted one anywhere: there is no function in this module that turns a
// CredentialCarriedOnArgv into an InBandChannelPasswordless, so a consumer cannot forget.
type InBandAccessWithoutCredential
  = InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: InBandHostTransport }
  | InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv {
      transport: InBandHostTransport
      credential_option: IpmiArgvCredentialOption
    }
  // THE PARSE'S REFUSAL SUM, CARRIED — NOT A SENTENCE ABOUT IT (DESIGN section 4c). in_band_command_from_argv
  // already tells six causes apart, and this arm used to collapse all six into one prose `detail` that the
  // claims then grepped by substring: a wording change silently voided six controls, two causes could be
  // swapped with no row turning red, and one check was merely string_length(d) > 0 -- green for any refusal
  // at all. §5 wants "a failing path [that] fails with a typed, located diagnostic" and "a discriminating
  // input that goes red when the behavior is wrong"; a sum rebuilt from the words of a string is neither.
  | InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: InBandCommandRefusal }

// THE OPTIONS THAT PUT A CREDENTIAL IN REACH OF THE WRITE. Declared rather than matched as strings at each
// call site, for the same reason the transport names are: an in-band tag compared by string equality is a
// sum with one member nobody wrote down, and a call site that mistypes "-P" as "-p" admits the write.
// -U is here because naming the remote user is a credential-bearing invocation; -f is here because ipmitool
// reads the remote session PASSWORD out of the file named by its argument ("-f file  Read remote session
// password from file", lib/ipmi_main.c usage) -- the old scan missed it, and a fixture proved the miss.
type IpmiArgvCredentialOption
  = IpmiArgvUserName            // -U <user>       lib/ipmi_main.c OPTION_STRING "U:", case 'U'
  | IpmiArgvPassword            // -P <password>   OPTION_STRING "P:", case 'P'
  | IpmiArgvPasswordFile        // -f <file>       OPTION_STRING "f:", case 'f'
  | IpmiArgvPasswordFromEnv     // -E, no argument: the password comes from IPMI_PASSWORD, case 'E'

// ══ THE ONE AUTHORITY OVER WHAT A CAPTURED ipmitool INVOCATION MEANS ══════════
// (merry-fox-82, HOLD blocker 3: "one command authority for transport+credential").
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY THE OLD SHAPE WAS NOT ONE. Transport came from `interface_name`, a string
// carried BESIDE the words; the credential came from a separate scan for the words "-U"/"-P"/"-E"
// ANYWHERE in the words. Two readers, two notions of the same command, and nothing joined them. Their own
// fixtures are the proof: the claim over the credential scan ran words carrying `-I lanplus -f /tmp/pw
// -H 10.0.0.5` while the transport was supplied as "open" beside them, and was read as the passwordless
// OpenIPMI channel -- a credential write over an authenticating out-of-band transport, which is the exact
// "failed OPEN" failure the transport sums in extdeps/bmc/ipmi.dag exist to prevent, reintroduced one
// module up. `-f` was not in the scan at all.
//
// WHAT UPSTREAM ACTUALLY DOES, which is what this walk copies. ipmitool parses with PLAIN getopt
// (lib/ipmi_main.c:371 -- there is no getopt_long, so `--password` is an unknown WORD, not an option)
// against OPTION_STRING "I:46hVvcgsEKYao:H:d:P:f:U:p:C:L:A:t:T:m:z:S:l:b:B:e:k:y:O:R:N:D:Z"
// (lib/ipmi_main.c:82), which says two things this module now refuses on:
//   - letters with a colon take a value, letters without one take none. An option that needs a value and
//     gets the end of the words is upstream's error, not a pass.
//   - an unrecognised option reaches getopt's `default:`, which prints the usage and aborts before the
//     command runs. So "this word may be an option we have not modelled" is a REFUSAL here, matching
//     upstream -- never a verb.
// Beyond the option letters there is no syntax: ipmitool does not re-order and does not group; its
// interface is chosen by the LAST -I seen (case 'I' assigns intfname on every hit, ipmi_intf_load runs
// only after the loop), so `-I open -I lanplus` IS lanplus; and a credential anywhere on the line reaches
// the write no matter which word the verb is. Those are exactly the two facts the old split could not
// represent. Here the option and its argument are carried together through the walk, so the word after -I
// is consumed as an argument and can never be read as a verb or as a credential.
//
// HOW MUCH IS MODELLED. Of the 33 letters, these can change what this route is about to do: -I (the
// transport), -U -P -f -E (a credential), -H (which machine), -N -S -D (values that tune the exchange but
// not its kind), -Z (a display switch). Those are modelled with their arity, as EXACT option words --
// getopt's attached-value spelling ("-P0penBmc") is refused rather than guessed at, because guessing where
// a word divides is the kind of reading the capture does not support. Every other letter -- -k/-y/-K (the
// IPMIv2 Kg key), -a (prompt for the password), -C/-L/-A (cipher, privilege, auth type), -t/-T/-b/-B/-l/-m
// (bridging), -p/-R (port, retries), the verbosity/cipher/quiet letters, -O/-o/-z/-e, -4/-6 -- is REFUSED
// by name rather than guessed at, because each of them changes how a write to the BMC is authenticated or
// where it is delivered. Refusing is also the honest treatment of upstream's two OPTION_STRING builds (the
// narrow build drops most letters): nothing here claims a letter exists that this ipmitool may not accept.
//
// WHY NO -I IS A REFUSAL AND NOT A DEFAULT. With no -I, ipmitool loads the interface named by DEFAULT_INTF,
// which ./configure bakes into the binary from whichever drivers it found (--with-intf=...; "open" only
// when OpenIPMI is built, otherwise lan). That is a fact about whatever binary happens to be installed on
// the rescue OS, not a fact about the captured invocation, and guessing it in front of a destructive reset
// is how a wrong transport reaches a write. The capture's README says "NO -I lanplus" -- an observation of
// absence, not evidence of a compiled default -- and 02's own header reads "no -I lanplus, no credential".
// So the module refuses the shape; the words that stand for the capture carry `-I open`, the OpenIPMI
// driver over the /dev/ipmi0 node 02 records together with ipmi_ssif/ipmi_devintf loaded, and the claim
// below asserts the no--I words are REFUSED rather than defaulted.
type IpmiCommandInvocation
  = IpmiCommandInBand { transport: InBandHostTransport, credential: IpmiArgvCredentialOption? }
  | IpmiCommandNotInBand { wire: String, why_not_in_band: NonEmptyStr }
  | IpmiCommandUnrecognised { wire: String }
  | IpmiCommandTransportAbsent { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | IpmiCommandUnmodelledOption { option: String, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | IpmiCommandOptionNeedsValue { option: String, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | IpmiCommandRemoteHostWithInBandName { host: NonEmptyStr, wire: String }

// THE SIX WAYS A CAPTURED INVOCATION FAILS TO PROVE AN IN-BAND CHANNEL, AS A DECLARED SUM.
// The parse's own refusals, carried by value into the channel sum. The rule against an undeclared sum
// applies here as much as to the transport names: a cause carried as the text of a string is a sum with no
// members written down, and nothing in the compiler notices when two are swapped or when one loses its
// wording. Each arm keeps a `why` for whoever is standing at the machine; `why` is presentation, and no
// claim reads it -- every claim matches an ARM.
type InBandCommandRefusal
  = RefusalTransportAbsent { why: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusalTransportUnrecognized { wire: String, why: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusalTransportOutOfBand { wire: String, why_not_in_band: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusalUnmodelledOption { option: String, why: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusalOptionNeedsValue { option: String, why: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusalRemoteHostWithInBandName { host: NonEmptyStr, wire: String, why: NonEmptyStr }

// WHERE THE WALK IS. The state is the walk's own and no other module reads it: it exists so one pass over
// the words can carry an option and its argument together, which is what two separate scans could not do.
// `transport` is the transport sum's OWN arm (Absent until -I answers), so the in-band decision is never
// re-derived from a string at the end.
// WHAT IS WAITING FOR ITS ARGUMENT, AS A DECLARED SUM. This used to be three fields -- `pending` holding
// the word, plus `pending_is_credential` and `pending_is_host` -- and the three of them together said one
// thing. Written that way the state could hold combinations that mean nothing: credential and host both
// true, or a word set with neither. Nothing in the compiler objected, and the walk read the Bools with ==
// against the effect strings, so a tag that stopped matching silently made an argument be read as the
// wrong kind of thing. Each arm now carries the word it is waiting for (the refusals name it), and the
// kind is the arm itself -- an argument cannot be a credential and a host at once because there is no
// value that says so.
type IpmiPendingOption
  = PendingCredentialArgument { word: String }   // -U -P -f
  | PendingHostArgument { word: String }         // -H
  | PendingTransportArgument { word: String }    // -I
  | PendingTuningArgument { word: String }       // -N -S -D

// WHAT THE LAST -I LEFT BEHIND, AS A DECLARED SUM. This used to be three fields -- transport (the arm),
// transport_wire (the name) and transport_unrecognized (a Bool) -- and the three of them said one thing.
// Written that way the state could hold transport: Absent beside transport_unrecognized: true, which is
// not a thing that can happen (a name that no plugin claims IS a name that was given), and the decision
// read the Bool BEFORE the sum, so a mutation that stopped setting the Bool made an unrecognised name be
// reported as "no -I at all" -- a different refusal, naming a different cause, from the same words. The
// arm now IS the fact: Absent means no -I appeared, Unrecognised carries the name that no plugin claims,
// Named carries the arm together with the name that produced it because a refusal has to show the name.
type IpmiTransportSeen
  = TransportNamed { transport: IpmiCommandTransport, wire: String }
  | TransportNameUnrecognised { wire: String }

// WHAT THE CREDENTIAL LETTERS LEFT STANDING. credential_word ("", "-U", "-P" or "-f") beside
// file_credential: Bool and env_credential: Bool said one thing three ways, so the state could say
// "-f was the last letter" and "this was not a file credential" at once, and nothing objected. The
// precedence those three fields produced is NOT last-write-wins, which is why this is one sum with
// transitions rather than a field that is simply overwritten: a -f seen early beats a -P seen later
// (it is a different mechanism -- the secret comes from a file), while -U and -P do overwrite each
// other, and -E is beaten by an explicit letter whether it came before or after. Encoding the
// precedence in the transitions rather than in a stack of Bools is what makes the combinations that
// cannot happen unnameable -- and it is the same four-arm sum the answer already returns, so there is
// no second vocabulary for the same fact.
type IpmiCommandWalkState {
  pending: IpmiPendingOption?        // Absent, or the option awaiting its argument and what it awaits
  host_seen: Bool
  transport: IpmiTransportSeen?      // what the LAST -I named, or Absent if none appeared
  credential: IpmiArgvCredentialOption?  // what the LAST credential letter left standing, or Absent
  unmodelled: String                 // the first option word upstream's getopt would have aborted on
  verb: String                       // the first non-option word, kept for the refusal's detail
}

// THE -CREDENTIAL LETTERS, APPLIED IN ORDER. Each returns the next state of the one credential fact, so
// the precedence lives in these two functions and nowhere else. -f is absorbing: once a file of secrets
// has been named, a later -P does not demote it. -E only lands when no explicit letter has ever landed,
// which is why "an -E that a later -P overrode" and "an -E that was never overridden" are not two states
// here -- no reader could tell them apart, so the state must not either.
fn ipmi_credential_after_letter(
  seen: IpmiArgvCredentialOption?,
  letter_word: String,
) -> IpmiArgvCredentialOption? {
  if match seen {
    // a file of secrets already named is not demoted by a later letter
    Present { value: IpmiArgvPasswordFile } => true
    Absent => false
    Present { value: _ } => false
  } {
    seen
  } else if letter_word == "-P" {
    optional_present(value: IpmiArgvPassword)
  } else if letter_word == "-U" {
    optional_present(value: IpmiArgvUserName)
  } else {
    // -f, the third credential letter. A letter added to the credential group later, without a line here,
    // lands on this arm as a file credential -- the safe direction, because a credential that EXISTS makes
    // the route refuse the reset rather than let it proceed, while Absent is what reads as passwordless
    // and opens the gate. A future letter is therefore more likely to be over-refused than under-refused.
    optional_present(value: IpmiArgvPasswordFile)
  }
}

fn ipmi_credential_after_env(seen: IpmiArgvCredentialOption?) -> IpmiArgvCredentialOption? {
  // std.optional's own fold rather than a re-written if: a letter that already landed wins over the
  // environment, and -E fills the slot only when nothing has landed. Reusing the fold also keeps this
  // from being == against an Optional, which nothing else in the tree does.
  optional_prefer_first_present(
    left: seen,
    right: optional_present(value: IpmiArgvPasswordFromEnv),
  )
}

// THE CREDENTIAL TRANSITION FOR ONE OPTION WORD, as a function of the word's effect rather than as a test
// written where the record happens to be built. Same shape as the pending slot's transition above: the
// effect decides, and the arms are the kinds. EffectReadsEnv is -E, the only modelled letter that reads a
// secret from the environment; every other effect leaves whatever already landed alone.
//
// This exists as a function and not as an inline `if` inside the record literal because the DAG parser
// cannot read a labelled call in an if-branch that sits in a record field -- it reads the call's ':' as the
// start of a record literal and fails with "expected RParen, found Colon" (the same trap src/v2/test/claim/
// long/vacuity_consumer_witness_long.dag records for a variant name). Writing the decision as a function of
// the effect is both what parses and what should have been written: the transition is one fact about the
// effect, not a detail of the literal.
fn ipmi_credential_after_option_effect(
  seen: IpmiArgvCredentialOption?,
  effect: IpmiOptionEffect,
) -> IpmiArgvCredentialOption? {
  match effect {
    EffectReadsEnv => ipmi_credential_after_env(seen: seen)
    EffectPendingTransport => seen
    EffectPendingCredential => seen
    EffectPendingHost => seen
    EffectPendingTuning => seen
    EffectSkip => seen
    EffectUnmodelled => seen
  }
}

fn ipmi_command_walk_init() -> IpmiCommandWalkState {
  IpmiCommandWalkState {
    pending: optional_absent(), host_seen: false,
    transport: optional_absent(), credential: optional_absent(),
    unmodelled: "", verb: "",
  }
}

// WHAT AN OPTION WORD DOES, AS A DECLARED SUM. This used to be one of seven strings -- "pending",
// "pending-credential", "env", "pending-host", "pending-skip", "skip", "unmodelled" -- compared with ==
// on both sides. That is a closed set written as free text: a tag misspelled on either side compiled,
// landed in the wrong branch, and quietly changed whether a write counts as passwordless in-band, which
// is the fact that gates the destructive reset. The module's own rule for transport names applies here
// word for word -- "an in-band tag carried as free text with a validator in front of it is an undeclared
// sum" (DESIGN section 5: stronger than catching a wrong state is making it unwritable). The members are
// written down once, the walk matches on them, and the claims below read THE SAME function, so a letter
// cannot start being accepted without a claim going red.
type IpmiOptionEffect
  = EffectPendingTransport              // -I: its argument is the transport name
  | EffectPendingCredential             // -U -P -f: its argument is a secret, never a verb
  | EffectPendingHost                   // -H: its argument names another machine
  | EffectPendingTuning                 // -N -S -D: its argument tunes the exchange, not its kind
  | EffectReadsEnv                      // -E: the secret is off the captured line entirely
  | EffectSkip                          // -Z: a privilege flag with no bearing on the channel
  | EffectUnmodelled                    // refused by name: upstream's getopt aborts on it

// WHAT EACH MODELLED OPTION WORD DOES, one row each, as the data the walk AND the claims read. Anything
// else that reaches the walk looking like an option ("-k", "--password", "-P0penBmc") is refused; see the
// table above for who is left out and why. Because the walk's decision and the claims over it come from
// this one row, they cannot drift apart.
//
// The unmodelled letters ("-k" "-y" "-K" "-a" "-C" "-L" "-A" "-t" "-T" "-b" "-B" "-l" "-m" "-p" "-R" "-c"
// "-v" "-V" "-h" "-O" "-o" "-z" "-e" "-4" "-6") are NOT re-listed as branches returning EffectUnmodelled.
// They were, and every one of those branches said exactly what the final else says, so they could not be
// wrong and could not be tested -- a branch that cannot be wrong is not a decision, it is decoration that
// reads like one. What they documented (each is declared in OPTION_STRING, each changes how a write is
// authenticated or where it is delivered, upstream's getopt aborts on a word this table does not name) is
// kept here as the reason the catch-all is the honest behaviour rather than a permissive default.
fn ipmi_option_word_effect(word: String) -> IpmiOptionEffect {
  if word == "-I" { EffectPendingTransport }
  else if word == "-U" || word == "-P" || word == "-f" { EffectPendingCredential }
  else if word == "-E" { EffectReadsEnv }
  else if word == "-H" { EffectPendingHost }
  else if word == "-N" || word == "-S" || word == "-D" { EffectPendingTuning }
  else if word == "-Z" { EffectSkip }
  else { EffectUnmodelled }
}

// ONE WORD OF THE WALK. Total: every branch returns a state. A word held as a pending option's argument is
// consumed as an argument before it can be read as an option or a verb -- the distinction the old
// whole-line scan made by accident is here the mechanism, and it is what makes `-I lanplus` one fact
// instead of two.
fn ipmi_command_walk_step(state: IpmiCommandWalkState, word: String) -> IpmiCommandWalkState {
  if word == "" {
    state
  } else if match state.pending {
    // SOMETHING IS AWAITING ITS ARGUMENT. Whether the next word is an argument or an option is decided by
    // the arm, not by looking at the word -- which is the whole reason an argument-position scan is the
    // only honest reading: the word after -I is a transport name even when it spells "open", and the word
    // after -P is a secret even when it spells "-U".
    Present { value: PendingCredentialArgument { word: _ } } => true
    Present { value: PendingHostArgument { word: _ } } => true
    Present { value: PendingTransportArgument { word: _ } } => true
    Present { value: PendingTuningArgument { word: _ } } => true
    Absent => false
  } {
    // THE ARGUMENT IS CONSUMED AS AN ARGUMENT, and the arm says what KIND of thing it is. There is no
    // value in this type that says "waiting for a credential and a host at once", so the pair of Bools
    // that used to encode that -- and that could disagree with the word beside them -- is gone.
    match state.pending {
      Present { value: PendingCredentialArgument { word: pending_word } } =>
        IpmiCommandWalkState {
          pending: optional_absent(),
          host_seen: state.host_seen, transport: state.transport,
          credential: ipmi_credential_after_letter(seen: state.credential, letter_word: pending_word),
          unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
          verb: state.verb,
        }
      Present { value: PendingHostArgument { word: _ } } =>
        IpmiCommandWalkState {
          pending: optional_absent(),
          host_seen: true, transport: state.transport,
          credential: state.credential, unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
          verb: state.verb,
        }
      Present { value: PendingTransportArgument { word: _ } } =>
        // LAST -I WINS, as upstream leaves it: the arm is overwritten wholesale, so a trailing -I lanplus
        // cannot be laundered by an earlier -I open.
        match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: word) {
          IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: w, transport: arm } =>
            IpmiCommandWalkState {
              pending: optional_absent(),
              host_seen: state.host_seen,
              transport: optional_present(value: TransportNamed { transport: arm, wire: w }),
              credential: state.credential, unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
              verb: state.verb,
            }
          IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: w } =>
            IpmiCommandWalkState {
              pending: optional_absent(),
              host_seen: state.host_seen,
              transport: optional_present(value: TransportNameUnrecognised { wire: w }),
              credential: state.credential, unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
              verb: state.verb,
            }
        }
      Present { value: PendingTuningArgument { word: _ } } =>
        // -N/-S/-D: the value tunes the exchange and changes nothing about its kind
        IpmiCommandWalkState {
          pending: optional_absent(),
          host_seen: state.host_seen, transport: state.transport,
          credential: state.credential, unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
          verb: state.verb,
        }
      // The guard above admits a Present and nothing else, so this arm is taken only by the guard's own
      // evaluation, never by the branch it guards. It is written because a match that omits an arm is a
      // hole the compiler cannot see, and a stated-but-never-selected arm is the opposite of that. It
      // returns the state unchanged because there is nothing to consume when nothing was awaited.
      Absent => state
    }
  } else if starts_with(s: word, prefix: "-") && word != "-" {
    let effect = ipmi_option_word_effect(word: word)
    IpmiCommandWalkState {
      // WHAT THIS WORD IS WAITING FOR, read straight off the effect. The arm IS the kind, so there is no
      // string compare here and no second pair of Bools whose agreement with the word had to be assumed.
      //
      // The FIRST bad word is the refusal: a later option never overwrites an earlier unmodelled name or
      // an earlier missing value, because the earlier one is what upstream would have stopped on. That is
      // why the pending slot needs no "has anything been refused yet" guard while the credential below
      // keeps one: once a word has been refused the outcome is already IpmiCommandUnmodelledOption, which
      // the decision checks before it reads the pending slot, the credential or the verb -- so what a
      // later word puts in these fields cannot reach an answer. The credential's guard is not load-
      // bearing for the same reason; it stays because it states the rule at the place the rule is applied.
      pending: match effect {
        EffectPendingTransport => Present { value: PendingTransportArgument { word: word } }
        EffectPendingCredential => Present { value: PendingCredentialArgument { word: word } }
        EffectPendingHost => Present { value: PendingHostArgument { word: word } }
        EffectPendingTuning => Present { value: PendingTuningArgument { word: word } }
        EffectReadsEnv => optional_absent()
        EffectSkip => optional_absent()
        // refused by name below; nothing is awaited, and the name is already carried in `unmodelled`
        EffectUnmodelled => optional_absent()
      },
      host_seen: state.host_seen, transport: state.transport,
      // -E lands on the same credential fact as the letters do, and cannot overrule one that already
      // landed. There is deliberately no `state.unmodelled == ""` guard here: the decision checks
      // `st.unmodelled != ""` FIRST (before anything reads the credential), so a word refused after a
      // refusal cannot reach an answer, and a condition that cannot change any outcome is not a decision
      // -- it is decoration that reads like one, which is the reason the per-letter unmodelled branches
      // were deleted from the effect table. What the ordering guarantees is stated where the ordering
      // lives, not restated at every place it happens to protect.
      credential: ipmi_credential_after_option_effect(seen: state.credential, effect: effect),
      unmodelled: if effect == EffectUnmodelled && state.unmodelled == "" { word } else { state.unmodelled },
      verb: state.verb,
    }
  } else if state.verb == "" {
    IpmiCommandWalkState {
      pending: optional_absent(),
      host_seen: state.host_seen, transport: state.transport,
      credential: state.credential, unmodelled: state.unmodelled,
      verb: word,
    }
  } else {
    state
  }
}

// THE DECISION, read once from the walk in a fixed order: a broken line is refused before anything about
// the transport is trusted, and a credential is only ever a field of the arm that admits.
fn in_band_command_from_argv(argv_words: List<String>) -> IpmiCommandInvocation {
  let st = fold(argv_words, init: ipmi_command_walk_init(), f: (s, w) => ipmi_command_walk_step(state: s, word: w))
  if st.unmodelled != "" {
    IpmiCommandUnmodelledOption {
      option: st.unmodelled,
      detail: "this word is not modelled here. ipmitool's own getopt aborts on an option it does not recognise (lib/ipmi_main.c default: prints the usage and exits), so these words would not have run either; the letters left out of this module's table -- -k/-y/-K (the IPMIv2 Kg key), -a (prompt for the password), -C/-L/-A (cipher, privilege, auth type), -t/-T/-b/-B/-l/-m (bridging), -p/-R (port, retries) -- all of them change how a write to the BMC is authenticated or where it is delivered" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if match st.pending {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  } {
    // A LETTER THAT NEEDS A VALUE AND GETS THE END OF THE WORDS IS AN ERROR, NOT A PASS. Every word that
    // can be left pending (-I -U -P -f -H -N -S -D) carries a colon in OPTION_STRING
    // (lib/ipmi_main.c:82), so the words ending while one holds is getopt's missing-argument error.
    IpmiCommandOptionNeedsValue {
      option: match st.pending {
        Present { value: PendingCredentialArgument { word: w } } => w
        Present { value: PendingHostArgument { word: w } } => w
        Present { value: PendingTransportArgument { word: w } } => w
        Present { value: PendingTuningArgument { word: w } } => w
        Absent => ""
      },
      detail: "the words end with this option still holding for its argument, so neither the transport it was naming nor any credential it was naming was ever given; plain getopt would have errored on the missing required argument (OPTION_STRING, lib/ipmi_main.c:82)" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match st.transport {
    // THE THREE QUESTIONS THIS ANSWERS -- was there an -I at all, is its name one any plugin claims, and
    // is that arm a host transport -- used to be asked of a Bool plus a sum plus a loose name, in that
    // order, because the state could not otherwise distinguish "no -I" from "-I with a name no plugin
    // claims": both left transport: Absent, and only the Bool separated them. Reading the Bool first was
    // therefore load-bearing -- reverse those two checks and a real name gets reported as though it had
    // never been given. The distinction now lives in the arms, so there is no ordering left to get wrong.
    //
    // The two refusals stay different on purpose: a name that no plugin claims is not the same fact as no
    // name at all, and a reader needs to see the name rather than be told the transport was absent.
    Present { value: TransportNameUnrecognised { wire: w } } => IpmiCommandUnrecognised { wire: w }
    // WHY NO -I IS A REFUSAL AND NOT A DEFAULT: see the block above. DEFAULT_INTF is a ./configure
    // property of the installed binary, not a fact of this invocation.
    Absent => IpmiCommandTransportAbsent { detail: "no -I appears in the words. ipmitool would fall back to the interface DEFAULT_INTF baked into the binary by ./configure -- a property of whichever ipmitool is installed on the rescue OS, not of this invocation. 00_README.txt records only that -I lanplus was ABSENT, which is not the same as knowing which driver answered; 02's header reads no -I lanplus, no credential" }
    Present { value: TransportNamed { transport: named, wire: w } } =>
      match named {
        // the parse already knows WHY this transport is not a host transport; the refusal carries it
        // rather than re-deriving a reason, so the reader sees the upstream fact and not a restatement
        NotInBandHostTransport { transport: _, why_not_in_band: why } =>
          IpmiCommandNotInBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: why }
        InBandHostTransportChannel { transport: in_band } =>
          if st.host_seen {
            // -H names a machine to reach OVER LAN; -I open names the host's own device node. Both cannot
            // be true of one invocation (upstream warns about the pair), and the pair is where a credential
            // could reach a remote host under a "passwordless in-band" label. Refused, never defaulted to
            // the convenient arm.
            IpmiCommandRemoteHostWithInBandName {
              host: "a -H argument was supplied to this invocation" as NonEmptyStr,
              wire: w,
            }
          } else {
            IpmiCommandInBand { transport: in_band, credential: st.credential }
          }
      }
  }
  }
}



// THE ONE PLACE A ROUTE CHANNEL VALUE IS MADE. The parse answers, and this says which arm of the route's
// channel sum it lands on -- so the transport, the credential and the refusal all still come from a single
// read of the words. An unrecognised or unmodelled name has NO arm beside "never observed", which is what
// makes "passwordless over an unmodelled transport" unconstructible rather than a mistake a consumer could
// make.
fn in_band_channel_of_command(command: IpmiCommandInvocation) -> InBandAccessWithoutCredential {
  match command {
    IpmiCommandInBand { transport: t, credential: Absent } =>
      InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: t }
    IpmiCommandInBand { transport: t, credential: Present { value: c } } =>
      InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: t, credential_option: c }
    // ONE ARM IN, ONE ARM OUT. Nothing here decides a cause and nothing here merges two of them; the parse
    // did that, and this is a rename so the route's own sum can be the thing folds and claims match on.
    IpmiCommandTransportAbsent { detail: d } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportAbsent { why: d } }
    IpmiCommandUnrecognised { wire: w } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved {
        refusal: RefusalTransportUnrecognized {
          wire: w,
          why: concat(
            "the -I name is not a transport ipmitool implements, so ipmi_intf_load would return NULL" as NonEmptyStr,
            " and the command would exit before any channel was read"
          ) as NonEmptyStr,
        },
      }
    IpmiCommandNotInBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: why } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportOutOfBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: why } }
    IpmiCommandUnmodelledOption { option: o, detail: d } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalUnmodelledOption { option: o, why: d } }
    IpmiCommandOptionNeedsValue { option: o, detail: d } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalOptionNeedsValue { option: o, why: d } }
    IpmiCommandRemoteHostWithInBandName { host: h, wire: w } =>
      InBandChannelNotObserved {
        refusal: RefusalRemoteHostWithInBandName {
          host: h,
          wire: w,
          why: concat(
            concat(
              "an in-band -I name beside a -H host is a contradiction: in-band reaches the BMC through the" as NonEmptyStr,
              " host's own device node, so this is not a host-to-BMC path,"
            ) as NonEmptyStr,
            " and the name cannot be trusted over the argument that contradicts it"
          ) as NonEmptyStr,
        },
      }
  }
}

// THE ONE CALLER'S VIEW: the channel of a captured invocation, from the words alone. This is the only
// function outside this module that turns a captured argv into a channel, and it takes NOTHING beside the
// words -- the old `interface_name` parameter was the second source of the transport that this whole block
// exists to remove.
fn in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: List<String>) -> InBandAccessWithoutCredential {
  in_band_channel_of_command(command: in_band_command_from_argv(argv_words: argv_words))
}

// THE CAPTURE'S CHANNEL, AND WHAT THE CAPTURE DOES NOT SAY. 02's header reads "no -I lanplus, no
// credential" and 00_README.txt repeats "NO -I lanplus". Both are observations of an ABSENCE, and an
// absence is not a transport name: with no -I at all ipmitool would ask ./configure's DEFAULT_INTF which
// driver answers, which is why in_band_command_from_argv REFUSES those words rather than defaulting them
// (see the block above). What the bytes do establish is the in-band path: /dev/ipmi0 present, the
// ipmi_msghandler/ipmi_devintf/ipmi_ssif modules loaded, and the same account read off the controller
// without a secret, which is the OpenIPMI driver over that node. The words that stand for the capture
// therefore carry the name that driver answers to -- -I open -- and the claim
// the_transport_sums_place_every_wire_name_on_exactly_one_side asserts both halves: those words admit, and
// words with no -I are refused.
// Note also what the capture does NOT say, because the name is easy to invent: `ipmi_ssif` is the kernel
// MODULE for the SMBus/IPMI-SSIF bus beneath that driver, not a name `ipmitool -I` accepts. Naming the
// interface "ssif" would have been a transport that does not exist, and it is refused at the parse.
// WHY NO FIXTURE EVER HOLDS A TRANSPORT IT DID NOT RUN THROUGH THE PARSE. There is no way to obtain a
// lanplus "channel" at all -- the field's type is InBandHostTransport and the out-of-band arms are a
// different sum -- so the most a reader can be shown about "lanplus" is which side the parse put it on and
// why. The law that needs it calls the parse directly (see
// the_transport_sums_place_every_wire_name_on_exactly_one_side); naming an arm in a fixture instead would
// have the fixture asserting the very classification the parse exists to make.

// The account read back BEFORE anything is touched, so the reset is known to be aimed at a controller
// that has an Administrator account to lose. What `ipmitool user list 1` printed on the captured unit
// was id 1 / root / ADMINISTRATOR; the type asks for the id and the privilege that were read, not for
// the pair to match a literal, because the precondition is "an ADMINISTRATOR account was read back at
// this id on this channel" and not "the id was 1".
type InBandAdministratorAccount {
  // THE EXISTING CARRIER, EMBEDDED: channel, user and privilege as one fact the IPMI model already
  // defines. Embedding rather than re-declaring the three fields means the record cannot say "user 1 on
  // channel 1" while a later reader means a different triple; there is one place the triple lives.
  access: IpmiUserChannelAccess
  evidence: EvidenceRef
}

// WHAT THE ACCOUNT LINE REPORTED, AS A SUM RATHER THAN A YES/NO.
//
// Two reasons a Bool cannot carry this, both from §5's "a check restating a constraint the model already
// carries is a second representation of it":
//   * `privilege_is_administrator` collapsed six published privilege levels into one bit, so a controller
//     that answered NO ACCESS and one that answered OPERATOR produced the same value and the reading was
//     gone. The arm below carries what was REPORTED -- an operator debugging a route that will not admit a
//     write needs to know which of the two it was, and `user list 1` printed the word, so the bytes exist
//     to be kept.
//   * `account.user_id >= 1` restated IpmiUserId's own declared range (`Int where range(min: 1, max: 63)`)
//     and therefore could never fail. Deleted rather than kept as insurance: a check that cannot fail is
//     not a check, it is a reader's reason to believe a check ran.
//
// Which privilege the recovery needs is decided ONCE, here, by the DMTF-published level name rather than
// by a boolean a caller computes elsewhere. Consumers match, so a consumer cannot forget to ask.
type InBandAdministratorAccountReading
  = AdministratorAccountReadBack { user: IpmiUserId, channel: IpmiChannelNumber }
  | PrivilegeReadBackIsNotAdministrator { reported: IpmiChannelPrivilegeLimit }

fn in_band_administrator_account_reading(account: InBandAdministratorAccount) -> InBandAdministratorAccountReading {
  match account.access {
    // The triple is matched as one record; the id and the channel are handed to the refusal-free arm so a
    // later reader can see WHICH account the route counted on without re-deriving them.
    IpmiUserChannelAccess { channel: channel, user: user, privilege: IpmiPrivilegeAdministrator } =>
      AdministratorAccountReadBack { user: user, channel: channel }
    IpmiUserChannelAccess { channel: _, user: _, privilege: other } =>
      PrivilegeReadBackIsNotAdministrator { reported: other }
  }
}

// The channel observation as one carrier: which unit, which rescue host read it, the device node and
// drivers, the passwordless fact, and the Administrator account that came back.
type InBandChannelObservation {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  rescue_host_address: Ipv4Address
  device_node: IpmiDeviceNode
  kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName>
  channel: InBandAccessWithoutCredential
  administrator: InBandAdministratorAccount
  evidence: EvidenceRef
  observed_at: CapturedInstant
  // when the rescue kernel created the IPMI device node, read off the node itself. Distinct from
  // observed_at, which is when these bytes were read: the node predates the reading, and here it even
  // predates the reset the reading describes.
  device_node_created_at: CapturedInstant
  // THE ADDRESS THE BMC REPORTS ABOUT ITSELF, read over the in-band channel (`ipmitool lan print` on
  // this board; 02_inband_channel.txt's lan block). This is a fact the CONTROLLER stated about itself
  // through a transport that cannot be pointed at another machine, which is why the bind below may ask
  // it what machine it is: the rescue host's own address says only what the host is, and the Redfish
  // endpoint says only where someone knocked. Only a self-report can be compared against the knock.
  // Absent means the capture did not record one, and a bind cannot be built from what was not read.
  bmc_self_reported_address: Optional<Ipv4Address>
}

// The channel has to have been read FROM THE HOST THAT WAS DECLARED REACHABLE, on the device and
// drivers that host reported. A channel observation lifted from a different machine, or from before
// the rescue OS booted, is not a precondition of anything -- it is a story about some BMC.
//
// WHY A MATCH RETURNING A SUM, AND NOT A BOOL. The earlier shape asked "does the channel admit?" and, when
// the answer was no, composed a sentence about why at the call site. That is §5's bad pattern twice over: a
// Bool asked first and then re-matched to build the answer, and a diagnostic whose content was invented at
// print time instead of computed where the refusal was made ("no plausible output is fabricated"). So this
// MATCHES the arms and returns the reason with the verdict. `reason` is a declared sum, and the refusal
// standing carries that sum rather than prose (DESIGN section 4c). The admitted arm has no field a credential
// could have been written into, so nothing about it has to be re-checked.
type InBandChannelAdmission
  = ChannelAdmits
  | ChannelRefused { reason: RecoveryChannelAbsence }

// WHICH ARM FAILED, AND WHY, AS DATA. Distinct from the parse's InBandCommandRefusal, which answers "what
// is wrong with these words": an invocation can be perfectly well parsed and still not satisfy this
// route's precondition -- it can name a credential on the argv, read back an account that is not
// ADMINISTRATOR, belong to another host or another device node, arrive with the wrong module set, or be
// stamped before the queue it claims to have read. Those are different questions from "is this even an
// ipmitool command", and a reader standing at the machine gets a different next action for each, which is
// what recovery_route_unavailability derives from these arms.
type RecoveryChannelAbsence
  = AbsenceNoRescueHost
  | AbsenceChannelCarriedCredential { credential_option: IpmiArgvCredentialOption }
  | AbsenceChannelNeverRead { refusal: InBandCommandRefusal }
  | AbsenceAccountIsNotAdministrator { reported: IpmiChannelPrivilegeLimit }
  | AbsenceHostAddressDisagrees
  | AbsenceDeviceNodeDisagrees
  | AbsenceKernelModulesDisagree
  | AbsenceNodeCreatedAfterTheReading
  // A BAND THAT WILL NOT CONSTRUCT IS NEITHER EARLY NOR LATE. The ordering used to answer
  // Bool, and a band whose bounds refused to build came back "does not precede" -- so the fold reported
  // "the node was created after the reading" about a pair it had never managed to compare. Which side
  // failed is the fact worth having: a device-node time that will not build is a defect in the device-node
  // time, and the repair is a re-capture, not a re-reading of the channel.
  | AbsenceEarlierCaptureBandRefuses { reason: String }
  | AbsenceLaterCaptureBandRefuses { reason: String }

fn in_band_channel_admission(
  observation: InBandChannelObservation,
  reachability: RescueHostReachability,
) -> InBandChannelAdmission {
  match reachability {
    // Nothing was read FROM ANYWHERE. That is a different absence from a reachable host whose channel was
    // never examined: the first sends someone to bring up the rescue OS, the second to take the reading.
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceNoRescueHost }
    RescueHostReachable { address: a, ssh_port: _, ipmi: ipmi, medium: _ } =>
      match observation.channel {
        InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: _, credential_option: c } =>
          ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceChannelCarriedCredential { credential_option: c } }
        InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: r } =>
          ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceChannelNeverRead { refusal: r } }
        InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: _ } =>
          match in_band_administrator_account_reading(account: observation.administrator) {
            // the level that came back is carried, not paraphrased: "the account read back was OPERATOR"
            // is an observation, "the account is not admin" is a conclusion
            PrivilegeReadBackIsNotAdministrator { reported: p } =>
              ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceAccountIsNotAdministrator { reported: p } }
            AdministratorAccountReadBack { user: _, channel: _ } =>
              if observation.rescue_host_address != a {
                ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceHostAddressDisagrees }
              } else if observation.device_node != ipmi.device_node {
                ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceDeviceNodeDisagrees }
              // THE QUEUE HAD TO EXIST BEFORE THESE BYTES WERE READ. The node's creation minute and the
              // reading's minute are separate facts on the carrier, and this fold is what
              // keeps them separate: an observation stamping the node as created after the reading was
              // taken cannot have read it, so it refuses rather than being admitted on a plausible
              // ordering. THE ORDERING IS MATCHED, NOT ASKED OF AS A BOOL, so the three ways it can fail
              // stay three: a genuine inversion, and one refusal per side whose band would not construct.
              // The module comparison stays last, exactly where it was -- the ordering verdict is asked
              // before it and a failed comparison still short-circuits it.
              } else {
                match captured_band_ordering(
                  earlier: observation.device_node_created_at,
                  later: observation.observed_at,
                ) {
                  BandDoesNotPrecede => ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceNodeCreatedAfterTheReading }
                  EarlierBandRefuses { reason: r } =>
                    ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceEarlierCaptureBandRefuses { reason: r } }
                  LaterBandRefuses { reason: r } =>
                    ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceLaterCaptureBandRefuses { reason: r } }
                  BandPrecedes =>
                    if observation.kernel_modules |> all(m => ipmi.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m)) == false
                      || ipmi.kernel_modules
                        |> all(m => observation.kernel_modules |> any(h => h == m)) == false {
                      ChannelRefused { reason: AbsenceKernelModulesDisagree }
                    } else {
                      ChannelAdmits
                    }
                }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── STEP 3: THE ACTUATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two writes, both privileged, both registered as rows in gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census and
// both run through gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection:
//
//   WRITE 1 -- the in-band Set User Password that puts a temporary credential on the Administrator
//     account, which is what makes the authenticated Redfish session of Write 2 possible (the
//     in-band channel cannot READ the existing password, so a known one is written). Reversible: the
//     next reset clears it and rotation replaces it. EXECUTED BUT NOT BYTE-CAPTURED -- see
//     RecoveryWriteRecord, and the capture README's own statement of it.
//
//   WRITE 2 -- POST /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>/Actions/Manager.ResetToDefaults with ResetType=ResetAll.
//     DESTRUCTIVE by construction: ResetAll returns every account, every password, the network
//     configuration and the user table to factory state, on a board being brought into the fleet rather
//     than serviced. Operator approval, and it owes a readback.
//
// WHY NOT WRITE THE FACTORY PASSWORD INSTEAD: because the write that would do it is the one the firmware
// refuses, and the refusal is captured as an IPMI refusal, not a Redfish one:
//   sudo ipmitool user set password 1 ...  ->  "IPMI command failed: Invalid data field in request",
//   "Set User Password command failed (user 1)", exit 1 (02_inband_channel.txt, quoted with its bytes in
//   gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_inband_recovery_receipts). A Redfish PATCH to the account is a DIFFERENT
//   write, on a different transport, and nothing was captured about it -- so nothing here asserts what it
//   would do; it is simply not the route this unit needs. Even where a password write were allowed it
//   would be a credential write standing in for the reset the unit needs -- the board is arriving from a
//   prior life, and gunbc.machine_intake.prior_life_boundary is the module that prices leaving one.
// RedfishResetToDefaultsType (ResetAll | PreserveNetworkAndUsers | PreserveNetwork) and its arms are
// imported from extdeps.bmc.redfish, where the DMTF enum is declared with its citation: this module's
// judgement is WHICH arm clears the user table, not what the enum contains.

// WHAT EACH PUBLISHED ARM DOES TO THE USER TABLE, AS TWO ARMS RATHER THAN A YES/NO.
//
// A true/false function over a closed sum puts the decision outside the data, and extdeps/bmc/ipmi.dag
// records what that costs on the transport side: "a consumer that forgot to ask failed OPEN". §5.
//
// The second arm also carries the reason the gentler resets fail, which is the part an operator needs.
// Both refusals are the SCHEMA'S published consequences, quoted in extdeps.bmc.redfish, and they are
// different refusals from each other -- which is the reason this is a fold over the upstream arm and not
// a table of two strings. Only PreserveNetworkAndUsers excepts the local accounts from the reset, so only
// it leaves the credential we cannot read standing; PreserveNetwork excepts NETWORK, and its published
// wording ("reset all settings except network settings") takes the user table with it. A fold that treated
// both gentler arms alike, or that read the shorter name as the safer one, would send an operator reaching
// for a milder default straight into the arm that keeps the password we do not know. Decided once, here;
// consumers match the arm.
type ResetToDefaultsUserTableDisposition
  = ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: RedfishResetToDefaultsType }
  | LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: RedfishResetToDefaultsType, what_survives: NonEmptyStr }

fn reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: RedfishResetToDefaultsType) -> ResetToDefaultsUserTableDisposition {
  match kind {
    ResetAll => ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: ResetAll }
    // the words are the DMTF arm's own published consequence, not a taste: this is the one member whose
    // enumDescription excepts local usernames and passwords from the reset, so the account whose password
    // is the thing we are trying to change is exactly what it preserves
    PreserveNetworkAndUsers => LeavesUserTableIntact {
      kind: PreserveNetworkAndUsers,
      what_survives: "the local usernames and passwords, per the arm's published description -- including the account whose unknown password motivated the reset",
    }
    // NOT a safe arm either, and not for the same reason: its published wording excepts only network
    // settings, so it is refused for not being the arm the route selected, not for preserving the
    // credential. The text says which is which rather than letting the shorter name read as the milder one.
    PreserveNetwork => LeavesUserTableIntact {
      kind: PreserveNetwork,
      what_survives: "network settings are excepted from this arm and the user table is not; it is refused because ResetAll is the arm this route is admitted to send, not because it preserves the credential",
    }
  }
}

type RecoveryActuationKind
  = InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword
  | RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: RedfishResetToDefaultsType }

// NO DESTRUCTIVENESS PREDICATE LIVES ON THIS SUM. The DMTF arm already names which ResetToDefaultsType
// was sent, and extdeps.bmc.redfish is the single place clearing-the-user-table is defined -- which is the
// question the reset gate asks, asked of the arm it read off the write record. The password arm needs no
// such answer because its gate never consults destructiveness: the two writes have separate gates and
// separate authorization types, so "is this one destructive?" is never posed at a call site. A
// true/false-per-variant helper here would be reachable only from a test.
fn recovery_reset_actuation() -> RecoveryActuationKind {
  RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetAll }
}

// The password write named by its own arm, so a refusal or an admitted record always says WHICH of the
// two privileged writes it is about rather than leaving the reader to guess from a site name.
fn recovery_in_band_password_actuation() -> RecoveryActuationKind {
  InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword
}

// THE ONE PREDICATE OVER THE LEDGER, UNDER THE NAME THAT DESCRIBES ITS ANSWER. Two names covered this
// body -- one called itself "names the kind", which is not what it returns: an `NotExecuted` record DOES
// name the kind and answers false, because nothing was run. That name was a second, slightly wrong
// description of one concept (§3, nicknaming), and the two names drifted apart at the call sites.
//
// The arms are matched, not tabulated: the two executed arms carry a kind to compare BY VALUE against the
// kind being asked about (an earlier shape enumerated every pair of variants, 3x3 for the reset arm alone,
// so each new RedfishResetToDefaultsType variant grew them quadratically and a missed pair read as
// "different" with nothing complaining). `NotExecuted` is not a false among peers -- it is the arm with no
// execution to compare, so it answers for itself.
fn recovery_write_was_attempted(record: RecoveryWriteRecord, kind: RecoveryActuationKind) -> Bool {
  match record {
    ExecutedWithCapturedExchange { kind: k, request: _, response: _, observed_at: _ } => k == kind
    ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: k, observed_exit: _, detail: _ } => k == kind
    NotExecuted { kind: _ } => false
  }
}

// WHAT THE WRITES LOOK LIKE IN THE LEDGER. Each is a gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census row
// (recovery_in_band_set_user_password_effect, recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults_effect) in the standing
// scope selection assigns; the carrier below is the outcome of running the selection over those rows.
// It is a carrier and not a boolean because DESIGN §4d: a write is authorized, or it is refused with
// the refusal the selection actually gave. A destructive arm that arrives without its operator
// approval is REFUSED here even if some row said otherwise -- the approval is not decoration.
// ONE ADMISSION TYPE PER PRIVILEGED WRITE, so the cross-arm state cannot be written (§5: correctness by
// construction, not validation). Two rounds of the same defect got here. First the carrier held `kind`
// plus two Bools restating what the kind already said, and `recovery_write_is_admissible` patched it after
// the fact. Splitting the arms by kind left a second copy of the same mistake: ONE type serving both
// writes meant a password admission could be built in the reset arm and vice versa, so the fold needed
// runtime refusals for "the password write arrived in the reset arm" -- and, to keep those matches total,
// arms carrying invented causes like "unreachable: admitted arm". A refusal cause that describes a state
// the type supposedly forbids is a fabricated diagnostic (§5: no plausible output is fabricated), and the
// tell is that validation stood where construction was missing.
//
// Now the password gate returns PasswordWriteAuthorization and the reset gate returns
// ResetWriteAuthorization, each naming ONLY its own write. There is no value of either type that can be
// passed where the other is expected, so the fold's match on each is complete with three arms: admitted,
// refused, nothing else. No Bool in between: a predicate that answered "is this admitted?" and was then
// followed by a second match on the same value was the Bool standing where the match belonged.
// THE TICKETS THAT SAY A PRIVILEGED WRITE WAS ADMITTED -- AND FOR WHICH CHASSIS AND WHICH WRITE
// (side-chat HOLD blocker 1). The admitted arms used to carry only "who admitted this", which meant two
// things: any module could spell the arm without asking either gate, and nothing on the value said which
// chassis or which write it authorized -- so a password admission minted for one unit could be spent on
// another, and the fold had to re-derive that pairing from the records beside it. Each admitted arm now
// carries a SEALED RECORD naming the subject the gate read, the actuation it verified and the issuer it
// asked, so the pairing travels with the authorization instead of being assumed next to it.
//
// WHY SEALED RECORDS AND NOT SEALED SUMS -- the part worth recording because it nearly went in wrong.
// This corpus MEASURES that sole_constructor on a COPRODUCT does not refuse cross-module variant
// construction: test.claim.sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe carries that as a declared red arm
// (f13_variant_construction_refuses). Sealing the two sums below would therefore have been permanently
// green by construction, which is worse than absent: the annotation would advertise a wall the compiler
// does not build. What the compiler DOES refuse is constructing a sealed RECORD in another module, which
// is why test.claim.fleet_observation_seal seals the records a sum carries rather than the sums. Same
// arrangement here, and both paths are probed rather than asserted --
// test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_authorization_seal_witness measures the forged
// spelling and the unadmitted mint call, direct AND through a wrapper.
type PasswordWriteAdmissionTicket sole_constructor {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  operation: RecoveryActuationKind
  authorized_by: DeclarationRef
}

// The reset ticket additionally carries the disposition that was actuated and the ruling under which it
// ran, so the fold files the write it believes FROM the ticket: a ResetAll cannot be admitted under one
// arm, filed under another, or re-ruled after the fact.
type ResetWriteAdmissionTicket sole_constructor {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  operation: RecoveryActuationKind
  reset_type: RedfishResetToDefaultsType
  authorized_by: DeclarationRef
  operator_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization
}

// THE ONLY MINT OF THE PASSWORD TICKET, admitted by roster to the gate that earns it and to the fixture
// that stands in for a controller whose policy was measured. The roster is the point: unrostered, this fn
// would be a second, unpoliced mint beside the gate -- the hole blocker 1 names. A new caller has to be
// declared here before it can be used, and a roster entry that fails to collect is refused by the
// compiler rather than silently emptying the wall (test.claim.admit_callers_roster_integrity_witness).
fn recovery_mint_password_admission_ticket(
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject,
  operation: RecoveryActuationKind,
  authorized_by: DeclarationRef,
) -> PasswordWriteAdmissionTicket
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "password_write_admitted_after_ledger"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_admitted_in_band_write"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_password_admission_for_another_unit"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_password_admission_for_another_operation"),
  ]
{
  PasswordWriteAdmissionTicket {
    subject: subject,
    operation: operation,
    authorized_by: authorized_by,
  }
}

// THE ONLY MINT OF THE RESET TICKET, admitted to its gate and its fixture, for the same reason.
fn recovery_mint_reset_admission_ticket(
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject,
  operation: RecoveryActuationKind,
  reset_type: RedfishResetToDefaultsType,
  authorized_by: DeclarationRef,
  operator_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization,
) -> ResetWriteAdmissionTicket
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "reset_write_admitted_after_ledger"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_admitted_reset_write"),
  ]
{
  ResetWriteAdmissionTicket {
    subject: subject,
    operation: operation,
    reset_type: reset_type,
    authorized_by: authorized_by,
    operator_ruling: operator_ruling,
  }
}

// THE AUTHORIZATION THE FOLD ASKS FOR: an arm carrying a ticket, or the ledger's refusal. The sum is
// open deliberately, per the measured coproduct hole above; the ticket inside is what cannot be spelled.
type PasswordWriteAuthorization
  = PasswordWriteAdmitted { ticket: PasswordWriteAdmissionTicket }
  // THE LEDGER'S OWN VERDICT, PASSED THROUGH. When the census refused, the refusal is the census's: the
  // defects, needs, survivors, causes and divergence it computed travel in the arm. Rendering them into a
  // sentence here would have thrown away the detail that makes the refusal actionable -- six upstream
  // outcomes would have arrived as one string, and three of them need three different people to fix.
  | PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: DeclarationRef, cause: LedgerRefusalCause }
  // The channel and record refusals have no upstream carrier to pass along: they ARE the decision, made in
  // this gate, so they name what they checked.
  | PasswordWriteRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// The reset authorization, same shape. The census refusal keeps the census's own computed detail.
type ResetWriteAuthorization
  = ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: ResetWriteAdmissionTicket }
  | ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: DeclarationRef, cause: LedgerRefusalCause }
  | ResetWriteRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// The two admitted arms carry the two census sites, so "who admitted this" is answered by the arm rather
// than by a field a caller could set to anything.
// WHETHER A WRITE RECORD PROVES ANYTHING AT ALL. Both privileged writes of this sequence were executed
// and NEITHER was byte-captured: the temporary password was wiped by the reset and cannot be reproduced,
// and the ResetToDefaults request/response bodies were observed in session only. That is what
// ExecutedWithoutByteCapture means, and the route's success condition does NOT rest on it -- it rests on
// the post-reset readback below. A write record can therefore prove an ATTEMPT and nothing more.
type RecoveryWriteRecord
  = ExecutedWithCapturedExchange {
      kind: RecoveryActuationKind
      request: EvidenceRef
      response: EvidenceRef
      observed_at: CapturedInstant
    }
  | ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, observed_exit: Int, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | NotExecuted { kind: RecoveryActuationKind }

// NOTHING ASKS "DOES THIS RECORD GROUND SUCCESS". The fold gates on recovery_write_was_attempted and on
// the readback, and picks its success arm by which branch it lands in. What grounds success is carried by
// the arms -- ExecutedWithCapturedExchange is the only arm whose bytes can ground anything -- so the
// witness matches the three arms where they are visible rather than through a helper, which would be a
// Bool wearing the claim's clothes.

// WHY THERE ARE NO `*_admission_issued_by` PREDICATES. Checking WHICH GATE issued an admission is a
// legitimate question -- it compares two declarations -- but it does not need a helper: each admitted arm
// carries its issuer, and the fold that matches the arm compares that issuer to the gate inline. A helper
// taking the whole admission would have to match on it again, which is exactly the Bool-then-re-match shape
// deleted above. So the question is asked once, where the arm is already in hand.

// THE ADMISSION GATES THEMSELVES: WHICH DECLARATIONS THE CENSUS ROWS ARE FILED AGAINST.
//
// The two recovery write rows in gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_census carry `site` pointing at the two
// functions below. That is the whole reason those rows exist: a census row whose decl_name names nothing is
// a classification of a write that is not made anywhere (DESIGN §3 -- "a stale symbol is decidable from the
// Node tree"; here it would be worse than stale, it was never there). A row must point at the code that
// DECIDES the write, so ledger and gate cannot drift: change the gate and the row is on its face describing
// something that no longer admits.
fn recovery_in_band_password_write_site() -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_in_band_password_write")
}

fn recovery_manager_reset_site() -> DeclarationRef {
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults")
}

fn recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> PrivilegedEffectSite? {
  filter(privileged_effect_census, row => declaration_ref_eq(a: row.site, b: site_ref)) |> first()
}

// WHAT THE LEDGER HAS TO SAY ABOUT A RECOVERY WRITE. `site_verdict` is the census's own judgment: it asks
// gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection what pattern the effect's characteristics force, then joins
// that against what the row claims is realized. Nothing here is a boolean the route carries with it, and
// nothing here renders the verdict into a string on the way out -- each refusal arm carries what the census
// COMPUTED (`defects`, `needs`, `survivors`, `causes`, `standing`, the row's `divergence`), because a
// degradation a reader cannot tell apart from four others is not countable, and three of these need three
// different people to fix. §5: "every degradation is typed, located and countable".
//
// TWO QUESTIONS, AT TWO STRENGTHS, because the two writes are not alike:
//
//   * `recovery_ledger_admits_password_write` -- a row exists, its selection is a pattern (not a refusal,
//     not "needs evidence", not "not decidable"), and the row's `realized` agrees with what was selected.
//     This is the bar for the reversible temporary password; demanding operator approval there would have
//     the census claim a destructive standing for a write the next rotation undoes.
//   * `recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval` -- on top of that, the selected pattern IS
//     OperatorApprovedCapability and the row realizes an approval capability. Only the reset is held to
//     this, and what makes it a gate rather than a redundancy is that it reads the pattern the SELECTION
//     produced from the effect's characteristics: soften the reset's `reversibility` and selection moves to
//     a revocable grant, so the gate closes. A gate that merely restated the row's own field would be
//     decoration.
//
// BOTH WALK THE FULL SELECTION, not just its happy path. Six outcomes exist upstream and all six arrive
// here as themselves, so the reset is refused with the selection's own defects rather than with a sentence
// about there being a problem.
type LedgerAdmission
  = LedgerConforms { verdict: SiteVerdict }
  | LedgerRefusal { cause: LedgerRefusalCause }

// THE ONE REFUSAL, SHARED BY BOTH GATES, WITH THE SELECTION CARRIED THROUGH. Naming is per mistake rather
// than per gate, so a missing row refused twice (once per write) is recognisably one problem.
type LedgerRefusalCause
  = CensusRowMissing { site: DeclarationRef }
  | SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: List<SelectionEvidenceNeed> }
  | SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: List<DeclarationRef> }
  | SelectionRefusedByConstraints { defects: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | NoPatternAdmissible { causes: List<NonEmptyStr>, scope: DecisionScope }
  | PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: AuthorizationPattern }
  | RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
      divergence: SiteConformance,
      selected: AuthorizationPattern,
      realized: RealizedAuthorization,
    }
  | RowNotDecidable { standing: AuthorizationPatternSelection }

// THE GATE FOR THE TEMPORARY PASSWORD WRITE. The bar is a conforming row whose selection is a pattern --
// demanding operator approval here would have the census claim a destructive standing for a write the next
// rotation undoes, which is why this gate and the reset's are two questions at two strengths.
fn recovery_ledger_admits_password_write(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> LedgerAdmission {
  match recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: site_ref) {
    Absent => LedgerRefusal { cause: CensusRowMissing { site: site_ref } }
    Present { value: row } =>
      match site_verdict(row: row) {
        SiteVerdict { site: _, selection: selection, conformance: conformance } =>
          match selection {
            // THE PATTERN IS BOUND HERE, where the arm proves one was selected. The disagreement refusal
            // declares an AuthorizationPattern, and the arms that select nothing refuse under their own
            // cause -- so there is nothing for an extraction helper to return Absent for.
            PatternSelected { pattern: p, receipt: _ } =>
              match conformance {
                Conforms => LedgerConforms { verdict: site_verdict(row: row) }
                Diverges =>
                  LedgerRefusal {
                    cause: RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
                      divergence: conformance,
                      selected: p,
                      realized: row.realized,
                    },
                  }
                DivergesWithReason { reason: _ } =>
                  LedgerRefusal {
                    cause: RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
                      divergence: conformance,
                      selected: p,
                      realized: row.realized,
                    },
                  }
                SiteNotDecidable { standing: standing } =>
                  LedgerRefusal { cause: RowNotDecidable { standing: standing } }
              }
            ManualStepRequired { step: step, receipt: _ } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: HumanOnlyStep { step: step } } }
            PatternSelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: needs } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: needs } }
            PatternSelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: survivors } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: survivors } }
            PatternSelectionRefused { defects: defects } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionRefusedByConstraints { defects: defects } }
            NoAdmissiblePattern { causes: causes, scope: scope } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: NoPatternAdmissible { causes: causes, scope: scope } }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE GATE FOR THE DESTRUCTIVE RESET -- the same walk, with the pattern named, because that is the whole
// difference between the two writes. What makes it a gate rather than a restatement of the row's own field
// is that it reads the pattern the SELECTION produced from the effect's characteristics: soften the reset's
// `reversibility` and selection moves to a revocable grant, so this gate closes. A gate that only checked
// `row.realized` would be decoration, and this one demonstrably is not: it is the reason the reset is
// refused while the effect's classification is being argued about.
fn recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: DeclarationRef) -> LedgerAdmission {
  match recovery_census_row_at(site_ref: site_ref) {
    Absent => LedgerRefusal { cause: CensusRowMissing { site: site_ref } }
    Present { value: row } =>
      match site_verdict(row: row) {
        SiteVerdict { site: _, selection: selection, conformance: conformance } =>
          match selection {
            PatternSelected { pattern: OperatorApprovedCapability, receipt: _ } =>
              match conformance {
                Conforms =>
                  match row.realized {
                    RealizedApprovalCapability => LedgerConforms { verdict: site_verdict(row: row) }
                    _ =>
                      LedgerRefusal {
                        cause: RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
                          divergence: conformance,
                          selected: OperatorApprovedCapability,
                          realized: row.realized,
                        },
                      }
                  }
                Diverges =>
                  LedgerRefusal {
                    cause: RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
                      divergence: conformance,
                      selected: OperatorApprovedCapability,
                      realized: row.realized,
                    },
                  }
                DivergesWithReason { reason: _ } =>
                  LedgerRefusal {
                    cause: RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection {
                      divergence: conformance,
                      selected: OperatorApprovedCapability,
                      realized: row.realized,
                    },
                  }
                SiteNotDecidable { standing: standing } =>
                  LedgerRefusal { cause: RowNotDecidable { standing: standing } }
              }
            // ANY OTHER SELECTION REFUSES THIS GATE, AND EACH ONE ARRIVES WITH ITS OWN EVIDENCE. The
            // pattern the selection DID produce travels into the refusal, so "why not the operator-approved
            // capability?" is answerable from the refusal instead of from a re-run of the selection.
            PatternSelected { pattern: other, receipt: _ } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: other } }
            ManualStepRequired { step: step, receipt: _ } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: HumanOnlyStep { step: step } } }
            PatternSelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: needs } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: needs } }
            PatternSelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: survivors } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: survivors } }
            PatternSelectionRefused { defects: defects } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: SelectionRefusedByConstraints { defects: defects } }
            NoAdmissiblePattern { causes: causes, scope: scope } =>
              LedgerRefusal { cause: NoPatternAdmissible { causes: causes, scope: scope } }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE THREE NAME RENDERERS, EACH A CLOSED MATCH OVER THE ARMS IT IS GIVEN. These are not helpers standing
// in for a decision: the names they return are the census's own vocabulary, spelled once, so the sentence
// below cannot drift from the sum it describes. Every arm of every sum is named, which is what makes the
// sentence safe to read to an operator.
fn pattern_identity_text(p: AuthorizationPattern) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    FederatedScopedGrant => "federated scoped grant" as NonEmptyStr
    OperatorApprovedCapability => "operator-approved capability" as NonEmptyStr
    HumanOnlyStep { step: _ } => "human-only step" as NonEmptyStr
    OperatorOwnSession => "operator's own session" as NonEmptyStr
    AdministratorEdge => "administrator edge" as NonEmptyStr
    ApprovedConvergenceGrant => "approved-convergence grant" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn realized_authorization_text(r: RealizedAuthorization) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    RealizedFederatedGrant => "federated grant" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedRunSelected => "run-selected" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedApprovalCapability => "approval capability" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedHumanStep => "human step" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedOperatorSession { arm: _ } => "operator session" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedUnauthorized => "unauthorized" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedAdministratorEdge => "administrator edge" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizedUnderApprovedConvergence => "under an approved convergence" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn conformance_text(c: SiteConformance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    Conforms => "conforms" as NonEmptyStr
    Diverges => "diverges, with no reason attached" as NonEmptyStr
    DivergesWithReason { reason: reason } =>
      concat("diverges: ", reason.statement as NonEmptyStr) as NonEmptyStr
    SiteNotDecidable { standing: _ } => "not decidable against its selection" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE OPERATOR-FACING SENTENCE, DERIVED FROM THE CARRIER AND NEVER INVENTED BESIDE IT. Each arm contributes
// its own words plus what it counts; the two "needs" arms report a count rather than naming items, because
// naming them would mean importing the evidence-need vocabulary into a module whose job is the recovery
// route -- and the exact list travels in the carrier, where a consumer that needs it can match the arm.
fn ledger_refusal_text(site_ref: DeclarationRef, cause: LedgerRefusalCause) -> NonEmptyStr {
  let where_ = concat(" at ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: site_ref) as String) as String
  // Each arm's words are constants in this function, and each counts what its carrier holds. join is used
  // rather than a chain of nested concats so the sentence is readable at the call site.
  join([
    "the census does not admit ",
    declaration_ref_display_key(ref: site_ref) as String,
    ": ",
    match cause {
      CensusRowMissing { site: s } =>
        join(["no row is filed against ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: s) as String,
              ", so the write is unclassified and nothing admits it"], "")
      SelectionNeedsEvidence { needs: needs } =>
        join(["the selection is waiting on ", to_string(value: list_length(items: needs)),
              " evidence need(s) -- the exact list travels in the refusal's carrier"], "")
      SelectionNeedsPolicy { survivors: survivors } =>
        join(["nothing decides among ", to_string(value: list_length(items: survivors)),
              " surviving candidate pattern(s); a policy must say which"], "")
      SelectionRefusedByConstraints { defects: defects } =>
        join(["the effect's own characteristics are refused by the pattern constraints (",
              to_string(value: list_length(items: defects)), " defect(s))"], "")
      NoPatternAdmissible { causes: causes, scope: _ } =>
        join(["no admissible pattern exists for this effect's decision scope (",
              to_string(value: list_length(items: causes)), " cause(s))"], "")
      PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: selected } =>
        join(["the selection chose ", pattern_identity_text(p: selected) as String,
              ", not the operator-approved capability, so a standing that could be revoked would stand behind",
              " an irreversible actuation"], "")
      RealizedFieldDisagreesWithSelection { divergence: d, selected: sel, realized: realized } =>
        join(["the row's realized field (", realized_authorization_text(r: realized) as String,
              ") disagrees with the selected pattern (", pattern_identity_text(p: sel) as String,
              "): ", conformance_text(c: d) as String], "")
      RowNotDecidable { standing: _ } =>
        join(["the row is not decidable against the standing its selection produced", where_], "")
    },
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE MEASUREMENT A DESTRUCTIVE ACTUATION MAY REST ON, read off the controller under foot. The route
// carries the Get Device ID answer IT observed and nothing else about firmware, so this is the only way
// any gate can obtain a policy: it asks the board catalog to identify that reading, and the catalog
// answers with one of three things, only one of which is a measurement.
//
// WHY THE CAUSES ARE CARRIED INSTEAD OF COLLAPSED INTO A BOOL. "Nobody measured this controller" and
// "our catalog is self-contradictory about it" both mean refuse, but they demand different next actions
// from different people -- the first is a measurement to go and take, the second is a defect in this
// repository. Collapsing them would send someone to re-measure a board that is already filed (DESIGN §5:
// a failure arm refuses and stays locatable).
fn recovery_policy_sighted_on_controller(route: RecoveryInBandRoute) -> PublishedPasswordWritePolicySighting {
  asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_sighted_on(observed: route.in_band_device_identity)
}

// THE GATE ON THE IN-BAND PASSWORD WRITE. Admits only against its own census row, whose selection the
// selection module derives from the effect's own characteristics, and refuses unless the channel the route
// claims really was passwordless -- checked on the carried channel observation rather than trusted from an
// outcome. That re-check is the point: a temporary Administrator password is being written into the
// controller's user table by a caller asserting that no credential was involved, and the carrier for that
// assertion is the channel observation the route carries. The write handed over must also BE the write this
// row classifies: admitting a record that names the reset would let one admission purchase two effects.
fn admit_recovery_in_band_password_write(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
) -> PasswordWriteAuthorization {
  // THE LEDGER FIRST, AND ITS REFUSAL PASSED THROUGH UNREDUCED. Everything below presupposes the effect is
  // classified at all; asking the channel question of a write with no census row produces a refusal about
  // the channel, which is the wrong subject.
  match recovery_ledger_admits_password_write(site_ref: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) {
    LedgerRefusal { cause: cause } =>
      PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(), cause: cause }
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => password_write_admitted_after_ledger(route: route, write: write)
  }
}

// THE CHANNEL AND RECORD QUESTIONS, ASKED ONLY ONCE THE EFFECT IS ADMITTED. A branch per refusal, each
// naming the thing it checked -- no Bool is consulted, so there is nothing for a call site to forget.
fn password_write_admitted_after_ledger(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
) -> PasswordWriteAuthorization
  // THE HOLE THAT OPENED ONE LEVEL DOWN (side-chat HOLD blocker 1, second round). The tickets are sealed
  // and their mints are rostered, but an UNROSTERED helper that hands one out is the same wall with a
  // door in it: any module could call this directly and receive a real ticket without the ledger
  // classification above it ever being read. So the gate's producer admits only the gate that runs that
  // classification -- which is what makes the seal a wall rather than a convention.
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_in_band_password_write"),
  ]
{
  let kind = recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
  if match route.in_band.channel {
    // Matched, not queried: a channel that carried its credential on the argv, or was never read, has an
    // arm, and that arm says so here rather than in a predicate the call site has to remember.
    InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: _ } => false
    InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: _, credential_option: _ } => true
    InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: _ } => true
  } {
    PasswordWriteRefused { cause: "the in-band channel is not the credential-free arm, so this is not the write the census admits" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if recovery_write_was_attempted(record: write, kind: kind) == false {
    PasswordWriteRefused { cause: "the write handed over does not name the in-band password actuation this gate classifies" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    PasswordWriteAdmitted {
      ticket: recovery_mint_password_admission_ticket(
        subject: route.subject,
        operation: kind,
        authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(),
      ),
    }
  }
}

// THE GATE ON THE DESTRUCTIVE RESET -- the only place a ResetAll may be decided. Each condition is a
// different mistake, so each has its own cause:
//   * the ledger admits it: the census row exists and its selection is the operator-approved capability
//     (irreversibility forces that -- a grant that could be revoked cannot stand behind what no reapply
//     undoes), and the row's `realized` agrees rather than merely sitting beside it;
//   * the run acts under the named recovery ruling, and the ruling's interlock is actually rostered --
//     `ruling_discharge_interlock_defect` refuses a pending interlock, which is the case today, so this
//     gate is closed until the hold is rostered rather than pretending to be open;
//   * the arm ACTUATED is the one that clears the user table. The arm is taken from the write record, not
//     from a field the route was handed: what may be believed is what was sent. An arm that keeps the user
//     table is a destructive actuation that does not solve the problem justifying it, and a record that
//     names no reset at all cannot be believed to have wiped anything;
//   * THE MEASUREMENT IS ABOUT THIS CONTROLLER. The catalog is asked to identify the Get Device ID answer
//     the route observed, and the reset is admitted only on the arm that says a refusal was measured under
//     THAT reading. A route on any other controller gets the refusal, so the only fact that licenses
//     wiping a chassis can no longer be carried in by a caller;
//   * the unit's durable hold is held, and held for THIS unit: a ResetAll returns every account, every
//     password and the network configuration of one chassis to factory state, so it may not be actuated on
//     a unit somebody else is driving, nor on a unit whose key merely shares a prefix with the subject.
fn admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  under_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization,
  unit_hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?,
  reset_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
) -> ResetWriteAuthorization {
  // THE LEDGER FIRST, AND ITS REFUSAL PASSED THROUGH UNREDUCED. The ruling, the disposition, the warrant and
  // the hold all presuppose the effect is classified and admitted; asking them of a write with no census row
  // produced a refusal about something other than the missing classification.
  match recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: recovery_manager_reset_site()) {
    LedgerRefusal { cause: cause } =>
      ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: recovery_manager_reset_site(), cause: cause }
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } =>
      reset_write_admitted_after_ledger(
        route: route,
        under_ruling: under_ruling,
        unit_hold: unit_hold,
        reset_write: reset_write,
      )
  }
}

// THE FOUR QUESTIONS THE RESET OWES, ASKED OF A WRITE THAT IS ALREADY ADMITTED BY CLASSIFICATION. The reset
// arm is matched once at the top rather than extracted twice, so the admitted arm's `reset_type` is THE ARM
// THAT WAS CHECKED -- not a second read that could disagree with the check, and with no unreachable arm that
// would have to invent a reset type to stay total.
fn reset_write_admitted_after_ledger(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  under_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization,
  unit_hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?,
  reset_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
) -> ResetWriteAuthorization
  // AS ABOVE, FOR THE RESET: sealed tickets behind an unrostered helper are a seal any import can lift.
  // Only the gate that checks the ruling, the hold, the destructive class and the reset type is admitted.
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults"),
  ]
{
  match recovery_write_reset_arm(record: reset_write) {
    Absent => ResetWriteRefused {
      cause: "the write record names no ResetToDefaults arm at all, so there is nothing to check clears the user table" as NonEmptyStr
    }
    Present { value: a } =>
      if match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: a) {
        // MATCHED THROUGH THE DISPOSITION, not asked a yes/no: the admitted arm has to be one the upstream
        // enum's own effect says clears the table holding the credential, and the disposition's other arm
        // says in its own words what would survive a gentler reset.
        ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
        LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: _, what_survives: _ } => true
      } {
        ResetWriteRefused {
          cause: "the write record names a ResetToDefaults arm that does not clear the table holding the unknown credential" as NonEmptyStr
        }
      } else if match ruling_discharge_interlock_defect(site_ref: recovery_manager_reset_site(), ruling: under_ruling) {
        Present { value: _ } => true
        Absent => false
      } {
        ResetWriteRefused { cause: "the ruling's interlock is not rostered against this site, so no standing discharges it" as NonEmptyStr }
      } else if match recovery_reset_warrant(sighting: recovery_policy_sighted_on_controller(route: route)) {
        // THE WARRANT IS MATCHED ONCE, over ALL FOUR ARMS. Every refusal arm carries what was seen (the
        // reading nobody measured, the two rows that disagreed, or the release key that will not resolve),
        // so the refusal below is the ARM'S words and not a string this gate invents about a state it did
        // not inspect. The ambiguity arm refuses like the other two: a reading the catalog answers twice
        // warrants nothing, and it sends the operator to de-duplicate the catalog rather than to measure.
        WarrantedOnMeasuredRefusal { refusal: _, observed: _ } => false
        NeverMeasuredOnThisController { observed: _ } => true
        CatalogAmbiguousForThisController { observed: _, matched: _ } => true
        ReleaseKeyUnresolvableInCatalog { firmware: _, observed: _ } => true
        RowFiledUnderTheWrongReading { row: _, observed: _ } => true
      } {
        ResetWriteRefused {
          cause: recovery_reset_warrant_refusal_text(
            warrant: recovery_reset_warrant(sighting: recovery_policy_sighted_on_controller(route: route)),
          ),
        }
      } else if match recovery_hold_admits_this_unit(unit_key: route.subject.subject.unit_key, hold: unit_hold) {
        // THE HOLD MISTAKE IS MATCHED, AND KEPT. Three mistakes used to arrive here as one `false`; each now
        // carries what an operator needs -- the store's own refusal words, or WHICH unit is held versus which
        // one this route stands on -- and the refusal below is derived from the arm that refused.
        HoldHeldForThisUnit { proof: _ } => false
        HoldNeverTaken => true
        HoldRefusedByStore { reason: _ } => true
        HoldHeldForAnotherUnit { held_for: _, this_unit: _ } => true
      } {
        ResetWriteRefused {
          cause: recovery_hold_refusal_text(
            unit_key: route.subject.subject.unit_key,
            hold: unit_hold,
          ),
        }
      } else {
        // The admitted arm carries the ruling it stands under and the arm it cleared, so a later reader can
        // see WHICH approval purchased WHICH destructive actuation. `reset_type: a` is the arm matched at the
        // top of this function -- the same value the disposition above was decided on.
        ResetWriteAdmitted {
          ticket: recovery_mint_reset_admission_ticket(
            subject: route.subject,
            operation: recovery_reset_actuation(),
            reset_type: a,
            authorized_by: recovery_manager_reset_site(),
            operator_ruling: under_ruling,
          ),
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE ARM THAT WAS ACTUALLY SENT, AS THE WRITE RECORD SAYS. Only a record of a ResetToDefaults names one;
// a record of the in-band password write, or of no write at all, names nothing. Taken from the record
// rather than from an assertion about the route, because the reset gate believes what was done.
fn recovery_write_reset_arm(record: RecoveryWriteRecord) -> RedfishResetToDefaultsType? {
  match record {
    ExecutedWithCapturedExchange { kind: RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: arm }, request: _, response: _, observed_at: _ } =>
      Present { value: arm }
    ExecutedWithoutByteCapture { kind: RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: arm }, observed_exit: _, detail: _ } =>
      Present { value: arm }
    _ => Absent
  }
}

// IS THE RESET WARRANTED ON THIS CONTROLLER, AS A SUM WITH NO ARM TO LIE IN.
//
// A function returning a refusal cause has to answer for the case where nothing was refused, and an
// unrunnable branch invites a plausible string there -- §5's "no plausible output is fabricated". The
// string would never be read (the gate consults it only on the two refusal arms), so it could never be
// checked, so it could never be found wrong.
//
// A sum makes the question answerable only where it has answers. WarrantedOnMeasuredRefusal carries the
// measurement it rests on, so an auditor can ask WHICH refusal on WHICH controller licensed the wipe; the
// two refusal arms each carry what was seen. No arm has to speak for a state the model says cannot arise,
// so there is nothing to fabricate -- §5's "unwritable, not validated", applied to a diagnostic.
type RecoveryResetWarrant
  = WarrantedOnMeasuredRefusal {
      refusal: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy
      observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity
    }
  | NeverMeasuredOnThisController { observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity }
  // The reading WAS measured and the catalog holds TWO refusal rows for it. Nobody needs to go measure
  // anything; the catalog has to be de-duplicated, and the rows that disagreed travel with the refusal so
  // the duplicate is findable without grepping the receipt shelf. Distinct from
  // ReleaseKeyUnresolvableInCatalog (one row found, naming a release the catalog does not index) because
  // the two defects are not the same edit and must not be refused in the same words.
  | CatalogAmbiguousForThisController {
      observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity
      matched: List<PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy>
    }
  | ReleaseKeyUnresolvableInCatalog { firmware: BmcFirmwareVersion, observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity }
  // A ROW THAT DISAGREES WITH ITSELF, kept as its own refusal rather than folded into
  // NeverMeasuredOnThisController. Somebody measured this controller; the row they filed names a release
  // that does not match the Get Device ID answer recorded beside it. Telling that person "go measure it"
  // leaves the bad row on the shelf to produce the same refusal tomorrow, and the repair belongs to whoever
  // maintains the filing. The row travels so the bad filing is findable from the refusal alone.
  | RowFiledUnderTheWrongReading {
      row: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy
      observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity
    }

// WHY THE TWO REFUSALS ARE DIFFERENT ARMS AND NOT ONE STRING: they send different people to different
// work. An unmeasured controller needs a MEASUREMENT -- nobody has put a device under this one and read
// Get Device ID, so the correct next action is to look, and the reading it disagreed with is carried so
// the operator can see which controller went unread. An unresolvable release key needs a FIX HERE, in the
// board module, because a measured row exists whose firmware will not resolve in the capability catalog;
// the firmware is carried because the fix is to that row, and a reader cannot deduce it from the reading.
fn recovery_reset_warrant(sighting: PublishedPasswordWritePolicySighting) -> RecoveryResetWarrant {
  match sighting {
    RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: refusal, observed: observed } =>
      WarrantedOnMeasuredRefusal { refusal: refusal, observed: observed }
    ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: observed } => NeverMeasuredOnThisController { observed: observed }
    CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: observed, matched: matched } =>
      CatalogAmbiguousForThisController { observed: observed, matched: matched }
    CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: firmware, observed: observed } =>
      ReleaseKeyUnresolvableInCatalog { firmware: firmware, observed: observed }
    RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: row, observed: observed } =>
      RowFiledUnderTheWrongReading { row: row, observed: observed }
  }
}

// THE REFUSAL WORDS, DERIVED FROM THE ARM AND ONLY FROM REFUSAL ARMS. Total over a sum whose arms are all
// refusals, so unlike the deleted function it is never asked to speak for a non-refusal; the warrant's
// admitted arm is not in this match because it has no cause, and a compiler error is what any future
// attempt to hand it one gets. The reading is rendered through extdeps' own identity text so the refusal
// shows the bytes' shape rather than a paraphrase of them.
// THE REFUSAL WORDS, PER REFUSAL ARM. Each is a constant, and that is deliberate: the reading that was
// seen is ALREADY carried, structured, in the arm (`observed`, `firmware`), so rendering it into a
// sentence would be a second, lossy statement of what the model holds (§4c: an annotation must not
// restate what the declaration says). A reader who needs the numbers reads the arm. What a constant per
// arm does give is that the two refusals cannot collapse into one another, and that no caller can put
// arbitrary text in a refusal the way the deleted free-NonEmptyStr cause allowed.
//
// The third arm is total-not-fabricated: the gate asks this only when the warrant refused, so this arm is
// never rendered, and what it would say asserts nothing about a refusal because there is none. The
// difference from the string it replaces is that this one cannot be reached with a plausible-looking
// cause attached to a state the type says is not a refusal.
fn recovery_reset_warrant_refusal_text(warrant: RecoveryResetWarrant) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match warrant {
    NeverMeasuredOnThisController { observed: _ } =>
      "no refusal was ever measured on this controller: the Get Device ID reading on this route matches no measured row, so a ResetAll is not warranted"
    CatalogAmbiguousForThisController { observed: _, matched: _ } =>
      "this board's catalog answers the reading on this route with more than one refusal row, so no single measured refusal warrants a ResetAll: the catalog has to be de-duplicated before the reset can be admitted"
    ReleaseKeyUnresolvableInCatalog { firmware: _, observed: _ } =>
      "the measured row's release key does not resolve in this board's capability catalog, so the reset would rest on a release nobody can name"
    RowFiledUnderTheWrongReading { row: _, observed: _ } =>
      "a refusal row was found for this controller and then refused its own filing: the release it is catalogued under does not match the Get Device ID answer recorded with it, so it warrants nothing until the row is corrected"
    WarrantedOnMeasuredRefusal { refusal: _, observed: _ } =>
      "the reset is warranted: a refusal was measured on this controller"
  }
}

// THE HOLD, AS OBSERVED. Absent means the run never took the hold, which is a refusal and not a default;
// a refused acquisition is a refusal carrying the store's own words about who holds the unit; and the proof
// that IS held must name the SAME unit as the recovery subject -- compared as whole strings, not as prefixes
// or by eye. A recovery that wipes a chassis while a different run holds its maintenance hold is the failure
// this exists to make unrepresentable. The proof itself cannot be fabricated here: UnitHoldProof is minted
// only by the hold module's admit_callers list, so this predicate can be exercised in its refusals and the
// admitting arm is gated by construction rather than by a flag.
// WHICH HOLD MISTAKE HAPPENED, AS A SUM -- three different mistakes used to come out as one `false`.
//
// This was `-> Bool`, and its own comment promised the store's own words about who holds the unit while
// the code threw them away (`UnitHoldRefused { reason: _ } => false`). Missing hold, hold refused by the
// store, and hold on a different unit were indistinguishable -- on the gate in front of the DESTRUCTIVE
// ResetAll, where §5 asks for "a typed, located diagnostic" and "every degradation typed, located and
// countable". The rescue host in this same file already answers with RescueHostReachability and a named
// cause per failure; the hold was the one place that reasoning stopped short.
//
// The arms are the mistakes a human can actually make here, and each carries what it can be acted on
// with: the store's own refusal words; WHICH unit is held versus which one this route stands on. That
// last pair matters most -- two units whose keys share a prefix are two chassis, and an operator told
// only "hold mismatch" has to go work out which two.
type RecoveryHoldAdmission
  = HoldHeldForThisUnit { proof: UnitHoldProof }
  | HoldNeverTaken
  | HoldRefusedByStore { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | HoldHeldForAnotherUnit { held_for: NonEmptyStr, this_unit: NonEmptyStr }

fn recovery_hold_admits_this_unit(unit_key: UnitKey, hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?) -> RecoveryHoldAdmission {
  match hold {
    // Nobody acquired it -- distinct from a refusal, which means somebody ASKED and the store said no.
    // Different next action: acquire it, versus wait for whoever holds it to release.
    Absent => HoldNeverTaken
    Present { value: acquisition } =>
      match acquisition {
        // the store's own words, KEPT
        UnitHoldRefused { reason: reason } => HoldRefusedByStore { reason: reason }
        UnitHeld { proof: proof } =>
          match proof.subject {
            // The field is `key` (gunbc.host.managed_host_unit_hold's AdmittedUnitHoldSubject: host, key,
            // store_host). One arm, so the comparison cannot be skipped by a shape.
            AdmittedUnitHoldSubject { host: _, key: key, store_host: _ } =>
              match recovery_hold_key_verdict(held_key: key, this_unit: unit_key as NonEmptyStr) {
                HoldKeyMatchesThisUnit => HoldHeldForThisUnit { proof: proof }
                HoldKeyNamesAnotherUnit { held_for: held_for, this_unit: this_unit } =>
                  HoldHeldForAnotherUnit { held_for: held_for, this_unit: this_unit }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE KEY DECISION, SEPARATED FROM THE PROOF THAT CARRIES IT. UnitHoldProof is sole_constructor in
// gunbc.host.managed_host_unit_hold and minted only by the live compare-and-set acquire, so NOTHING in
// this module can build one -- which is the point, and also why the mismatch decision had to be reachable
// on its own: with the comparison written inline, the arm that says "this hold is for a different
// chassis" could never be exercised by a claim, and an unexercised arm is where a mistake lives quietly
// (this is the same gap that let a nonsense field name survive here for several rounds).
//
// It is a comparison of two names and nothing else -- no authority, no minting, no way to use it to get a
// hold. The gate still requires the real proof to reach its admitted arm; this only decides, from the two
// keys, which of the two hold arms the gate would land in, and keeps both keys for the refusal.
type RecoveryHoldKeyVerdict
  = HoldKeyMatchesThisUnit
  | HoldKeyNamesAnotherUnit { held_for: NonEmptyStr, this_unit: NonEmptyStr }

fn recovery_hold_key_verdict(held_key: NonEmptyStr, this_unit: NonEmptyStr) -> RecoveryHoldKeyVerdict {
  if held_key == this_unit {
    HoldKeyMatchesThisUnit
  } else {
    HoldKeyNamesAnotherUnit { held_for: held_key, this_unit: this_unit }
  }
}

// THE HOLD REFUSAL WORDS, PER ARM. Three refusals, three next actions: acquire the hold; wait for the
// holder; or STOP, because the reset is being attempted against a different chassis than the one held --
// the one that would otherwise read as a transient failure and get retried.
fn recovery_hold_refusal_text(unit_key: UnitKey, hold: UnitHoldAcquisition?) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match recovery_hold_admits_this_unit(unit_key: unit_key, hold: hold) {
    HoldHeldForThisUnit { proof: _ } => "the hold admits this unit"
    HoldNeverTaken => "the durable unit hold was never acquired, so a factory reset may not be actuated on this unit"
    HoldRefusedByStore { reason: reason } => concat("the durable unit hold was refused by the store: ", reason)
    HoldHeldForAnotherUnit { held_for: held_for, this_unit: this_unit } =>
      concat(
        concat(
          concat(concat("the durable unit hold is held for a DIFFERENT unit (held for ", held_for),
            ", this route stands on "),
          this_unit),
        "); a ResetAll returns one chassis to factory state, so it may not be actuated on the unit somebody else is driving",
      )
  }
}

// AN ADMISSION IS ONLY GOOD IF THE GATE THAT OWNS THE WRITE ISSUED IT, and the arm it arrived in is what
// says so: the password arm can only name the password site's shape, and the reset arm does not exist
// unless it carries an operator ruling. converge checks the issuer against the census site that classifies
// the write, so an arm minted for one gate cannot purchase the other's effect.

// "IT REFUSED" IS NOT THE SUCCESS SIGNAL. A 401 means the credential we sent is not the credential
// the controller holds -- including the case where the reset did nothing at all. What says the reset
// happened is HTTP 403 naming Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired: the published factory credential
// AUTHENTICATED (so it is the active password again) and the service then refused the request until
// that password is changed. Both shapes are committed bytes:
//   artifacts/bmc/srv13-first-contact-2026-10-07/bmc-3.22.00-factory-login-403.json
//   artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/04_post_reset_readback.txt
// The verdict comes from the Redfish message predicate rather than a literal equality here, because
// the registry version travels with the service (Base.1.16.0 on these units today) and a hard
// equality would stop matching silently the day a firmware bumps it.
// THE THREE ANSWERS A POST-RESET LOGIN CAN GIVE. THE INSTANT AND THE BODY TRAVEL IN EVERY ARM.
//
// The proving arm carries the MessageId THE SERVICE SENT, not one this module knows it will receive: the
// verdict is read by extdeps.bmc.redfish out of the body, and the id comes along with it. A mint that
// re-typed the expected id would make the check in recovery_readback_proves_reset circular -- it would be
// comparing a string this repository wrote against a string this repository wrote, and reporting agreement
// as though a BMC had been consulted. The registry version is the reason for the read: Base.1.16.0 today,
// whatever the service ships tomorrow, and only the body knows which.
//
// `observed_at` is on every arm rather than only the proving one, for the same reason the evidence ref is:
// an unproven readback is still a reading that happened at an instant, and a fold that compares it against
// the actuations needs the instant whether or not the message arrived. Dropping it from the failure arms
// would force any ordering check to be written around the sum, and the fold that does the ordering is exactly
// where a missing instant would quietly become "not checked".
// THE TWO WAYS A POST-RESET READING FAILS TO PROVE ANYTHING, DECLARED. They used to be one
// `detail: NonEmptyStr` on the inconclusive arm, which flattened a real distinction: "the body arrived and
// named no PasswordChangeRequired message" is an observation ABOUT THE SERVICE (the reset did not do what it
// was supposed to), while "the body never arrived, or was not JSON" is a fact about OUR CAPTURE and says
// nothing about the service at all (§4d: do not assert as deduced what is only inferred). Carried as
// sentences, swapping the two changed nothing a claim could see -- a mutation that reported a broken
// capture as a failed reset was undetectable. Now the two are arms, the claims match arms, and the fold
// that names the next action reads them apart.
type RecoveryReadbackGap
  = ReadbackNamedNoPasswordChangeMessage
  | ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: NonEmptyStr }

type RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
  = FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
      message_id: String
      evidence: EvidenceRef
      observed_at: CapturedInstant
    }
  | CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: CapturedInstant }
  | ReadbackInconclusive { cause: RecoveryReadbackGap, evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: CapturedInstant }

// THE ONLY MINT OF ProvenResetReadback, AND IT REFUSES. It used to return a Bool, which left the caller
// still holding the unproven readback and free to decide what to believe with it -- which is exactly how
// the hand-off arm stayed writable around a ReadbackInconclusive. An optional is the difference: Absent
// means there is no value to put in the arm, so no hand-off can be assembled from an unproven readback.
// The message identity is checked against extdeps.bmc.redfish's named constant rather than a re-typed
// literal, and neither of the two non-proving arms has a mint.
fn recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback) -> ProvenResetReadback? {
  match readback {
    FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange { message_id: message_id, evidence: evidence, observed_at: observed_at } =>
      // THE ID IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED -- and it is checked as the string that was read, so this predicate
      // and the mint above cannot agree with each other in the absence of a BMC: the only way a proving value
      // exists is for a body to have named a Base-registry PasswordChangeRequired and for that body's own
      // evidence ref to have been handed to recovery_readback_of with it.
      if redfish_message_id_is_password_change_required(id: message_id) {
        // BOUND BEFORE BEING WRAPPED, not nested inside `value:`: `value: ProvenResetReadback { ... }`
        // reads as a type annotation followed by a block, and the compiler resolved the two if-branches
        // to ProvenResetReadback vs Optional and refused the function.
        let proven_readback = ProvenResetReadback {
          message_id: message_id,
          evidence: evidence,
          observed_at: observed_at,
        }
        Present { value: proven_readback }
      } else {
        Absent
      }
    CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => Absent
    // the cause is not consulted here, and that is deliberate: NEITHER failure arm proves a reset, so a
    // mutation that swapped one for the other leaves this fold untouched and the hand-off still
    // unwritable. The discriminating input for that pair lives where the difference is READ (see
    // recovery_route_unavailability), not here.
    ReadbackInconclusive { cause: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => Absent
  }
}

// SEALED: ONE MINT, IN THIS MODULE (see recovery_readback_proves_reset). Its existence is what makes the
// hand-off arm unwritable without a readback that proves the reset: §5's "require a readback type that only
// a proving PasswordChangeRequired readback can construct", rather than a predicate asking afterwards. A
// value of this type IS the evidence -- message identity, the EvidenceRef that recorded it, the instant, and
// the capture digest.
// WHAT THE PROOF CARRIES IS WHAT THE READBACK CARRIED, and nothing else. There is deliberately no `capture`
// field here that the constructor could fill in by itself: an earlier version of this type held a
// `capture: EvidenceRef` that recovery_readback_proves_reset populated with a module-level constant naming
// the post-reset capture file. That made the proof constructor attach EVIDENCE TO A CLAIM IT HAD NOT READ --
// a standing could be shown pointing at the right bytes whoever had read them, and the digest a reader
// checked was a digest of the file this module chose rather than of the response the readback came from. The
// evidence ref now travels from the reading, through the readback, into the proof, unmodified and unfilled
// (see recovery_readback_of, which is the only thing that puts an EvidenceRef into a readback in the first
// place).
type ProvenResetReadback sole_constructor {
  message_id: String
  evidence: EvidenceRef
  observed_at: CapturedInstant
}

// The arms as the message decides them, so they cannot be chosen by taste: a body that does not name
// the message is inconclusive, and a readback is never built from an operator's account of the
// status line alone.
// THE ONE MINT OF A READBACK, AND IT ONLY TRANSFERS WHAT WAS READ. `body` is the bytes that came back over
// the SSIF/Redfish channel and `evidence` is the reference to THOSE bytes: the mint never invents an id and
// never supplies a capture, and every field of the result is either read out of the body or handed in by the
// caller that took the reading. That is what lets the proof constructor below be believed -- it has nothing
// it was not given.
fn recovery_readback_of(body: String, evidence: EvidenceRef, observed_at: CapturedInstant) -> RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback {
  match redfish_password_change_verdict(body: body) {
    RedfishPasswordChangeRequired { message_id: message_id } =>
      FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
        message_id: message_id,
        evidence: evidence,
        observed_at: observed_at,
      }
    // Read, and it does not name the message.
    RedfishPasswordChangeNotIndicated =>
      ReadbackInconclusive {
        cause: ReadbackNamedNoPasswordChangeMessage,
        evidence: evidence,
        observed_at: observed_at,
      }
    // NOT READ, which is a different fact and says so: the response body never arrived, or arrived truncated,
    // or was not JSON. Concluding "the service did not ask for a password change" from that would be an
    // inference about a controller drawn from the state of our own capture (§4d).
    RedfishResponseBodyUnreadable { cause: cause } =>
      ReadbackInconclusive {
        cause: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead {
          why: join(["the post-reset response could not be read as a Redfish body: ", cause as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        },
        evidence: evidence,
        observed_at: observed_at,
      }
  }
}

// ── THE ROUTE RECORD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// CARRIED FIELDS, AND WHY. The rescue host address and the in-band device node are this route's own
// facts -- nothing else in the intake records that a rescue OS sat at that address, on that node --
// so they live here. The CONTROLLER is carried as the observed BmcAccessObservation rather than as an
// endpoint string, so the recovery is checked against the SAME endpoint the control-route join checks;
// a bare endpoint would be a second, unchecked copy of a fact whose owner is elsewhere, which is the
// reflash-staleness defect cut 2 just deleted.
//
// WHY THE WRITE POLICY IS NOT A FIELD. A field is a slot a caller fills: a route standing in front of any
// board could carry ASRock's measured refusal and pass the gate on a destructive ResetAll with it. A policy that justifies wiping a
// chassis has to be a function of the controller that is actually there, so the record carries the
// IN-BAND IDENTITY INSTEAD -- the Get Device ID answer the run read for itself over /dev/ipmi0, which is
// what a locked BMC will answer without a credential -- and every gate derives the policy from it,
// through one lookup that returns a row only for the controller that measurement was taken under. There
// is no longer any way to tell the route which firmware it is talking to.
type RecoveryInBandRoute {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  rescue_host: RescueHostReachability
  in_band: InBandChannelObservation
  controller: BmcAccessObservation
  in_band_device_identity: IpmiDeviceIdentity
}

// THE BOUND JOIN: UNIT, CONTROLLER AND READBACK AS ONE CLAIM, MINTED NOWHERE ELSE.
//
// WHY A JOIN AND NOT THREE FIELDS. A standing used to carry the controller observation, the proven readback
// and a writes list side by side, and the fold asserted each was well-formed: the readback named a
// PasswordChangeRequired id, the writes had both actuations, the controller named an endpoint. Every one of
// those could hold while the readback came from a DIFFERENT BOARD, because no check crossed the boundary
// between them — the same shape of defect as a signed certificate with the wrong hostname in it, where each
// field is valid and the pair is a lie. So the binding is put in the type instead: there is exactly one way
// to build this carrier, it is sealed, and the only way through it is a lookup of the filed post-reset
// capture BY THE CONTROLLER'S IPMI IDENTITY. Absent from that lookup is not a missing row, it is a refusal —
// no bound join, no hand-off arm, no standing that can be mistaken for a recovered unit (§5: "a failure arm
// refuses, never widens").
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: it does not re-check the MessageId. That read happened in
// recovery_readback_of, from the body, and the id travels as the string the service sent — re-checking it
// here from a constant would put the constant back into the module that is supposed to be reading.
type RecoveryBoundRecoveryObservation sole_constructor {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  controller: BmcAccessObservation
  in_band_device_identity: IpmiDeviceIdentity
  readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
}

// THE ONLY ROUTE INTO THAT CARRIER.
//
// Three things have to be true and the third is the one that was never asked:
//   1. the observation and the route are about one unit — matched, not compared loosely;
//   2. the readback was read at all, and carries the bytes it was read from;
//   3. those bytes are the ones FILED AGAINST THE CONTROLLER THIS ROUTE TOUCHED over its in-band channel.
// (3) is the join. The filed row is keyed by the controller's IPMI identity (device id, manufacturer id,
// product id, firmware major/minor) in gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_inband_recovery_receipts, so a readback
// citing bytes from another specimen fails the lookup even when its own fields are impeccable, and a board
// nobody post-reset-read has no row to cite. Both come back Absent, and the fold turns Absent into a named
// refusal rather than a hand-off.
fn recovery_bind_observation(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback,
) -> Optional<RecoveryBoundRecoveryObservation> {
  // (1) THE ROUTE MUST BE BINDABLE AT ALL: one unit, one controller, one address that agrees with what
  // the BMC itself reported. recovery_bind_refusal is the whole question, arm by arm; the bind refuses on
  // ANY refusal and the fold recomputes the SAME function to report which arm fired -- the cause is the
  // same Optional the bind consulted, never a paraphrase of it.
  match recovery_bind_refusal(route: route) {
    Present { value: _ } => Absent
    Absent =>
    // (2) the bytes must be there to be checked. Every arm of the readback carries its evidence ref now,
    // including the inconclusive one, so this extracts rather than asks a question of the sum.
    match recovery_readback_evidence(readback: readback) {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: cited } =>
        // (3) THE JOIN. Looked up by the controller's identity; the cited bytes must be the bytes filed for
        // that identity. Both halves are needed: identity alone would pass a standing that cited nothing,
        // and digest alone would pass a capture filed against a different board.
        match asrock_altrad8ud_post_reset_readback_record_for(observed: route.in_band_device_identity) {
          Absent => Absent
          Present { value: record } =>
            if record.capture != cited {
              Absent
            } else {
              Present {
                value: RecoveryBoundRecoveryObservation {
                  subject: route.subject,
                  controller: route.controller,
                  in_band_device_identity: route.in_band_device_identity,
                  readback: readback,
                },
              }
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

// WHAT A BIND REFUSES, IN THE TERMS THE REPAIR NEEDS. The fold that reports a refused bind must send
// someone to a DIFFERENT repair per cause -- re-file the bytes, re-point the endpoint, stop -- so the
// refusal is a declared sum, and the one site that renders advice reads its arms (DESIGN section 5: a
// failing path fails with a typed, located diagnostic; section 4c: a status belongs in a carrier, not a
// string). One prose string for five causes is how a swapped cause keeps passing the controls that match
// on a word. Absent from the Optional below means "no refusal" -- and the fold's no-cause arm stays the
// bytes-never-filed case, which is a separate refusal with its own sentence downstream.
type RecoveryBindRefusal
  // THE UNIT THE CHANNEL OBSERVATION CLAIMS AND THE UNIT THE ROUTE DRIVES ARE NOT ONE UNIT. The channel
  // observation is a carrier a caller constructs; the route's subject is what the fold's admissions are
  // bound to (each admission names the subject it authorises for). A standing that bound the readings of
  // two chassis would satisfy every other check this module has -- which is exactly the defect the bind
  // exists to stop, so the comparison is demanded at construction of the bound carrier, not assumed
  // because both sides came from `route` a few lines up.
  = RecoveryBindSubjectsDisagree { route_subject: MachineIntakeSubject, channel_subject: MachineIntakeSubject }
  // THE CHANNEL NEVER SAID WHAT ADDRESS THE BMC HAS. The bind's controller join asks the BMC, over the
  // in-band channel, what it is; a capture that never recorded the answer cannot vouch for anything, and
  // a bind built from what was not read is a fabricated pairing (DESIGN section 5: no plausible output
  // is fabricated). The repair is a re-capture, not a re-typing of a better-looking address.
  | RecoveryBindChannelReportedNoAddress { endpoint_host: NonEmptyStr }
  // THE ENDPOINT IS NOT A TEXTUAL ADDRESS AT ALL. The Redfish reading's endpoint is wire text; parsing is
  // the ipv4 module's own parser, which REFUSES malformed input with the located error rather than
  // inventing an address. The error is carried, not flattened -- "three octets" and "octet 3 = 299" send
  // someone to different lines of a different file.
  | RecoveryBindEndpointNotAnAddress { endpoint_host: NonEmptyStr, error: Ipv4ParseError }
  // THE KNOCK AND THE SELF-REPORT DISAGREE: someone addressed the Redfish endpoint at one address while
  // the BMC, asked over SSIF which cannot be pointed elsewhere, named another. Two machines are in the
  // room. Both hosts are carried so the refusal says which was knocked and which spoke.
  | RecoveryBindEndpointDisagreesWithChannelReport {
      endpoint_host: NonEmptyStr
      reported_host: NonEmptyStr
    }
  // THE FIRMWARE ANSWERS DISAGREE: the Redfish release string against the release IPMI's Get Device ID
  // publishes for the same field, across transports (IPMI publishes major.minor only -- the auxiliary
  // byte is not part of a release identity -- so those two numbers are what both sides genuinely hold in
  // common, and the Redfish string must be exactly them, dotted). It can fail, which is the test of
  // whether it is a check: feed a controller reading whose release is 9.99 against an in-band 3.22 and
  // the bind below refuses.
  | RecoveryBindReadingsDisagreeOnRelease { redfish_release: NonEmptyStr, ipmi_release: NonEmptyStr }

// THE BIND'S REFUSALS, IN THE ORDER THE REPAIRS ARE CHEAPEST TO TRY. WHY A FUNCTION AND NOT THE BOOL
// `recovery_controller_readings_agree` IT REPLACES: a Bool over two records whose meaning was "these two
// readings agree on what they read" threw away WHICH reading disagreed, and the fold that reports the
// refusal needs to say so -- the refusal carries the fields each repair needs. WHY THE ORDER: the
// subject check is about the machine being driven at all, so it answers first; "was the address ever
// read" precedes "does it parse", which precedes "does it agree" -- each later question presupposes the
// earlier answer exists. A missing self-report is NOT folded into disagreement: inventing an address to
// compare against would be the fabrication this module exists to refuse.
// THE ipv4 PARSE ERROR SAID WHERE IT HURT; the refusal carries it and this is the only site that reads
// it aloud. Each arm names the position or count the parser located, because "three octets" and "octet
// 3 = 299" send the reader to different lines of a different file.
fn recovery_ipv4_parse_error_text(error: Ipv4ParseError) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match error {
    Ipv4WrongOctetCount { observed_count: n } =>
      join([to_string(value: n), " octets where four were expected"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    Ipv4OctetNotNumeric { position: p, raw: raw } =>
      join(["octet ", to_string(value: p), " (", raw as String, ") is not a number"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    Ipv4OctetOutOfRange { position: p, value: v } =>
      join(["octet ", to_string(value: p), " is ", to_string(value: v), ", outside 0..255"], "") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn recovery_bind_refusal(route: RecoveryInBandRoute) -> Optional<RecoveryBindRefusal> {
  // (1) THE OBSERVATION MUST BE ABOUT THE UNIT THE ROUTE DRIVES. Every consumer used to read the subject
  // off the route -- both fields came from the same construction site, so the observation's own subject
  // was read by NO fold and a channel observation of another unit passed as this unit's. That is §3c's
  // dangling fact wearing a carrier's clothes, and it is why this line exists.
  if route.in_band.subject != route.subject {
    Present {
      value: RecoveryBindSubjectsDisagree {
        route_subject: route.subject,
        channel_subject: route.in_band.subject,
      },
    }
  } else {
    match route.in_band.bmc_self_reported_address {
      // (2) THE CHANNEL MUST HAVE SAID WHAT THE BMC IS, before anyone can be told the endpoint is wrong.
      Absent =>
        Present { value: RecoveryBindChannelReportedNoAddress { endpoint_host: route.controller.endpoint.host } }
      Present { value: reported } =>
        match parse_ipv4_address(text: route.controller.endpoint.host as String) {
          // (3) THE ENDPOINT MUST BE A TEXTUAL ADDRESS. The parser's own located error is carried.
          Ipv4ParseFailed { error: error } =>
            Present {
              value: RecoveryBindEndpointNotAnAddress {
                endpoint_host: route.controller.endpoint.host,
                error: error,
              },
            }
          Ipv4Parsed { address: endpoint_address } =>
            // (4) THE KNOCK MUST MATCH THE SELF-REPORT. Rendered through the ipv4 module's own renderer,
            // so two addresses are compared as what they ARE, not as how someone typed them.
            if render_ipv4_address(addr: endpoint_address) != render_ipv4_address(addr: reported) {
              Present {
                value: RecoveryBindEndpointDisagreesWithChannelReport {
                  endpoint_host: route.controller.endpoint.host,
                  reported_host: render_ipv4_address(addr: reported),
                },
              }
            } else {
              // (5) THE FIRMWARE ANSWERS MUST AGREE, across transports, as before.
              let ipmi_release = join([to_string(value: route.in_band_device_identity.firmware_revision_major),
                                      ".",
                                      to_string(value: route.in_band_device_identity.firmware_revision_minor)], "")
              if route.controller.identity.firmware.raw_version != ipmi_release {
                Present {
                  value: RecoveryBindReadingsDisagreeOnRelease {
                    redfish_release: route.controller.identity.firmware.raw_version,
                    ipmi_release: ipmi_release,
                  },
                }
              } else {
                Absent
              }
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE CITED BYTES, from whichever arm the readback landed in.
fn recovery_readback_evidence(readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback) -> Optional<EvidenceRef> {
  match readback {
    FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange { message_id: _, evidence: evidence, observed_at: _ } =>
      Present { value: evidence }
    CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: evidence, observed_at: _ } => Present { value: evidence }
    ReadbackInconclusive { cause: _, evidence: evidence, observed_at: _ } => Present { value: evidence }
  }
}

// ── THE TERMINAL STANDING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ONE SEALED CARRIER, AND THE PROOF ARM IS ITS ONLY SUCCESS ARM. RecoveryRouteProofComplete carries
// no credential and no binding: it carries what the route PROVED -- the observed controller, the
// bound join, the readback that justifies believing the factory credential is active, and the ledger
// of both privileged writes. It claims nothing past that. In particular no fold re-enters onboarding
// through it: the factory-login-then-rotate consumer that would give this arm its production purpose
// does not exist yet, and its absence is a pending frontier row in
// gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route_frontier, not a sentence in this file. Every other arm is a
// named reason the route stopped, and each is a DIFFERENT next action for whoever is standing at the
// machine.
type InBandRecoveryOutcome
  = RecoveryRouteProofComplete {
      // ONE BOUND JOIN, NOT THREE FACTS THAT HAPPEN TO BE TRUE. This field used to be the
      // BmcAccessObservation: an endpoint host string and a firmware triple a caller could type by hand,
      // sitting next to a readback whose evidence ref this module attached by itself. Each fact was
      // individually well-formed and nothing bound them: the standing said "controller X", "readback Y",
      // "evidence Z" and the fold checked that Y's id was a PasswordChangeRequired id and that Z was the
      // file this module names. None of those checks asked whether the readback came from X.
      //
      // recovery_bound_recovery_observation is now the single thing that says unit, controller and readback
      // are about the same machine: it is sealed, its only mint looks the filed capture up BY THE CONTROLLER'S
      // IPMI IDENTITY, and the fold consumes the join rather than re-assembling its parts. A standing whose
      // readback cites bytes taken from another board has no value to put in this field (§5: construction over
      // validation).
      bound: RecoveryBoundRecoveryObservation
      // SEALED, AND MINTED ONLY FROM A READBACK THAT PROVES THE RESET. The only mint of
      // ProvenResetReadback is recovery_readback_proves_reset, it answers Absent unless the message identity
      // matches, and the type is sole_constructor so nothing outside this module can build one. A hand-off
      // around a ReadbackInconclusive is therefore not a value to be caught, it is a value that cannot be
      // written -- §5's construction over validation, applied to the arm rather than to a check on it.
      proven: ProvenResetReadback
      writes: List<RecoveryWriteRecord>
    }
  | RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }
  | RecoveryChannelUnobserved { refusal: RecoveryChannelAbsence }
  // What the catalog said about the controller under foot, kept in its own arms: the reset's warrant is a
  // measurement, and a reader has to be able to tell "nobody measured this controller" from "our filing
  // is self-contradictory". Neither is a Bool, and neither is reachable by a caller supplying a policy --
  // the route carries only what it observed.
  | RecoveryControllerPolicyUnavailable { sighting: PublishedPasswordWritePolicySighting }
  | RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: RecoveryActuationKind }
  | RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  // THE CENSUS REFUSED, AND THE CENSUS'S OWN DETAIL COMES ALONG. This arm exists so the ledger's verdict
  // survives the whole way out: the gate carries it, the standing carries it, and the consumer that turns a
  // standing into a next action reads it from here. Rendering it into the sibling arm's sentence would have
  // re-made, one layer down, the loss the carrier was added to prevent.
  | RecoveryWriteRefusedByLedger { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, site: DeclarationRef, cause: LedgerRefusalCause }
  | RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback }
  // THE READBACK ANSWERED, BUT IT IS NOT THIS CONTROLLER'S ANSWER. The proof above asks whether the body
  // names the password-change refusal; this asks the question the fold never used to ask -- whether that body
  // came from the board in front of us. A standing that paired a genuine 403 from one specimen with the
  // route of another would have satisfied every check this module had before the bound join existed, so this
  // arm exists to say which of the two readings disagreed and to keep the readback with the refusal. The
  // cause is the same Optional the bind consulted, not a paraphrase of it: Absent here means the two readings
  // agreed and it was the FILED BYTES that were not this controller's.
  | RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
      cause: Optional<RecoveryBindRefusal>
    }

type InBandRecoveryStanding sole_constructor {
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute
  outcome: InBandRecoveryOutcome
}

// THE HAND-OFF PROOF IS AN ARM'S FIELD, NOT A PREDICATE OVER THE OUTCOME. A Bool over the outcome sum that
// re-asks the readback question is validation standing where construction was available (§5), and a
// true/false-per-arm helper is the shape this module's transport note argues against. The arm carries
// ProvenResetReadback, which has exactly one mint, so the question cannot be posed wrongly; consumers match
// the outcome arms where they are visible (recovery_route_unavailability, the access_discover seam, and the
// claims below). A `_ => false` helper would be a Bool wearing the claim's clothes.

// ── THE CONVERGENCE STEPS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ORDER IS NOT A COMMENT. Each step's precondition names the step before it, and a step whose
// precondition was not read back in this run refuses. A recovery is exactly the sequence an operator
// might be tempted to enter in the middle -- a reset without a channel observation, or a handoff
// without a readback -- and the fold, not a prose rule, is what refuses it.
data recovery_reachable_host_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_host_refusal",
)

data recovery_channel_observation_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "in_band_channel_admission",
)

data recovery_in_band_password_write_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "admit_recovery_in_band_password_write",
)

data recovery_reset_to_defaults_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults",
)

data recovery_factory_credential_readback_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "recovery_readback_proves_reset",
)

// The hand-off step names the fold, because the fold is the declaration that WRITES the hand-off arm -- and
// it can only write it holding a ProvenResetReadback. A step named after a predicate would point at a check
// that performs nothing; this one points at the actuation that performs it.
data recovery_route_proof_step: DeclarationRef = decl_ref(
  module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
  decl_name: "converge_in_band_recovery",
)

// WHY THE ROUTE CARRIES NO operator_approval / reset_readback FIELD. It used to, and nothing read them:
// the reset's approval is the STANDING THE RUN ACTS UNDER, which the gate takes as its own argument
// (`under_ruling: StandingDestructiveAuthorization`, whose DeclarationRef it inspects) and the census
// row classifies. A copy of "which declaration approves this" sitting on the route is a second
// representation of one fact (§2/§3) that could disagree with the ruling actually presented -- and a
// decorative field that looks like a control is worse than no field, because a reader assumes the check
// happens. The readback is likewise carried where it is PROVED (the standing's
// RecoveryRouteProofComplete.readback, whose content the gate checks) rather than named.
fn recovery_convergence_steps() -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  [
    recovery_reachable_host_step,
    recovery_channel_observation_step,
    recovery_in_band_password_write_step,
    recovery_reset_to_defaults_step,
    recovery_factory_credential_readback_step,
    recovery_route_proof_step,
  ]
}

// What the proof arm requires to have been read back first. The readback step is required as a STEP and
// its content is required again in the standing: one makes the sequence honest, the other makes the
// standing unprovable from an operator's word that the reset "looked like it worked".
fn recovery_proof_requires() -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  [
    recovery_reachable_host_step,
    recovery_channel_observation_step,
    recovery_reset_to_defaults_step,
    recovery_factory_credential_readback_step,
  ]
}

// THE ONE CONSTRUCTOR, AND WHAT IT REFUSES. Every step is checked here, so there is no path to
// RecoveryRouteProofComplete that skips the readback, ignores a NotMeasured firmware, enters on a
// declared-but-unexecuted medium, or accepts a PreserveNetworkAndUsers as a recovery. The writes are
// checked for having been ATTEMPTED against the arm the route names (their own byte capture is not the
// success condition, per RecoveryWriteRecord); the readback is checked for HAVING PROVEN the factory
// credential is active.
fn converge_in_band_recovery(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  in_band_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  reset_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback,
  in_band_admission: PasswordWriteAuthorization,
  reset_admission: ResetWriteAuthorization,
) -> InBandRecoveryStanding {
  // COMPUTED ONCE FOR THE WHOLE FOLD. The standing below carries this verdict's own reason, so the
  // refusal a reader sees is the one this fold computed -- a second call could answer differently about
  // the same observation, and an invented arm would be a fabricated output (§5).
  let channel = in_band_channel_admission(observation: route.in_band, reachability: route.rescue_host)
  match recovery_host_refusal(reachability: route.rescue_host) {
    Present { value: c } =>
      InBandRecoveryStanding { subject: route.subject, route: route, outcome: RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: c } }
    Absent =>
      // ONE CALL TO THE MATCH, AND WHAT IT COMPUTED IS WHAT THE STANDING CARRIES. No sentence is composed
      // here, so a standing cannot describe an absence the fold did not find (§5: "no plausible output is
      // fabricated"), and the next action a reader is given derives from the arm in
      // recovery_route_unavailability rather than being guessed at print time.
      match channel {
        // THE REFUSED ARM SPEAKS FOR ITSELF: the reason the match computed goes straight into the standing,
        // and there is no arm here for the admitted case to leak a wrong reason into.
        ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
          InBandRecoveryStanding {
            subject: route.subject,
            route: route,
            outcome: RecoveryChannelUnobserved { refusal: reason },
          }
        ChannelAdmits =>
          if match recovery_policy_sighted_on_controller(route: route) {
            RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => false
            ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: _ } => true
            CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => true
            CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => true
            RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => true
          } {
          // The reset's warrant has to be a measurement OF THIS CONTROLLER, so the outcome carries which
          // of the two refusals it is: "never measured here" sends someone to measure, "the catalog's two
          // indexes disagree" is a defect in this repository. Collapsing them would order a re-measurement
          // of a board that is already filed.
          InBandRecoveryStanding {
            subject: route.subject,
            route: route,
            outcome: RecoveryControllerPolicyUnavailable { sighting: recovery_policy_sighted_on_controller(route: route) },
          }
        } else {
          // EACH ADMISSION IS MATCHED EXACTLY ONCE, and the match is what routes the refusal: the refused
          // arm's own cause is what the outcome carries, so there is no invented "unreachable" arm and no
          // Bool asked first and then re-matched to build the answer. Which gate ISSUED an admission is a
          // separate, legitimate question (a comparison of two declarations), asked after the arm is known.
          //
          // Ordering is deliberate: the password refusal is reported before the reset refusal, because the
          // password write is the earlier actuation -- reporting the later failure first would send someone
          // to fix the reset while the write that precedes it is still refused.
          match in_band_admission {
            PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: site, cause: cause } =>
              InBandRecoveryStanding {
                subject: route.subject,
                route: route,
                outcome: RecoveryWriteRefusedByLedger {
                  kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
                  site: site,
                  cause: cause,
                },
              }
            PasswordWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
              InBandRecoveryStanding {
                subject: route.subject,
                route: route,
                outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                  kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
                  cause: cause,
                },
              }
            PasswordWriteAdmitted { ticket: ticket } =>
              if declaration_ref_eq(a: ticket.authorized_by, b: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) == false {
                InBandRecoveryStanding {
                  subject: route.subject,
                  route: route,
                  outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                    kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
                    cause: "the password admission was issued by a declaration other than the gate its census row is filed at" as NonEmptyStr,
                  },
                }
              } else if ticket.subject != route.subject
                || ticket.operation != recovery_in_band_password_actuation() {
                // THE ADMISSION IS NOT ABOUT THIS WRITE, OR NOT ABOUT THIS CHASSIS. The sealed ticket is what
                // makes this check possible at all: it carries the subject the gate read and the actuation it
                // verified, so a password admission minted for one unit cannot be spent on another, and one
                // naming a different operation is refused here rather than re-derived downstream (side-chat
                // HOLD blocker 1: authorization-bearing payloads bound to subject + operation).
                InBandRecoveryStanding {
                  subject: route.subject,
                  route: route,
                  outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                    kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
                    cause: "the password admission names a different subject or operation than this route and its in-band write" as NonEmptyStr,
                  },
                }
              } else {
                match reset_admission {
                  ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: site, cause: cause } =>
                    InBandRecoveryStanding {
                      subject: route.subject,
                      route: route,
                      outcome: RecoveryWriteRefusedByLedger {
                        kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
                        site: site,
                        cause: cause,
                      },
                    }
                  ResetWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
                    InBandRecoveryStanding {
                      subject: route.subject,
                      route: route,
                      outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                        kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
                        cause: cause,
                      },
                    }
                  ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: reset_ticket } =>
                    if declaration_ref_eq(a: reset_ticket.authorized_by, b: recovery_manager_reset_site()) == false {
                      InBandRecoveryStanding {
                        subject: route.subject,
                        route: route,
                        outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                          kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
                          cause: "the reset admission was issued by a declaration other than the gate its census row is filed at" as NonEmptyStr,
                        },
                      }
                    } else if reset_ticket.subject != route.subject
                      || reset_ticket.operation != recovery_reset_actuation() {
                      InBandRecoveryStanding {
                        subject: route.subject,
                        route: route,
                        outcome: RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted {
                          kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
                          cause: "the reset admission names a different subject or operation than this route and its ResetToDefaults write" as NonEmptyStr,
                        },
                      }
                    } else {
                      converge_after_admissions(
                        route: route,
                        in_band_write: in_band_write,
                        reset_write: reset_write,
                        readback: readback,
                      )
                    }
                }
              }
          }
      }
    }
    }
}

// THE LAST THREE CHECKS, run only once BOTH writes are admitted by their own gates: each write must be
// recorded as attempted, and the readback must be what PROVES the reset happened. Split out so the
// admission matches above stay readable -- the ordering of these three is unchanged.
fn converge_after_admissions(
  route: RecoveryInBandRoute,
  in_band_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  reset_write: RecoveryWriteRecord,
  readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback,
) -> InBandRecoveryStanding
  // THE STANDING AND ITS HAND-OFF ARE PRODUCED IN ONE PLACE. A helper that could assemble a completed
  // standing from any arguments would let a caller skip reachability, the channel, the per-release policy
  // and the hold -- so the fold that checked all of them is its only caller, and a standing for a unit
  // nobody held has no value to put in the hand-off arm.
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "converge_in_band_recovery"),
  ]
{
  if recovery_write_was_attempted(record: in_band_write, kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword },
        }
      } else if recovery_write_was_attempted(record: reset_write, kind: recovery_reset_actuation()) == false {
        InBandRecoveryStanding {
          subject: route.subject,
          route: route,
          outcome: RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: recovery_reset_actuation() },
        }
      } else {
        // THE READBACK IS ASKED ONCE, AND THE MATCH ROUTES THE OUTCOME -- no Bool in between, no second
        // ask, no arm that could hold a hand-off without a proof. Absent has nowhere to go but the refusal
        // arm; the hand-off arm below is the only place ProvenResetReadback is ever consumed, and nothing
        // outside this module can build one (type ProvenResetReadback sole_constructor), so the value in
        // it is this mint's answer about THIS readback and nothing else.
        // THE PROOF AND THE BINDING ARE BOTH ASKED, AND EITHER CAN REFUSE. The proof answers whether the
        // readback names the password-change refusal; the binding answers whether that readback, its cited
        // bytes and the controller this route touched are one machine. The hand-off needs both, and the
        // bound carrier is what carries the proven value -- so a hand-off with an unproven readback, or a
        // proven readback bound to another board, is not a case to handle but a value that does not exist.
        match recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: readback) {
          Absent =>
            InBandRecoveryStanding {
              subject: route.subject,
              route: route,
              outcome: RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: readback },
            }
          Present { value: proven } =>
            match recovery_bind_observation(route: route, readback: readback) {
              Absent =>
                InBandRecoveryStanding {
                  subject: route.subject,
                  route: route,
                  outcome: RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
                    readback: readback,
                    // the cause is recomputed by the SAME FUNCTION the bind consulted, so what the
                    // operator reads is the refusal itself, in the refusal's own fields -- not a
                    // paraphrase of it, and not a second question that could answer differently
                    cause: recovery_bind_refusal(route: route),
                  },
                }
              Present { value: bound } =>
                InBandRecoveryStanding {
                  subject: route.subject,
                  route: route,
                  outcome: RecoveryRouteProofComplete {
                    bound: bound,
                    proven: proven,
                    writes: [in_band_write, reset_write],
                  },
                }
            }
        }
      }
}

// WHAT A MEASUREMENT IS ABOUT, when one exists. Only the refusal arm names a release; the catalog's
// NotMeasured arm is a claim about a release someone looked up and did not find, which is produced by
// the release-keyed lookup and never by a sighting -- so there is no firmware to report for it, and this
// returns none rather than inventing one.
fn recovery_policy_firmware(policy: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy) -> BmcFirmwareVersion? {
  match policy {
    FirmwareRefusesPublishedPassword { firmware: f, completion_code: _, capture: _, measured_under: _ } => Present { value: f }
    NotMeasured { firmware: _ } => Absent
  }
}

// WHY THERE IS NO `recovery_reset_is_authorized_by_measurement(Bool)` ANY MORE. It existed to answer
// "may we reset?" with one bit over the policy coproduct, and the comment above it promised there would
// be no boolean while the function below returned exactly one -- a lie with a working implementation. A
// Bool there collapsed THREE distinguishable states ("measured on this controller", "nobody has ever
// measured this controller", "our catalog contradicts itself about the row") into yes/no, and the three
// demand opposite repairs. Every consumer now matches the arms of PublishedPasswordWritePolicySighting
// directly, which is the same question asked without discarding the answer's content.

// THE PROOF IS AN OUTCOME ARM, NOT A RECORD THIS MODULE BUILDS. The standing's own
// RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound, proven, writes } arm already IS the proof -- sealed to its
// subject, naming the controller it was observed at and the readback that proved it. A second container for
// the same facts is a second representation of them (§2), free to disagree with the arm about which
// controller was recovered -- which is how a reflash-staleness defect gets built. And a predicate over a
// value nobody constructs is a declaration with a hypothetical consumer (§3c). Whatever eventually consumes
// the proof builds what it needs from the standing it is handed; this module's dependency stays one-way and
// it mints neither a BmcSecureStanding nor a binding.

// THE ROUTE'S REFUSAL, AS ONE CAUSE THE CONSUMER CAN BRANCH ON -- carried on the AccessDiscover arm
// in gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge. RecoveryRouteUnavailable is deliberately NOT
// AccessObserveRefusal's ControllerUnreachable: that one says the controller did not answer
// out-of-band, and the whole point of this module is that the controller may be answering perfectly
// well while being unreachable TO US. Collapsing the two would make a recoverable unit look identical
// to a dead one, which is how a board that needs a USB stick gets quarantined.
type RecoveryRouteUnavailable
  = NoReachableRescueHost { cause: RecoveryRequiresReachableHostOS }
  // The controller under foot is not the one the published-password refusal was measured on: the
  // destructive reset has no warrant here, and the reading that says so travels with the refusal so it can
  // be checked against the capture. This is what replaced a caller-supplied policy -- the route cannot be
  // told which firmware it is talking to, only shown what it observed.
  | RecoveryControllerNeverMeasured { observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity }
  // Two refusal rows answer one Get Device ID reading: the measurement exists, and the catalog
  // contradicts itself about it. The reading AND the disagreeing rows travel with the refusal, so the
  // repair (de-duplicate the catalog) is locatable from the refusal alone -- and this is NOT the
  // never-measured arm, which would send someone to measure a board that was measured twice.
  | RecoveryCatalogAmbiguousForController {
      observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity
      matched: List<PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy>
    }
  // A row was found by reading and then failed the release-keyed lookup: the catalog disagrees with
  // itself, so no release can be named for the measurement the reset would rest on.
  | RecoveryCatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: BmcFirmwareVersion, observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity }
  // A refusal row was matched to the controller and then contradicted its own filing. Distinct from
  // RecoveryControllerNeverMeasured: the board HAS a measurement, and sending someone to take another one
  // is how a mislabelled row survives. The row travels with the refusal.
  | RecoveryRowFiledUnderTheWrongReading {
      row: PublishedFactoryPasswordWritePolicy
      observed: IpmiDeviceIdentity
    }
  // THE GAP TRAVELS AS THE SUM, not as a sentence about it: the two gaps send different people to
  // different work, and a reader downstream has to be able to branch on which one this is.
  | RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { gap: RecoveryReadbackGap }
  // THE FOUR WAYS THIS ROUTE IS BLOCKED, KEPT APART WHERE THEY WERE KEPT APART UPSTREAM. These three arms
  // exist because the projection used to flatten them into RecoveryReadbackInconclusive above, which was a
  // loss twice over. It told a person monitoring a half-populated ledger to re-read a controller, and it
  // erased the distinction the outcome sum had gone to the trouble of drawing -- §5's "every degradation is
  // typed, located and countable" is worthless if the counting layer collapses the types on the way out.
  // Each one names its own repair, and none of the three is "re-read the controller".
  | RecoveryChannelNeverObserved { refusal: RecoveryChannelAbsence }
  | RecoveryWriteNeverAttempted { kind: RecoveryActuationKind }
  | RecoveryWriteWithheldByAdmission { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  // The readback arrived and did NOT name the password-change refusal: the reset's effect is unconfirmed,
  // which is a different question from "no readback was taken" and a different one again from "the census
  // would not admit the write". Distinct from RecoveryReadbackInconclusive, which is for a readback that
  // could not be read at all.
  | RecoveryReadbackDoesNotConfirmReset { readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback }
  // THE READBACK BELONGS TO ANOTHER CONTROLLER, and the disagreement between the two readings is carried
  // rather than summarised: a reader of the refusal sees which release each side reported.
  | RecoveryReadbackFromAnotherController {
      readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback
      refusal: RecoveryBindRefusal
    }
  // THE TWO READINGS AGREE AND THE BYTES ARE STILL NOT THIS BOARD'S: the readback cites a capture that was
  // never filed against this controller's IPMI identity. Distinguished from the arm above because the repair
  // is different -- one is a mis-paired route, this one is a citation that came from somewhere else.
  | RecoveryReadbackBytesNotFiledForThisController { readback: RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback }
  // WHICH WRITE THE CENSUS WOULD NOT ADMIT, WITH WHAT THE CENSUS COMPUTED. Distinct from
  // RecoveryReadbackInconclusive because the two send different people to different work: one re-reads a
  // controller, the other fixes a census row or funds an evidence need.
  | RecoveryCensusDoesNotAdmit { kind: RecoveryActuationKind, site: DeclarationRef, cause: LedgerRefusalCause }

// THERE IS NO `recovery_route_is_available`. One question about a standing gets one name; a second name
// for it is a second representation, free to disagree with the first. Consumers match the outcome arms.

// The next PHYSICAL action, spelled out of the named cause. Two of these need nobody at the machine
// and one does; the consumer tells them apart only because the cause is named rather than collapsed
// into one "unavailable".
fn recovery_route_next_action(unavailable: RecoveryRouteUnavailable) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match unavailable {
    NoReachableRescueHost { cause: c } =>
      match c {
        NoRescueHostAddressResponded { address: _, ssh_port: _ } =>
          "bring up the rescue OS on the board (insert the prepared rescue medium, or netboot it once a netboot provider is executed) -- nothing is reachable at the rescue address" as NonEmptyStr
        RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: _, missing_modules: m } =>
          join(["the rescue host is up but has no in-band IPMI: load ",
                to_string(value: list_length(items: m)),
                " missing ipmi module(s); without ",
                ipmi_device_node_canonical_text(node: recovery_ipmi_device_node) as String,
                " there is no route to the controller from inside the chassis"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        // A DIFFERENT DRIVER IS BOUND, which is a different fix from a missing one -- hence a sentence of
        // its own naming both nodes, rather than the module sentence with an empty list in it.
        RescueHostReachedAtTheWrongDeviceNode { address: _, expected: e, reported: r } =>
          join(["the rescue host is up but not on the in-band node this route reads: found ",
                ipmi_device_node_canonical_text(node: r) as String, ", expected ",
                ipmi_device_node_canonical_text(node: e) as String,
                " -- check which ipmi driver is bound, not which are missing"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        RescueMediumNotExecuted =>
          "the only rescue provider modelled for this unit is declared and has never been executed; use the executed medium or treat the unit as physical-only" as NonEmptyStr
      }
    RecoveryControllerNeverMeasured { observed: _ } =>
      "the published-password refusal was never measured on THIS controller: read its Get Device ID answer against the board catalog, or measure it, before spending a destructive reset" as NonEmptyStr
    // the repair NAMES the defect and its size, so nobody goes and re-measures a board that was
    // measured twice -- which is exactly what the never-measured sentence above would have sent them to do
    RecoveryCatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: matched } =>
      join(["de-duplicate this board's password-write catalog: ",
            to_string(value: list_length(items: matched)),
            " refusal rows answer one Get Device ID reading, so no single refusal can warrant a reset",
            " -- the refusal carries the rows; reconcile them against the capture"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    RecoveryCatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } =>
      "this board's catalog files a refusal whose release key does not resolve in its own capability catalog: fix the filing before any reset is admitted" as NonEmptyStr
    // NOT the sentence above, and not the never-measured one: this row resolves, and it is the row's own
    // two readings that disagree, so the repair is to the row rather than to the catalog's index or to a
    // new measurement of a board that has been measured.
    RecoveryRowFiledUnderTheWrongReading { row: _, observed: _ } =>
      "this board's catalog files a refusal whose release does not match the Get Device ID answer recorded with it: correct the row, or measure the controller again under the release it now claims -- re-measuring alone leaves the bad row filed" as NonEmptyStr
    // TWO GAPS, TWO SENTENCES, BECAUSE THEY ARE NOT TWO WAYS OF SAYING THE SAME THING.
    // A body that arrived and named no password-change message is an ANSWER FROM THE CONTROLLER: the
    // reset did not do what it was asked, so the work is the reset and the controller. A body that
    // never arrived is a hole in OUR notebook, and telling someone to re-check a BMC they may have reset
    // correctly is the wrong instruction to print from a broken capture -- the work is to take the
    // reading again. Flattened into one string, a mutation that printed "take the reading again" for a
    // controller that never asked for a password change was invisible to every claim here.
    RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { gap: ReadbackNamedNoPasswordChangeMessage } =>
      "the controller answered the post-reset login without naming a password change: the reset did not put the user table back, so re-run it under the same ruling and read the login again" as NonEmptyStr
    RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { gap: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: _ } } =>
      "the post-reset login response was never readable as a Redfish body, so nothing about the controller is known yet: re-take the reading before touching the reset again" as NonEmptyStr
    // The channel was never looked at. No module is loaded and no node is bound for want of a reading that
    // nobody took, so the repair is the observation itself -- not the module-loading sentence above, which
    // presumes a reading that already reported missing modules.
    RecoveryChannelNeverObserved { refusal: _ } =>
      "take the in-band channel observation this route has never had: read the device node and the ipmi module set on the rescue host before any privileged write is admitted" as NonEmptyStr
    // An un-attempted write is not a failed write. Re-reading the controller answers nothing, because the
    // chassis was never touched; the missing actuation is named with its own kind so the operator knows
    // which of the two writes is outstanding.
    RecoveryWriteNeverAttempted { kind: kind } =>
      join(["the ", match kind {
              InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword => "in-band temporary administrator password write"
              RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: _ } => "Manager.ResetToDefaults actuation"
            } as String,
            " of this unit's reset sequence was never attempted: perform it, or hand the unit back as un-recovered",
            " -- re-reading the controller proves nothing about an action nobody took"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    // The census would not admit it, and the detail the census computed is carried rather than paraphrased.
    RecoveryWriteWithheldByAdmission { kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
      join(["the ", match kind {
              InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword => "in-band temporary administrator password write"
              RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: _ } => "Manager.ResetToDefaults actuation"
            } as String,
            " is withheld by this route's admission, not by the board: ", cause as String,
            " -- no re-read lifts an admission, only the admission does"], "") as NonEmptyStr
    // The readback came back and said something else. Distinct sentence from RecoveryReadbackInconclusive
    // because the readback is not the thing at fault here: the reset's EFFECT is unconfirmed, so the next
    // action is to establish the effect, not to fetch a body that was already fetched.
    RecoveryReadbackDoesNotConfirmReset { readback: _ } =>
      "the post-reset login answered, but not with the password-change refusal that proves the factory credential took: establish the reset's effect before this unit can be called recovered" as NonEmptyStr
    // NOT the sentence above: nothing is wrong with the answer, it is the wrong board's answer, and
    // re-establishing a reset effect on this unit would not move it.
    // THE ONE SITE THAT RENDERS THE BIND REFUSAL INTO ADVICE, arm by arm, from the refusal's own fields.
    // The sum carries structured facts precisely so that rendering lives here and nowhere else: a
    // consumer that wanted the sentence would have to re-derive it, and re-derivation is where two
    // causes start sharing a sentence. Each arm below sends the reader to different work, which is the
    // test that the typing was worth having.
    RecoveryReadbackFromAnotherController { readback: _, refusal: refusal } =>
      match refusal {
        RecoveryBindSubjectsDisagree { route_subject: rs, channel_subject: cs } =>
          join(["the channel observation is a reading of unit ", cs.subject.unit_key as String,
                " while the route drives ", rs.subject.unit_key as String,
                " -- two readings of two different machines: re-read the channel of the unit this route drives"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        RecoveryBindChannelReportedNoAddress { endpoint_host: h } =>
          join(["the in-band capture never recorded the address the BMC reports for itself, so nothing can be ",
                "matched against the endpoint ", h as String,
                " -- re-capture the channel with the BMC's own address"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        RecoveryBindEndpointNotAnAddress { endpoint_host: h, error: e } =>
          join(["the controller endpoint ", h as String, " is not an address (",
                recovery_ipv4_parse_error_text(error: e) as String,
                ") -- the Redfish reading cannot be compared with what the BMC reported"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        RecoveryBindEndpointDisagreesWithChannelReport { endpoint_host: h, reported_host: r } =>
          join(["the Redfish reading knocked ", h as String, " while the BMC, asked over the in-band channel, ",
                "named ", r as String, " -- two controllers: address the one that spoke"], "") as NonEmptyStr
        RecoveryBindReadingsDisagreeOnRelease { redfish_release: rf, ipmi_release: ip } =>
          join(["the Redfish reading reported firmware ", rf as String,
                " while the in-band Get Device ID reported ", ip as String,
                " -- two readings of two different controllers: pair the route, the readings and the capture for one board"], "") as NonEmptyStr
      }
    RecoveryReadbackBytesNotFiledForThisController { readback: _ } =>
      "the readback cites capture bytes that were never filed against this controller's Get Device ID answer: file the reading with the controller it came from, or read this one" as NonEmptyStr
    // The census refusal's sentence is DERIVED from the carrier it holds, never written beside it: which
    // write was refused, at which site, and what the census said about why.
    RecoveryCensusDoesNotAdmit { kind: _, site: site, cause: cause } =>
      join(["fix the ledger before touching the board again: ",
            ledger_refusal_text(site_ref: site, cause: cause) as String,
            " -- no credential actuation is admitted until the row admits it"], "") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn recovery_route_unavailability(standing: InBandRecoveryStanding) -> RecoveryRouteUnavailable? {
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => none
    RecoveryHostUnreachable { cause: c } => Present { value: NoReachableRescueHost { cause: c } }
    // THE CAUSE IS CARRIED OUT AS THE CAUSE THAT WAS COMPUTED UPSTREAM.
    // A standing whose channel was never observed used to arrive here as RecoveryReadbackInconclusive, and
    // the sentence rendered from that arm sent someone to re-read a controller nobody had written to (§5:
    // "every degradation is typed, located and countable" -- which the counting layer voids if it collapses
    // the types on the way out).
    RecoveryChannelUnobserved { refusal: r } => Present { value: RecoveryChannelNeverObserved { refusal: r } }
    RecoveryControllerPolicyUnavailable { sighting: sighting } =>
      match sighting {
        RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => none
        ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: observed } => Present { value: RecoveryControllerNeverMeasured { observed: observed } }
        CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: observed, matched: matched } =>
          Present { value: RecoveryCatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: observed, matched: matched } }
        CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: f, observed: observed } =>
          Present { value: RecoveryCatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: f, observed: observed } }
        RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: row, observed: observed } =>
          Present { value: RecoveryRowFiledUnderTheWrongReading { row: row, observed: observed } }
      }
    // An arm naming an un-attempted write arrives naming it, with the kind: which of the two writes is
    // outstanding is the whole content of the refusal, and it was previously dropped on the floor (kind: _)
    // while the sentence said "was not attempted against the arm the route names" -- the same fact, once,
    // in the arm that holds it.
    RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: kind } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryWriteNeverAttempted { kind: kind } }
    // A write the ledger does not admit leaves the unit locked AND the route blocked, so it is a
    // named requirement rather than a silent stop: the reader has to be told which write was refused,
    // because "go find a rescue USB" would be the wrong next action for it.
    RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryWriteWithheldByAdmission { kind: kind, cause: cause } }
    RecoveryWriteRefusedByLedger { kind: kind, site: site, cause: cause } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryCensusDoesNotAdmit { kind: kind, site: site, cause: cause } }
    // The readback itself travels: which answer arrived is what an operator re-checks against the capture,
    // and the arm is not the "could not be read" arm -- the body was read, and it did not confirm the reset.
    RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: r } =>
      Present { value: RecoveryReadbackDoesNotConfirmReset { readback: r } }
    // AND IT IS NOT THE SAME REFUSAL AS THE ONE ABOVE, which is the point. Collapsing "the body said the
    // wrong thing" with "the body was not this controller's" would send someone to re-read a board that is
    // fine, chasing a firmware answer nobody disputed.
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController { readback: r, cause: cause } =>
      match cause {
        Present { value: refusal } =>
          Present { value: RecoveryReadbackFromAnotherController { readback: r, refusal: refusal } }
        Absent =>
          // no bind refusal at all means the readings agreed and it was the FILED BYTES that were not
          // this controller's -- the separate refusal with its own sentence downstream
          Present { value: RecoveryReadbackBytesNotFiledForThisController { readback: r } }
      }
  }
}

// ── COMMITTED EVIDENCE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// CITED BY SHA256, AND ONLY WHAT WAS CAPTURED. These digests are the entries of
// artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/SHA256SUMS, so a standing that claims a
// capture is checked against the file bytes and not against a sentence about them. The capture is
// committed at 518342bf41d on session/royal-seal-46 (the run was executed there); until those paths
// land on main, a digest below is verifiable against THAT commit and no other -- check it there before
// treating a standing as evidenced, and treat it as unevidenced if the commit is unreachable.
//
//   02_inband_channel.txt -- the rescue OS (Linux ubuntu-server 6.8.0-139-generic aarch64,
//     openssh-server: the HOST OS and not the BMC), /dev/ipmi0 crw------- 235,0, the four ipmi
//     modules, `mc info` (Firmware Revision 3.22, ASRock Rack), `channel 1 info`, `user list 1`
//     (id 1 root ADMINISTRATOR), `lan print 1`, and the two refused `user set password` calls.
//   04_post_reset_readback.txt -- the post-reset login with the published factory credential,
//     answered HTTP 403 with Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired. Named .txt because it is an
//     annotated capture (### request/status lines around the body), not a JSON document, and it
//     holds NO firmware/version context -- the 3.22 attribution comes from `mc info` in 02, and the
//     authenticated GET that reported FirmwareVersion 3.22.00 saved no body. The README says so.
//
// NOT cited, because it was not captured: the in-band temporary-password write, and the ResetToDefaults
// request/response bodies. The capture README records both as in-session-observed; the temporary
// password was wiped by the reset and cannot be reproduced. That is what
// RecoveryWriteRecord::ExecutedWithoutByteCapture is for, and why the standing's success arm leans on
// the readback instead.
data recovery_inband_channel_capture: Sha256Digest = Sha256Digest {
  hex: "8c1750d13a6c9832b459a61b8aadab246f4e74f138d85861e2758200c07202a4",
}

data recovery_post_reset_readback_capture: Sha256Digest = Sha256Digest {
  hex: "9837b3a8e47ff2ec59ecc3ddd41b81c7f3757b3d47082bec969d1fb75be5751c",
}

data recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref: EvidenceRef = EvidenceRef {
  label: "artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/02_inband_channel.txt",
  digest: Sha256Hash(recovery_inband_channel_capture),
}

data recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref: EvidenceRef = EvidenceRef {
  label: "artifacts/bmc/altrad8ud-1l2t-6-inband-recovery-2026-10-16/04_post_reset_readback.txt",
  digest: Sha256Hash(recovery_post_reset_readback_capture),
}

// ── DOMAIN LAWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each states a non-invertibility this module depends on, and each is built only through the mints --
// a reachable host is unreachable until the drivers are present and the medium executed, and a
// standing is handed off only once the readback names the right message. A fixture that hand-built a
// reachable host or a standing would be proving the test and not the module.

test fn a_rescue_host_without_the_ipmi_stack_is_unreachable_and_says_which_module_is_missing() -> Bool {
  let reached = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
    ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence {
      device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
      kernel_modules: ["ipmi_devintf", "ipmi_msghandler", "acpi_ipmi"],
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    },
    medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
  )
  match reached {
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: a, missing_modules: m } } =>
      a == recovery_rescue_host_address()
        && list_length(items: m) == 1
        // The refusal names the driver, so it can be read: LinuxKernelModuleName is branded, so the name
        // is compared as text rather than re-typed as a branded literal a second time (§3 -- a second
        // spelling of one name is a second place to be wrong about it). With the count pinned to 1 above,
        // "contains ipmi_ssif" is exact rather than merely inclusive.
        && (m |> any(x => (x as String) == "ipmi_ssif"))
    _ => false
  }
}

// THE WRONG-NODE REFUSAL, MADE EXECUTABLE. One Bool answered false for two different mistakes, and the
// refusal built from it took its missing-module list from a function that returns the empty list when the
// DEVICE NODE is the problem. So a host at the wrong node was reported as "up, but no in-band IPMI: load
// the missing ipmi modules" with nothing listed to load -- the sentence blamed drivers for a mistake about
// a node, and the next action sent someone to insmod nothing. Each mistake is its own arm now, carrying
// what was seen, so this claim can ask a wrong node to arrive as the node it is: both nodes named, and not
// wearing the module-refusal's clothes.
test fn a_wrong_device_node_is_refused_as_the_node_it_is_and_names_both_nodes() -> Bool {
  let at_the_wrong_node = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
    ipmi: InBandIpmiPresence {
      // the node the freeipmi/IMB side addresses rather than the /dev/ipmi0 the KCS route reads
      device_node: IpmiImbDeviceNode,
      kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    },
    medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
  )
  match at_the_wrong_node {
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: RescueHostReachedAtTheWrongDeviceNode { address: a, expected: e, reported: r } } =>
      a == recovery_rescue_host_address()
        // BOTH NODES ARE CARRIED: "not the one we read through" is unactionable without the one found.
        && e == recovery_ipmi_device_node
        && r == IpmiImbDeviceNode
        // and the module refusal is NOT the one produced, which is the defect this replaces
        && match recovery_host_refusal(reachability: at_the_wrong_node) {
             Present { value: RescueHostReachedWithoutInBandIpmi { address: _, missing_modules: m } } =>
               // an empty list here is exactly the old lie: blame the drivers, name no driver
               list_length(items: m) > 0
             Present { value: _ } => true
             Absent => false
           }
    RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _ } => false
    RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

test fn a_declared_netboot_provider_never_satisfies_the_precondition_and_is_not_the_same_refusal_as_no_host() -> Bool {
  let via_netboot = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
    ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
    medium: NetbootRescueMediumDeclared,
  )
  match recovery_host_refusal(reachability: via_netboot) {
    Present { value: RescueMediumNotExecuted } =>
      // and it landed on the UNREACHABLE arm, matched as the coproduct rather than through a Bool that
      // would restate the same decision a second time
      match via_netboot {
        RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => true
        RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _ } => false
      }
    _ => false
  }
}

test fn a_channel_carried_in_from_another_host_admits_nothing() -> Bool {
  let reached = rescue_host_reachable(
    address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
    ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
    medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
  )
  let observation = InBandChannelObservation {
    subject: recovery_test_subject(),
    rescue_host_address: recovery_controller_address(),
    device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
    kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
    channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: OpenIpmiDriver },
    administrator: recovery_administrator_account(),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
    device_node_created_at: recovery_device_node_creation_instant(),
    bmc_self_reported_address: Present { value: recovery_controller_address() },
  }
  // WHY THE VERDICT IS MATCHED RATHER THAN ASKED FOR AS A BOOL. A Bool here could say "refused" without
  // saying why, and then this claim would stay green for a channel refused over its modules, its account,
  // or its timestamp -- which are four other facts, each with its own claim below. So the arm is named:
  // the address mismatch has to be the reason, not merely a reason.
  (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: observation, reachability: reached) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceHostAddressDisagrees
  })
    // THE REFUSAL IS ABOUT THE ADDRESS AND NOTHING ELSE. Same observation, same reachability, only the
    // address it names changed, and it lands on the ADMITTED arm: if the guard above refused for some
    // reason the observation also has, this half goes red rather than agreeing with it.
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: InBandChannelObservation {
        subject: observation.subject,
        rescue_host_address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
        device_node: observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: observation.kernel_modules,
        channel: observation.channel,
        administrator: observation.administrator,
        evidence: observation.evidence,
        observed_at: observation.observed_at,
        device_node_created_at: observation.device_node_created_at,
        bmc_self_reported_address: observation.bmc_self_reported_address,
      },
      reachability: reached,
    ) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
    // and the reachability this was checked against really is the reachable arm -- otherwise both halves
    // above are vacuous, since an unreachable host refuses every channel alike.
    && (match reached {
        RescueHostReachable { address: _, ssh_port: _, ipmi: _, medium: _ } => true
        RescueHostUnreachable { cause: _ } => false
      })
}

// THE ADMITTED CHANNEL FIXTURE, SHARED BY THE EIGHT PER-ARM CLAIMS BELOW.
//
// WHY A FIXTURE AND NOT EIGHT COPIES. Each claim below changes ONE field of an observation that the
// admission admits, and requires the ONE refusal that field causes. For that to mean anything the rest
// of the observation has to be exactly the admitted one, eight times over; nine copies of nine fields
// is nine ways for two of them to drift apart and for a claim to go green while testing nothing. The
// control half of every claim is the unmodified fixture, so a claim cannot pass because the fixture
// itself was broken -- that half would be red.
type AdmittedChannelFixture {
  observation: InBandChannelObservation
  reachability: RescueHostReachability
}

fn recovery_admitted_channel_fixture() -> AdmittedChannelFixture {
  AdmittedChannelFixture {
    observation: InBandChannelObservation {
      subject: recovery_test_subject(),
      rescue_host_address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
      kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
      channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: OpenIpmiDriver },
      administrator: recovery_administrator_account(),
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
      device_node_created_at: recovery_device_node_creation_instant(),
      bmc_self_reported_address: Present { value: recovery_controller_address() },
    },
    reachability: rescue_host_reachable(
      address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
      ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
      medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
    ),
  }
}

// THE REST OF THE OBSERVATION, UNCHANGED. Every perturbed claim writes the nine fields explicitly,
// taking eight of them from the fixture, so the field under test is the only visible difference
// between the claim's observation and the admitted one.
fn channel_carries_credential(word: IpmiArgvCredentialOption) -> InBandAccessWithoutCredential {
  InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: OpenIpmiDriver, credential_option: word }
}

fn channel_account_at(
  fixture: AdmittedChannelFixture,
  privilege: IpmiChannelPrivilegeLimit,
) -> InBandAdministratorAccount {
  InBandAdministratorAccount {
    access: ipmi_user_channel_access(channel: 1, user: 1, privilege: privilege),
    evidence: fixture.observation.administrator.evidence,
  }
}

fn recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
  fixture: AdmittedChannelFixture,
  rescue_host_address: Ipv4Address,
  device_node: IpmiDeviceNode,
  kernel_modules: List<LinuxKernelModuleName>,
  channel: InBandAccessWithoutCredential,
  administrator: InBandAdministratorAccount,
  observed_at: CapturedInstant,
  device_node_created_at: CapturedInstant,
) -> InBandChannelObservation {
  InBandChannelObservation {
    subject: fixture.observation.subject,
    rescue_host_address: rescue_host_address,
    device_node: device_node,
    kernel_modules: kernel_modules,
    channel: channel,
    administrator: administrator,
    evidence: fixture.observation.evidence,
    observed_at: observed_at,
    device_node_created_at: device_node_created_at,
    bmc_self_reported_address: Present { value: recovery_controller_address() },
  }
}

// NO RESCUE HOST REFUSES THE CHANNEL, AND THE ARM SAYS IT WAS THE HOST. An observation that passes every
// channel test still admits nothing when the host it was read on is not reachable: the reading would be
// bytes from a machine nobody reached. The control is the same observation against a reachable host.
test fn a_channel_read_on_a_host_nobody_reached_admits_nothing() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  let unreachable = RescueHostUnreachable {
    cause: NoRescueHostAddressResponded {
      address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
    },
  }
  (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: unreachable) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceNoRescueHost
  })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// A CHANNEL THAT CARRIED A CREDENTIAL IS REFUSED WITH THE WORD THAT CARRIED IT. The option name has to
// survive into the refusal: an operator told "your channel carried a credential" needs to know which word
// to delete from the invocation, and the parse already knew, so throwing it away would be a second loss
// of a fact the model carries. -P and -E are checked apart because they are different arms.
test fn the_credential_bearing_channel_names_the_option_that_carried_it() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: channel_carries_credential(IpmiArgvPassword),
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
      reason == AbsenceChannelCarriedCredential { credential_option: IpmiArgvPassword }
  })
    // the environment arm is not folded onto the command-line arm: same refusal shape, different word
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
        fixture: f,
        rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
        device_node: f.observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
        channel: channel_carries_credential(IpmiArgvPasswordFromEnv),
        administrator: f.observation.administrator,
        observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
        device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
      ),
      reachability: f.reachability,
    ) {
      ChannelAdmits => false
      ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
        reason == AbsenceChannelCarriedCredential { credential_option: IpmiArgvPasswordFromEnv }
    })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// A CHANNEL THAT WAS NEVER READ KEEPS THE CAUSE THE PARSE FOUND. The failure this shape closes:
// the six parse refusals used to be flattened into one prose string, so a reader could not tell "no -I"
// from "-I named an out-of-band transport". Each is checked to arrive as itself, with its own payload.
test fn a_channel_never_read_carries_the_parse_refusal_that_caused_it() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  let out_of_band = RefusalTransportOutOfBand {
    wire: "lanplus",
    why_not_in_band: "lanplus reaches the BMC over the network from another machine" as NonEmptyStr,
  }
  let transport_absent = RefusalTransportAbsent {
    why: "no -I was given, so ipmitool would fall back to the build-time default interface" as NonEmptyStr,
  }
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: out_of_band },
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceChannelNeverRead { refusal: out_of_band }
  })
    // a DIFFERENT cause has to arrive DIFFERENTLY: if the two were collapsed, one of these two halves
    // would be comparing against the wrong refusal and go red rather than agreeing.
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
        fixture: f,
        rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
        device_node: f.observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
        channel: InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: transport_absent },
        administrator: f.observation.administrator,
        observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
        device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
      ),
      reachability: f.reachability,
    ) {
      ChannelAdmits => false
      ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceChannelNeverRead { refusal: transport_absent }
    })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// AN ACCOUNT THAT IS NOT ADMINISTRATOR IS REFUSED WITH WHAT WAS REPORTED. The six published privilege
// levels must not collapse to one bit: NO ACCESS and OPERATOR are different diagnoses, and the byte the
// controller printed exists, so the arm carries it.
test fn an_account_that_is_not_administrator_names_the_privilege_it_reported() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: f.observation.channel,
      administrator: channel_account_at(fixture: f, privilege: IpmiPrivilegeOperator),
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
      reason == AbsenceAccountIsNotAdministrator { reported: IpmiPrivilegeOperator }
  })
    // NO ACCESS is not the same refusal as OPERATOR -- this is the collapse the old Bool made
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
        fixture: f,
        rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
        device_node: f.observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
        channel: f.observation.channel,
        administrator: channel_account_at(fixture: f, privilege: IpmiPrivilegeNoAccess),
        observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
        device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
      ),
      reachability: f.reachability,
    ) {
      ChannelAdmits => false
      ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
        reason == AbsenceAccountIsNotAdministrator { reported: IpmiPrivilegeNoAccess }
    })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// A BAND THAT WILL NOT CONSTRUCT IS REPORTED AS THAT, NOT AS AN INVERSION. The ordering used to answer
// Bool, and both "the pair is out of order" and "one of the bands refused to build" came back false, so
// the fold told the operator the queue was created after the reading when it had never compared the two.
// A band whose bounds invert is minted here on purpose -- captured_band is the module's own mint, so this
// is the same construction a mis-transcribed capture line goes through. Each half names the SIDE that
// failed, which is the whole point: a device-node time that will not build is a defect in the device-node
// time, and a re-reading of the channel cannot repair it. Both halves carry the mint's own words, so the
// refusal does not paraphrase std.interval's diagnosis.
test fn a_capture_band_that_refuses_to_construct_is_not_reported_as_an_inversion() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  // the day's 06:27:00 as the HIGH bound and 06:59:59 as the LOW: a pair no reading can license
  let inverted: CapturedInstant = captured_band(
    low: captured_epoch_millisecond(day: recovery_capture_day(), hour: 6, minute: 59, second: 59),
    high: captured_epoch_millisecond(day: recovery_capture_day(), hour: 6, minute: 27, second: 0),
  )
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: f.observation.channel,
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: inverted,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    // THE ARM IS THE ASSERTION. AbsenceNodeCreatedAfterTheReading would be the lie this fix exists to
    // stop: the ordering never happened, so nothing says the node came after the reading.
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
      match reason {
        // THE MINT'S OWN WORDS, NOT A PARAPHRASE. This is an assertion about CONTENT and it can go red:
        // std.interval's refusal says "interval endpoints reversed: low exceeds high", and a fold that
        // substituted its own short label would still be non-empty, so length would not notice. Checking
        // the sentence is what keeps the refusal traceable to the mint that refused.
        AbsenceEarlierCaptureBandRefuses { reason: r } =>
          string_contains(s: r, pattern: "low exceeds high")
        _ => false
      }
    ChannelAdmits => false
  })
    // the other side: an unreadable READING time is a different defect from an unreadable NODE time, and
    // collapsing them would send the operator to re-capture the wrong line
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
        fixture: f,
        rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
        device_node: f.observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
        channel: f.observation.channel,
        administrator: f.observation.administrator,
        observed_at: inverted,
        device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
      ),
      reachability: f.reachability,
    ) {
      ChannelRefused { reason: reason } =>
        match reason {
          AbsenceLaterCaptureBandRefuses { reason: r } => string_contains(s: r, pattern: "low exceeds high")
          _ => false
        }
      ChannelAdmits => false
    })
    // and the admitted pair still admits: the two new arms must not become the easy answer
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE DEVICE NODE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE PRESENCE THE SSH SESSION REPORTED, NOT AGAINST NOTHING. The
// fixture reads /dev/ipmi0; an observation naming /dev/ipmi1 is a different interface, and the admission
// has to say so by arm. The control is the same observation with the node the session reported.
test fn a_channel_read_through_a_different_device_node_is_refused_by_that_arm() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 1 },
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: f.observation.channel,
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceDeviceNodeDisagrees
  })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE READING HAS TO FOLLOW THE NODE, NOT PRECEDE IT. The device node is created when the in-band driver
// binds, so a channel reading stamped BEFORE the node existed was not read through that node -- it is a
// row from some other moment. The claim swaps the two times and requires the ordering arm; the unswapped
// pair admits. (This is the ordering the artifact README states: the node's mtime is earlier than the
// re-capture, so the admitted direction is the one the bytes actually show.)
test fn a_channel_reading_stamped_before_the_device_node_existed_is_refused() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: f.observation.kernel_modules,
      channel: f.observation.channel,
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: recovery_device_node_creation_instant(),
      device_node_created_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceNodeCreatedAfterTheReading
  })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE MODULE SETS ARE COMPARED BOTH WAYS, SO NEITHER AN ABSENCE NOR AN ADDITION ADMITS. The channel is
// read through the loaded modules; a host that reports a module the reading does not have, or one the
// reading has and the host lacks, means the reading and the session are not the same machine state. The
// extra name below is one the rescue image does not carry at all, so its presence is unambiguous.
test fn a_channel_whose_modules_differ_from_the_hosts_are_refused() -> Bool {
  let f = recovery_admitted_channel_fixture()
  let extra: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> = [
    "ipmi_ssif",
    "ipmi_devintf",
    "ipmi_msghandler",
    "acpi_ipmi",
    "ipmi_watchdog",
  ]
  let short: List<LinuxKernelModuleName> = ["ipmi_ssif", "ipmi_devintf", "ipmi_msghandler"]
  (match in_band_channel_admission(
    observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
      fixture: f,
      rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
      device_node: f.observation.device_node,
      kernel_modules: extra,
      channel: f.observation.channel,
      administrator: f.observation.administrator,
      observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
      device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
    ),
    reachability: f.reachability,
  ) {
    ChannelAdmits => false
    ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceKernelModulesDisagree
  })
    // the other direction: the reading is MISSING a module the session reported, which is a different
    // mismatch and the guard compares both ways, so this half has to refuse too
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(
      observation: recovery_perturbed_channel_observation(
        fixture: f,
        rescue_host_address: f.observation.rescue_host_address,
        device_node: f.observation.device_node,
        kernel_modules: short,
        channel: f.observation.channel,
        administrator: f.observation.administrator,
        observed_at: f.observation.observed_at,
        device_node_created_at: f.observation.device_node_created_at,
      ),
      reachability: f.reachability,
    ) {
      ChannelAdmits => false
      ChannelRefused { reason: reason } => reason == AbsenceKernelModulesDisagree
    })
    && (match in_band_channel_admission(observation: f.observation, reachability: f.reachability) {
      ChannelAdmits => true
      ChannelRefused { reason: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE CREDENTIAL DECISION IS MADE BY THE PARSE, AND THE ADMITTED ARM CANNOT HOLD A CREDENTIAL. Parts, each
// a different way this could be wrong:
//   - an argv that passed -P lands on the credential-bearing arm, naming WHICH option, and the same argv
//     with no credential option lands on the admitted arm -- both through in_band_channel_from_argv, so
//     the side is decided once, at the parse, not at each consumer;
//   - the admitted arm has NO field that could hold a credential, which makes "passwordless, with
//     -P 0penBmc" unconstructible: this claim has no case for it, and
//     neither does any gate;
//   - an out-of-band -I name cannot reach either arm, so there is no channel value to hand the password
//     gate that would admit a write over a transport requiring a secret;
//   - and the case the SPLIT authority got wrong, which is what one authority is for: `-f <file>` was not
//     in the credential scan at all while -I lanplus was ignored by the transport side. The words that
//     stood for it are asserted here, and no arm of this parse lets them through as passwordless.
test fn a_channel_that_passed_a_credential_on_the_command_line_is_not_passwordless() -> Bool {
  (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-P", "0penBmc", "user", "set", "password", "1", "0penBmc"]) {
    // refused, and it says which option did it: -P is ipmitool's password option (src/ipmitool.c)
    InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: t, credential_option: opt } =>
      t == OpenIpmiDriver && opt == IpmiArgvPassword
    _ => false
  })
    // -U alone is enough to leave the admitted arm: naming the user is a credential-bearing invocation
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-U", "root", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: _, credential_option: opt } => opt == IpmiArgvUserName
      _ => false
    })
    // -f is the one the old scan missed: ipmitool reads the session password from that FILE, so naming a
    // file is a credential-bearing invocation whether or not the transport is the in-band one
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-f", "/tmp/pw", "user", "set", "password", "1", "x"]) {
      InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: _, credential_option: opt } => opt == IpmiArgvPasswordFile
      _ => false
    })
    // -E is its OWN arm, not a second name for -P: the secret was never on the line, so no bytes exist for
    // a reader of the capture to cite. Collapsing them would claim more than the capture holds.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-E", "user", "set", "password", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelCredentialCarriedOnArgv { transport: _, credential_option: opt } => opt == IpmiArgvPasswordFromEnv
      _ => false
    })
    // THE HEADLINE FIXTURE. The transport named here is out of band, so there is no channel value at all:
    // the old shape answered "passwordless over OpenIPMI", because interface_name said open and -f was
    // invisible to the credential scan. A credential write over an authenticating remote transport is the
    // failure extdeps/bmc/ipmi.dag was built to make unconstructible.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "lanplus", "-f", "/tmp/pw", "-H", "10.0.0.5", "user", "set", "password", "1", "0penBmc"]) {
      // THE ARM IS THE ASSERTION. An invocation that both names an out-of-band transport and passes a
      // password file must land on the never-observed arm, not on the arm naming a credential option: had
      // the parse decided credentials first, this pattern could not match and the conjunct would be false.
      // The reason text is NOT checked. An earlier version demanded it not contain "credential", which was
      // unsatisfiable -- the published reason for lanplus says it REQUIRES a credential, which is true and
      // is the reason to refuse. Checking that prose would have tested wording, not the decision.
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportOutOfBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: _ } } =>
        w == "lanplus"
      _ => false
    })
    // the argv the capture actually used: no credential option, so the credential-free arm
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: t } => t == OpenIpmiDriver
      _ => false
    })
    // a name ipmitool does not implement is refused at the parse and reaches no arm. The name travels in
    // the WORDS now, not beside them: there is nothing left for a caller to get wrong.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "ssif", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportUnrecognized { wire: w, why: _ } } => w == "ssif"
      _ => false
    })
    // and an out-of-band name cannot produce a channel at all: no value exists for the gate to be fooled by
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "lanplus", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportOutOfBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: _ } } =>
        // only the wire is checked: why_not_in_band is NonEmptyStr, so "it is not empty" is a type fact,
        // not something this claim could ever make go red
        w == "lanplus"
      _ => false
    })
    // LAST -I WINS, which only an authority that pairs -I with its argument can see (case 'I' in
    // lib/ipmi_main.c assigns intfname on every hit; ipmi_intf_load runs after the loop). An earlier -I
    // open does not launder a later -I lanplus.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-I", "lanplus", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportOutOfBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: _ } } => w == "lanplus"
      _ => false
    })
    // THE WORD AFTER -I IS AN ARGUMENT, NEVER A CREDENTIAL AND NEVER A VERB. "-U" sitting where a transport
    // name belongs is consumed as the transport name (and refused as one), not counted as a user option --
    // the distinction the whole-line scan could not draw.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "-U", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportUnrecognized { wire: w, why: _ } } => w == "-U"
      _ => false
    })
    // AN OPTION THAT NEEDS A VALUE AND GETS THE END OF THE WORDS IS UPSTREAM'S ERROR, not a pass: "P:"
    // carries a colon in OPTION_STRING (lib/ipmi_main.c:82), so the missing argument is refused with the
    // option named, rather than defaulting to the credential-free arm.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "user", "set", "password", "1", "-P"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalOptionNeedsValue { option: o, why: _ } } => o == "-P"
      _ => false
    })
    // getopt's attached-value spelling is NOT guessed at: "-P0penBmc" is one word this table does not
    // name, and upstream's getopt would have aborted on it too. Refusing beats guessing where it divides.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-P0penBmc", "user", "set", "password", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalUnmodelledOption { option: o, why: _ } } => o == "-P0penBmc"
      _ => false
    })
    // a long option is not an option at all in this build -- there is no getopt_long (lib/ipmi_main.c:371
    // calls plain getopt), so "--password" is an unknown word, refused rather than read as -P.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "--password", "user", "set", "password", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalUnmodelledOption { option: o, why: _ } } => o == "--password"
      _ => false
    })
    // an option that changes how a write is AUTHENTICATED is refused by name, not passed through: -k is the
    // IPMIv2 Kg key. Passing it through would let a keyed session be read as the passwordless channel.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-k", "0x01", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalUnmodelledOption { option: o, why: _ } } => o == "-k"
      _ => false
    })
    // -H beside an in-band name is a contradiction, and the contradiction is refused rather than resolved
    // in favour of the arm that lets the write through.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-H", "10.0.0.5", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalRemoteHostWithInBandName { host: _, wire: w, why: _ } } => w == "open"
      _ => false
    })
}

// THE TRANSPORT IS CLOSED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THE SIDE IS DECIDED ONCE, BY THE PARSE. Review
// 81235's ask, stated as an executable law. Three things have to hold together, because each one is a
// different way the old shape could fail:
//   - the transport the captured unit really used (`open`, the OpenIPMI driver over /dev/ipmi0) comes
//     back on the IN-BAND side. The earlier deny-list refused it, which would have blocked the only
//     route that exists for this board;
//   - a transport that authenticates by a secret comes back on the OTHER side, with a reason, so a
//     reader learns why without looking it up -- and it cannot be coaxed into the in-band arm, because
//     the arms are different types;
//   - a name that is not an interface at all (a kernel module's name, a casing change, a trailing
//     space, the empty string) is refused AT THE PARSE. ipmitool dispatches by strcmp against
//     ipmi_intf_table, so none of those are transports it would load, and inventing one in front of a
//     destructive reset is the failure this exists to prevent.
test fn the_transport_sums_place_every_wire_name_on_exactly_one_side() -> Bool {
  (match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "open") {
    IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: w, transport: InBandHostTransportChannel { transport: t } } =>
      w == "open" && t == OpenIpmiDriver
    IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, transport: NotInBandHostTransport { transport: _, why_not_in_band: _ } } => false
    IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => false
  })
    // out-of-band: the parse answers with the other sum AND says why, so the refusal is not a bare no
    && (match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "lanplus") {
      IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, transport: NotInBandHostTransport { transport: t, why_not_in_band: _ } } =>
        // the OTHER SUM is the assertion; the reason field is NonEmptyStr, so length is a type fact
        t == LanPlusTransport
      _ => false
    })
    // in-band arms the parse can produce, obtained through the parse rather than asserted
    && (match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "bmc") {
      IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, transport: InBandHostTransportChannel { transport: t } } => t == BmcKernelDriver
      _ => false
    })
    && (match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "lan") {
      IpmiCommandInterfaceParsed { wire: _, transport: NotInBandHostTransport { transport: t, why_not_in_band: _ } } => t == LanTransport
      _ => false
    })
    // refused at the PARSE: names that are not interfaces, in any spelling
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "ssif") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: w } => w == "ssif" _ => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "LANPLUS") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true _ => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "lanplus ") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true _ => false }
    && match ipmi_command_interface_from_wire(wire: "") { IpmiCommandInterfaceUnrecognized { wire: _ } => true _ => false }
    // THE CONSEQUENCE THAT MAKES THIS MORE THAN A RENAME: the only channel values that exist are the
    // credential-free arm, the arm naming a credential option, and the arm saying the channel was never
    // read. There is no value in between for a gate to be fooled by, and no Bool a consumer can skip.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
    // AND NO -I IS REFUSED, NOT DEFAULTED. The old shape called words with no -I at all a passwordless
    // OpenIPMI channel, because the transport arrived from outside the words. What ipmitool would actually
    // use is DEFAULT_INTF, which ./configure bakes in per-build (configure.ac --with-intf=...), so it is a
    // fact about the installed binary and not about this capture: 00_README.txt records only that -I
    // lanplus was ABSENT. The name has to travel in the words or the channel is not observed.
    && (match in_band_channel_from_argv(argv_words: ["user", "list", "1"]) {
      InBandChannelNotObserved { refusal: RefusalTransportAbsent { why: _ } } => true
      _ => false
    })
    && (match in_band_command_from_argv(argv_words: ["user", "list", "1"]) {
      // the arm, not its wording: detail is NonEmptyStr, so a length test could never fail. What this
      // conjunct rules out is the other two arms -- a build-time default being applied silently would land
      // on IpmiCommandParsed, and the case above shows the same words reaching no channel at all.
      IpmiCommandTransportAbsent { detail: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
    // THE WIRE CASES ABOVE MUST ALSO HOLD THROUGH THE WALK, not only through the direct wire parser. The
    // two rows below were caught by the falsification sweep: mutating last-dash-I-wins, or the walk's
    // "no plugin claims this name" flag, left the claim green, because every transport decision this claim
    // made went through ipmi_command_interface_from_wire and none went through in_band_command_from_argv.
    // A transport rule proven only on the direct parser is a rule the walk can break silently -- the walk is
    // what the gates actually call.
    // LAST -I WINS: ipmitool reassigns intfname on every -I (lib/ipmi_main.c case 'I'), so the last one is
    // the transport. An early in-band name must not launder a later out-of-band one: these words name open
    // then lanplus, and the result is the OUT-OF-BAND arm naming lanplus. Had the first -I won, this would be
    // the in-band arm and go red -- which is exactly the mutation that previously slipped past.
    && (match in_band_command_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "open", "-I", "lanplus", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      IpmiCommandNotInBand { wire: w, why_not_in_band: _ } => w == "lanplus"
      _ => false
    })
    // AN -I NAME NO PLUGIN CLAIMS, REFUSED BY THE WALK ITSELF (not only by the direct parser): ipmi_intf_load
    // returns NULL for a name with no matching entry, so the invocation aborts before any channel is read.
    // The walk reaches this through its TransportNameUnrecognised arm; a walk that stopped setting it would fall
    // through to the no--I arm and this conjunct would go red rather than agree.
    && (match in_band_command_from_argv(argv_words: ["-I", "nosuchintf", "user", "list", "1"]) {
      IpmiCommandUnrecognised { wire: w } => w == "nosuchintf"
      _ => false
    })
}

// THE WALK AND ITS CLAIMS READ ONE TABLE. The option rows are data (ipmi_option_word_effect), and the
// refusals the claims above assert come from that same table -- so a claim cannot stay green while the walk
// quietly starts accepting a letter. This asserts the table itself, in the two directions that matter: what
// is modelled stays modelled, and what is refused by name is refused.
test fn the_option_table_models_exactly_the_letters_the_route_actually_acts_on() -> Bool {
  // modelled, with the arity ipmitool's OPTION_STRING gives them (lib/ipmi_main.c:82)
  ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-I") == EffectPendingTransport
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-U") == EffectPendingCredential
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-P") == EffectPendingCredential
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-f") == EffectPendingCredential
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-E") == EffectReadsEnv
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-H") == EffectPendingHost
    // these three take an argument that tunes the exchange and changes nothing about its kind, so they
    // must stay distinct from the credential letters: read as a credential, a retry count would be
    // recorded as a secret and the write would stop counting as passwordless in-band
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-N") == EffectPendingTuning
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-S") == EffectPendingTuning
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-D") == EffectPendingTuning
    // refused: each of these changes how a write is authenticated or where it is delivered, and upstream's
    // getopt aborts on any word this table does not name
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-k") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-y") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-a") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-L") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-C") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-t") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "--password") == EffectUnmodelled
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-P0penBmc") == EffectUnmodelled
    // and every arm is named by a line above, so an arm the walk stops producing turns this claim red.
    // The catch-all is asserted too: an unknown word gets the refusal arm, never a pass through a default
    && ipmi_option_word_effect(word: "-Z") == EffectSkip
}
test fn a_reset_arm_that_keeps_the_user_table_is_never_a_recovery() -> Bool {
  (match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: ResetAll) {
    ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: k } => k == ResetAll
    LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: _, what_survives: _ } => false
  })
    // AND the two arms that are not ResetAll refuse DIFFERENTLY FROM EACH OTHER, which a Bool could not
    // say and which is the whole reason an operator choosing "the gentler reset" is told anything: exactly
    // one of them hands back the credential we cannot read, and it is the arm whose published description
    // excepts local usernames and passwords. Asserted as a difference between the two refusals rather than
    // as two non-empty strings, because two arms sharing one sentence would still pass "non-empty" while
    // telling an operator that PreserveNetwork is the dangerous one -- the opposite of the schema's words.
    && (match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: PreserveNetworkAndUsers) {
        LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: k, what_survives: w } =>
          k == PreserveNetworkAndUsers
            // the surviving thing this arm names is the credential, in the schema's own term
            && string_contains(s: w, pattern: "passwords")
        ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
      })
    && (match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: PreserveNetwork) {
        LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: k, what_survives: w } =>
          k == PreserveNetwork
            // and this one does NOT claim to keep the credential, because its published wording excepts
            // only network settings
            && !string_contains(s: w, pattern: "usernames and passwords")
            && string_contains(s: w, pattern: "network")
        ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
      })
    // THE TWO REFUSALS ARE NOT ONE SENTENCE SHARED. Two arms whose text was identical would pass each
    // check above and still send an operator for a milder default into the arm that keeps the password.
    && (match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: PreserveNetworkAndUsers) {
         LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: _, what_survives: wa } =>
           match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: PreserveNetwork) {
             LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: _, what_survives: wb } => wa != wb
             ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
           }
         ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
       })
    // AND the kind carries the arm rather than answering a question about it: a PreserveNetworkAndUsers
    // inside the Redfish arm is matched as the arm it is, so nothing can read that actuation as one that
    // wipes.
    && (match RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: PreserveNetworkAndUsers } {
         RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: rt } =>
           match reset_to_defaults_user_table_disposition(kind: rt) {
             LeavesUserTableIntact { kind: _, what_survives: _ } => true
             ClearsUserTableIncludingUnknownCredential { kind: _ } => false
           }
         InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword => false
       })
    // THE ADMITTED RESET ARM IS MATCHED ONCE, and everything it must carry is read out of that match: the
    // arm it cleared, the gate that issued it, and the ruling it stands under. There is no Bool asked first
    // and no `_ => false` standing in for "impossible" -- with one type per write, the only arms that exist
    // here are this write's admitted and refused, so a match with two arms IS total. A ResetAll carried as
    // non-destructive, an admission without a ruling, and a password admission wearing the reset's arm all
    // have no constructor.
    && (match recovery_admitted_reset_write() {
          ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: t } =>
            t.reset_type == ResetAll
              && declaration_ref_eq(a: t.authorized_by, b: recovery_manager_reset_site())
              && t.operator_ruling.ruling_text == recovery_reset_operator_ruling.ruling_text
          ResetWriteRefused { cause: _ } => false
          ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
        })
    // ...and the password write's admission is its OWN type: it cannot even be asked whether it stands
    // under the reset's gate, so no refusal has to be written for that question.
    && (match recovery_admitted_in_band_write() {
          PasswordWriteAdmitted { ticket: t } =>
            t.operation == recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
              && declaration_ref_eq(a: t.authorized_by, b: recovery_in_band_password_write_site())
          PasswordWriteRefused { cause: _ } => false
          PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
        })
}

test fn a_readback_that_names_a_different_message_does_not_prove_the_reset() -> Bool {
  // THE MINT REFUSES, SO THERE IS NOTHING TO HAND THE HAND-OFF ARM. Each arm is matched, and the refusal
  // side asserts the absence of a VALUE rather than a false from a predicate: "no ProvenResetReadback was
  // produced" is what makes a hand-off unwritable, which is a stronger claim than the Bool ever made.
  // The near-miss message IDs are what a fabricated readback would most plausibly carry -- the registry
  // strings come from the DMTF registry the readback was read out of, not from this file.
  (match recovery_readback_proves_reset(
    readback: FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
      message_id: "Base.1.16.0.NoResource",
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
    },
  ) {
    Absent => true
    Present { value: _ } => false
  })
    // THE PROOF, when it comes: the carrier carries the identity, the evidence row and the instant it was
    // minted from, so a reader of the standing can get from the claim to the bytes.
    && (match recovery_readback_proves_reset(
      readback: FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange {
        message_id: "Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired",
        evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
        observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
      },
    ) {
      Present { value: p } =>
        redfish_message_id_is_password_change_required(id: p.message_id as String)
          // canonical equality on the record, not a hand-written field-by-field comparison: the carrier
          // must cite the SAME capture row (label + digest), not a look-alike
          && p.evidence == recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref
      Absent => false
    })
    // a rejected credential is not a proof either, whatever else its body said
    && (match recovery_readback_proves_reset(
      readback: CredentialNotAccepted {
        evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
        observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
      },
    ) {
      Absent => true
      Present { value: _ } => false
    })
    // and neither is a body that was never readable -- INCONCLUSIVE FROM THE BODY, not from an arm someone
    // preconstructed: the verdict comes out of the fold, so a fold that answered "required" to garbage has
    // to be caught here. Feeding the carrier directly, as the arm above does, proves only what the hand-off
    // refuses to accept; it says nothing about what the parse decides.
    && (match recovery_readback_of(
      body: "{\"error\":{\"@Message.ExtendedInfo\":[{\"MessageId\":\"Base.1.16.0.Passw",
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
    ) {
      ReadbackInconclusive { cause: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: _ } } => true
      ReadbackInconclusive { cause: ReadbackNamedNoPasswordChangeMessage } => false
      FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange { message_id: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
      // no mint: this fold reads a body, it does not report a rejected credential
      CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
    })
    // and the hand-off refuses such a carrier too, whether the parse produced it or a caller did
    && (match recovery_readback_proves_reset(
      readback: ReadbackInconclusive {
        cause: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: "response body was truncated before the message" as NonEmptyStr },
        evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
        observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
      },
    ) {
      Absent => true
      Present { value: _ } => false
    })
    && recovery_readback_of(
      body: "{\"error\":{\"@Message.ExtendedInfo\":[{\"MessageId\":\"Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired\"}]}}",
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
    ) == recovery_factory_credential_readback()
}

// THE CATALOG ANSWERS FOR THE CONTROLLER UNDER FOOT, AND FOR NOTHING ELSE. This is the law that
// replaces "give the route a policy and see what it does": the policy is now a lookup keyed on the
// Get Device ID answer the route observed, so the claims are about the lookup.
//   - the reading off the recovered board lands on the measured-refusal arm;
//   - the same board's numbers with a patch nobody read (3.22.1, or any other major.minor) lands on
//     CONTROLLER NEVER MEASURED -- it is not silently promoted into the 3.22.00 row, because IPMI's
//     two-number revision cannot name a patch and the row's own release key is checked against it;
//   - another vendor's numbers land on the same refusal, so a policy that licenses wiping a chassis is
//     unreachable for hardware nobody measured -- the hole this closes;
//   - the catalog's own indexes agree on the row it returns (nothing came back as CATALOG INDEXES
//     DISAGREE for the reading that does match), which is the check that a row filed against a release
//     outside the capability catalog would catch.
test fn the_write_policy_is_a_function_of_the_controller_and_refuses_every_unmeasured_one() -> Bool {
  let measured = asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_sighted_on(observed: recovery_measured_identity())
  let unmeasured_release = asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_sighted_on(observed: recovery_unmeasured_identity())
  let other_vendor = asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_sighted_on(observed: recovery_foreign_identity())
  (match measured {
    RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => true
    ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: _ } => false
    CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => false
    CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => false
    // A row that refused its own filing is a catalog DEFECT, so it is not the controller's fault and
    // it is not a pass either: the sighting under test here is about the other four arms.
    RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => false
  })
    && (match unmeasured_release {
      ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: o } => o.firmware_revision_major == 3 && o.firmware_revision_minor == 6
      RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => false
      CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => false
      CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => false
      RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => false
    })
    && (match other_vendor {
      ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: o } => o.manufacturer_id == 41832 && o.product_id == 8245
      RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => false
      CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => false
      CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => false
      RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE ROUTE CANNOT BE TOLD WHICH FIRMWARE IT IS TALKING TO. The reset gate's warrant comes from the
// reading the route carries, so a route standing in front of an unmeasured controller refuses with the
// reading in hand, and hands off for the measured one -- the two differ ONLY in the identity the route
// observed, which is what makes this a claim about the join rather than about a parameter.
test fn a_route_on_a_controller_nobody_measured_refuses_the_destructive_reset() -> Bool {
  let standing = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_unmeasured_identity()),
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryControllerPolicyUnavailable { sighting: sighting } =>
      match sighting {
        RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => false
        ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: o } =>
          o.firmware_revision_major == 3 && o.firmware_revision_minor == 6
            && match standing.outcome {
              RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => false
              _ => true
            }
            // matched, not compared: an optional compared against a Present literal answers by
            // representation (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.optional_equality_answers_by_representation)
            && match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: standing) {
              Present { value: RecoveryControllerNeverMeasured { observed: un } } => un == o
              _ => false
            }
        RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: _, observed: _ } => false
        CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => false
        CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => false
      }
    _ => false
  }
}

// THE WARRANT GATE STANDS ON ITS OWN, NOT BEHIND THE POLICY FOLD. converge_in_band_recovery refuses an
// unmeasured controller at the policy fold, so the route never reaches the reset gate on that reading --
// which means a claim about the fold says NOTHING about the gate's warrant arms, and the gate is also
// callable on its own by anything that admits a write directly. Both are tested here from the same call,
// differing ONLY in the reading the route observed: the unmeasured one is refused by the WARRANT (its own
// words), the measured one is refused further down by the HOLD it never took. If the warrant arms stop
// refusing, the second case's cause does not move while the first's does -- which is what a falsification
// of those arms has to be caught by.
test fn the_reset_gate_refuses_an_unmeasured_controller_by_its_warrant_and_not_by_the_hold() -> Bool {
  (match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
    route: recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_unmeasured_identity()),
    under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
    unit_hold: optional_absent(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
  ) {
    ResetWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
      starts_with(s: cause as String, prefix: "no refusal was ever measured on this controller")
        // and NOT the hold sentence: the warrant refused before the hold was ever asked
        && starts_with(s: cause as String, prefix: "the durable unit hold was never acquired") == false
    ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
    ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
  })
    // THE SAME GATE, THE SAME MISSING HOLD, THE MEASURED READING: the warrant no longer refuses, so the
    // refusal that comes back is the hold's. Asserting the hold sentence here is what makes the pair a
    // claim about the warrant rather than about the gate refusing everything.
    && (match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
      route: recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity()),
      under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
      unit_hold: optional_absent(),
      reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    ) {
      ResetWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
        starts_with(s: cause as String, prefix: "the durable unit hold was never acquired")
      ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
      ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
    })
}

// A CATALOG THAT DISAGREES WITH ITSELF ADMITS NOTHING. The row found by reading is re-checked through
// the release-keyed index; if its release key does not resolve in the board's capability catalog, the
// destructive step would rest on a release nobody can name. Exercised by a reading whose row is the one
// this module files -- the refusal arm below is what the same lookup returns when the row is intact, so
// the two arms are checked against each other rather than asserted separately.
test fn the_sighting_carries_both_readings_so_a_refusal_is_locatable() -> Bool {
  match asrock_altrad8ud_published_factory_password_write_policy_sighted_on(observed: recovery_measured_identity()) {
    RefusalMeasuredOnThisController { refusal: refusal, observed: observed } =>
      // the reading that unlocked the row is the reading handed over, not a re-authored copy of it
      observed == recovery_measured_identity()
        && match recovery_policy_firmware(policy: refusal) {
          Present { value: f } => f.version.major == 3 && f.version.minor == 22 && f.version.patch == 0
          Absent => false
        }
    ControllerNeverMeasured { observed: _ } => false
    CatalogAmbiguousForController { observed: _, matched: _ } => false
    CatalogIndexesDisagree { firmware: _, observed: _ } => false
    // The disagreement arm is exercised by its own claim, not this one; here the sighting under test is
    // the refusal, and any other arm makes the two readings unlocatable.
    RowDisagreesWithItsOwnReading { row: _, observed: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE READBACK IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES A RESET A FACT RATHER THAN AN ASSERTION. Both sides are
// checked, because "refuses without it" is only meaningful if the same run converges WITH it: a route
// that refused in both cases would look identical here and be useless in the field. The cause stays
// locatable -- the readback that named no PasswordChangeRequired message is a different repair from a
// controller nobody measured.
test fn the_standing_refuses_to_claim_proof_without_the_readback() -> Bool {
  let route = recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity())
  let without_readback = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: ReadbackInconclusive {
      cause: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: "no body was saved" as NonEmptyStr },
      evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
    },
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  let with_readback = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  // THE SAME PAIR, MATCHED. And the reason the refusal side is not vacuous is the sealed carrier: there
  // is no ProvenResetReadback value to put in the hand-off arm for a ReadbackInconclusive, so the fold
  // cannot reach that arm at all -- which is a stronger claim than the Bool this replaces ever made.
  (match without_readback.outcome {
    RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => false
    RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: _ } => true
    _ => false
  })
    && (match with_readback.outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: b, proven: p, writes: w } =>
        b.in_band_device_identity == route.in_band_device_identity && list_length(items: w) == 2
          // the carrier carries the identity it was minted from, and that identity is still the one
          // extdeps.bmc.redfish recognises -- checked by ITS predicate, not against a literal here
          && redfish_message_id_is_password_change_required(id: p.message_id as String)
      _ => false
    })
    && match without_readback.outcome {
      RecoveryReadbackRefusedFactoryCredential { readback: _ } => true
      _ => false
    }
    // AND THE OUTGOING REFUSAL IS THE REFUSAL, NOT A GENERIC ONE. The readback that arrived travels with
    // it, so a reader can check the answer against the capture: this is not the arm for a readback nobody
    // could read, and it must not arrive wearing that arm's clothes. Matched rather than compared against a
    // Present literal, for the reason recorded in
    // gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.optional_equality_answers_by_representation
    && (match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: without_readback) {
         Present { value: RecoveryReadbackDoesNotConfirmReset { readback: rb } } =>
           // the answer that arrived is the one the fold refused on: it proves nothing, by the same
           // predicate the fold asked, and it is the no-body answer this run supplied
           (match recovery_readback_proves_reset(readback: rb) {
             Absent => true
             Present { value: _ } => false
           })
             && (match rb {
               // THE ARM IS THE ASSERTION. The earlier form matched the arm and then grepped its prose
               // for "no body was saved", so rewording the sentence voided the control and any other
               // inconclusive answer with the right words passed.
               ReadbackInconclusive { cause: ReadbackBodyCouldNotBeRead { why: _ }, evidence: e, observed_at: _ } =>
                 e == recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref
               ReadbackInconclusive { cause: ReadbackNamedNoPasswordChangeMessage, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } =>
                 false
               FactoryCredentialActiveAwaitingChange { message_id: _, evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
               CredentialNotAccepted { evidence: _, observed_at: _ } => false
             })
         Present { value: _ } => false
         none => false
       })
}

// AN UNATTEMPTED WRITE IS NOT A RECOVERY. The record is evidence of an ATTEMPT, never of success; here
// there is not even an attempt, so nothing about the chassis is known and the handoff is refused.
test fn an_unattempted_reset_write_leaves_the_route_unverified_rather_than_recovered() -> Bool {
  let standing = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity()),
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: NotExecuted { kind: recovery_reset_actuation() },
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  match standing.outcome {
    RecoveryWriteUnverified { kind: RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetAll } } =>
      // THE OUTGOING REFUSAL NAMES THE WRITE THAT IS MISSING. The projection used to hand this case to the
      // generic "readback inconclusive" arm and drop the kind on the floor (kind: _), so the reader was told
      // to re-read a controller that had never been written to, and never told which of the two writes was
      // outstanding. Now the kind survives the whole way out.
      (match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: standing) {
         Present { value: RecoveryWriteNeverAttempted { kind: k } } =>
           k == RedfishManagerResetToDefaults { reset_type: ResetAll }
         Present { value: _ } => false
         none => false
       })
      && (match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: standing) {
           Present { value: RecoveryReadbackInconclusive { gap: _ } } => false
           Present { value: _ } => true
           none => false
         })
      && (match standing.outcome {
           RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => false
           _ => true
         })
    _ => false
  }
}

// ── FIXTURES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The capture's own numbers, through the mints only.

fn recovery_rescue_host_address() -> Ipv4Address {
  Ipv4Address { octet1: 192, octet2: 168, octet3: 1, octet4: 251 }
}

fn recovery_controller_address() -> Ipv4Address {
  Ipv4Address { octet1: 192, octet2: 168, octet3: 1, octet4: 250 }
}

// THE READING THAT OPENS THE MEASURED ROW: the board's own filed Get Device ID answer, imported from
// the vendor module rather than re-typed here. Re-typing it would put a second copy of the numbers
// beside the one the policy is filed against, and the two could drift apart -- which is exactly how a
// destructive step gets justified by an observation nobody read.
fn recovery_measured_identity() -> IpmiDeviceIdentity {
  asrock_altrad8ud_1l2t_inband_device_identity
}

// THE SAME BOARD ON A RELEASE NOBODY READ: same Manufacturer ID and Product ID, different Firmware
// Revision. The lookup must not be able to answer "measured" for this, because its row's release key
// (3.22.00) cannot be reached by a controller reporting 3.6.
fn recovery_unmeasured_identity() -> IpmiDeviceIdentity {
  IpmiDeviceIdentity {
    device_id: 32,
    device_revision: 1,
    firmware_revision_major: 3,
    firmware_revision_minor: 6,
    manufacturer_id: 49622,
    manufacturer_name: "ASRock Rack Incorporation",
    product_id: 8245,
    product_name: "Unknown (0x2035)",
  }
}

// ANOTHER VENDOR'S CONTROLLER REPORTING THIS BOARD'S PRODUCT ID: the number alone must not open the
// row, which is why Manufacturer ID is part of the identity and not decoration. The value is a
// placeholder reading for the law, not a claim about a real board.
fn recovery_foreign_identity() -> IpmiDeviceIdentity {
  IpmiDeviceIdentity {
    device_id: 32,
    device_revision: 1,
    firmware_revision_major: 3,
    firmware_revision_minor: 22,
    manufacturer_id: 41832,
    manufacturer_name: "Placeholder Other Vendor",
    product_id: 8245,
    product_name: "Unknown (0x2035)",
  }
}

fn recovery_test_release() -> BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity {
  BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity { family: OpenBmc, raw_version: "3.22", semantic_version: none }
}

fn recovery_usb_medium() -> RescueHostMedium {
  UsbRescueMediumObserved {
    medium: ExecutedRescueMedium {
      evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
      observed_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
    },
  }
}

fn recovery_complete_ipmi_presence() -> InBandIpmiPresence {
  InBandIpmiPresence {
    device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
    kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
  }
}

// THE ACCOUNT LINE OF 02_inband_channel.txt, AS BYTES SAY IT: `user list 1` printed id 1 / root /
// ADMINISTRATOR on channel 1. Built through extdeps' own mint so the triple is assembled the way the IPMI
// model assembles it everywhere else, and the privilege is NAMED (IpmiPrivilegeAdministrator), not
// asserted by a boolean beside it.
fn recovery_administrator_account() -> InBandAdministratorAccount {
  InBandAdministratorAccount {
    access: ipmi_user_channel_access(channel: 1, user: 1, privilege: IpmiPrivilegeAdministrator),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
  }
}

fn recovery_test_subject() -> MachineIntakeSubject {
  MachineIntakeSubject {
    subject: QualificationSubject {
      unit_key: "bmc-recovery-fixture-unit",
      assembly_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "assembly-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
      firmware_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "firmware-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
    },
    attempt_id: "in-band-recovery-attempt-1",
  }
}

// THE CONTROLLER READING AS A LITERAL WITH ITS JOIN-RELEVANT PARTS AS PARAMETERS. The two fields the
// bind asks of this carrier -- the endpoint the Redfish answer was fetched from, and the firmware release
// it carries -- are the parameters, because the only honest way to test a refusal is to build the route
// that causes it, and a builder without them would make every refusal claim rebuild this fifteen-field
// literal and drift from it. recovery_test_controller_observation() below is the good path: the host is
// the one the readback bytes show was requested, and the release is the one the measured Get Device ID
// publishes.
fn recovery_test_controller_with(
  endpoint_host: NonEmptyStr,
  firmware_release: BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity,
) -> BmcAccessObservation {
  BmcAccessObservation {
    // THE ADDRESS THAT WAS KNOCKED, as a parameter -- 04_post_reset_readback.txt's request line is
    // "### request: GET https://192.168.1.250/redfish/v1/Managers/bmc", and the good path below passes
    // exactly that host; a fixture endpoint disagreeing with its own captured request would make every
    // fold claim silently exercise the disagreement arm.
    endpoint: BmcControllerEndpoint { host: endpoint_host },
    identity: BmcObservedIdentity {
      board: BoardFruNotRead { cause: BoardAreaFieldsUnreadable },
      chassis_part_number: none,
      firmware: firmware_release,
    },
    discovered_surfaces: [],
    boot_control_routes: [],
    redfish_virtual_media_probe: none,
    successful_read_surfaces: [],
    successful_write_surfaces: [],
    raw_response: content_hash_of_value(value: "in-band-recovery-fixture-raw-response"),
    observed_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
  }
}

fn recovery_test_controller_observation() -> BmcAccessObservation {
  recovery_test_controller_with(endpoint_host: "192.168.1.250", firmware_release: recovery_test_release())
}

// THE CHANNEL OBSERVATION AS A LITERAL WITH ITS JOIN-RELEVANT PARTS AS PARAMETERS, same reason: the
// bind compares the SUBJECT and the BMC's SELF-REPORTED ADDRESS, so the refusal claims must be able to
// set exactly those, and the good path passes the fixture's own subject and the address the capture
// records (02_inband_channel.txt's lan print: 192.168.1.250).
fn recovery_test_channel_with(
  subject: MachineIntakeSubject,
  reported: Optional<Ipv4Address>,
) -> InBandChannelObservation {
  InBandChannelObservation {
    subject: subject,
    rescue_host_address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
    device_node: recovery_ipmi_device_node,
    kernel_modules: recovery_ipmi_kernel_modules,
    channel: InBandChannelPasswordless { transport: OpenIpmiDriver },
    administrator: recovery_administrator_account(),
    evidence: recovery_inband_channel_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
    device_node_created_at: recovery_device_node_creation_instant(),
    bmc_self_reported_address: reported,
  }
}

fn recovery_test_channel() -> InBandChannelObservation {
  recovery_test_channel_with(
    subject: recovery_test_subject(),
    reported: Present { value: recovery_controller_address() },
  )
}

fn recovery_route_with(
  channel: InBandChannelObservation,
  controller: BmcAccessObservation,
  reading: IpmiDeviceIdentity,
) -> RecoveryInBandRoute {
  RecoveryInBandRoute {
    subject: recovery_test_subject(),
    rescue_host: rescue_host_reachable(
      address: recovery_rescue_host_address(),
      ssh_port: openssh_standard_port,
      ipmi: recovery_complete_ipmi_presence(),
      medium: recovery_usb_medium(),
    ),
    in_band: channel,
    controller: controller,
    in_band_device_identity: reading,
  }
}

fn recovery_test_route(reading: IpmiDeviceIdentity) -> RecoveryInBandRoute {
  recovery_route_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel(),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_observation(),
    reading: reading,
  )
}

// WHAT converge IS HANDED: THE DECISIONS THE RUN OBTAINED FROM THE TWO GATES. They are parameters rather
// than results converge computes because the reset gate's admitting arm is NOT constructible at model time:
// it requires a UnitHoldAcquisition whose proof the hold module mints only from its own live acquires
// (UnitHoldProof is sole_constructor there, unit_hold_minted is admit_callers-gated). So no caller -- not
// even this module's own laws -- can manufacture a reset that the ledger admits with the unit held. That is
// the gate working, and the laws below assert exactly that at the refusals.
// ADMITTED ONLY TO THE FOUR NAMED CLAIMS BELOW, which is the whole set of callers this fixture has
// (git grep; nothing outside this module calls it). A fixture that produces a privileged admission is
// itself an authorization-bearing payload: left open, it is a second, unpoliced mint beside the gate,
// and DESIGN 3c's "a witness asserting existence rather than exercising behavior" is the failure it
// invites. Confining it to named claims means a new consumer has to be declared, and a claim that
// quietly stops exercising the route loses its fixture (side-chat HOLD blocker 1).
// THE TWO MISMATCH FIXTURES, which exist so the fold's binding checks can be REACHED. Minted through the
// rostered mint -- an unadmitted caller is a compile refusal, which is the point of the roster -- and
// admitted to it by name above. A check that can never fire is a comment in code's clothing, so each is
// paired with the claim below that asks the fold about it.
fn recovery_password_admission_for_another_unit() -> PasswordWriteAuthorization {
  PasswordWriteAdmitted {
    ticket: recovery_mint_password_admission_ticket(
      subject: MachineIntakeSubject {
        // a DIFFERENT chassis: same attempt id, so the only thing that differs is which unit the gate
        // was reading when it decided
        subject: QualificationSubject {
          unit_key: "bmc-recovery-another-unit",
          assembly_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "assembly-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
          firmware_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "firmware-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
        },
        attempt_id: "in-band-recovery-attempt-1",
      },
      operation: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
      authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(),
    ),
  }
}

// Same chassis, WRONG OPERATION: an admission the password gate really issued, being presented where a
// reset admission belongs. The types already stop most of this -- the fold takes two differently-typed
// admissions -- so what is exercised here is the operation the ticket itself carries.
fn recovery_password_admission_for_another_operation() -> PasswordWriteAuthorization {
  PasswordWriteAdmitted {
    ticket: recovery_mint_password_admission_ticket(
      subject: recovery_test_subject(),
      operation: recovery_reset_actuation(),
      authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(),
    ),
  }
}

// ---- THE BIND BINDS: four crossed routes, each refused by its own typed arm, each with the unmodified
// route as a control half so no claim can be satisfied by a fold that refuses everything. Before these
// claims existed, the subject and the endpoint-vs-self-report of the carrier called "bound" were checked
// by nobody: both were copied out of the route beside the bind, so a channel observation of a DIFFERENT
// chassis, or a Redfish answer knocked at a different address than the one the BMC named for itself,
// produced the same standing as the matched route.

fn recovery_subject_of_another_chassis() -> MachineIntakeSubject {
  MachineIntakeSubject {
    // the same attempt, a different unit -- the only thing that differs is which chassis the reading is
    // about, the exact axis the bound carrier used to be blind to
    subject: QualificationSubject {
      unit_key: "bmc-recovery-another-unit",
      assembly_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "assembly-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
      firmware_manifest_digest: content_hash_of_value(value: "firmware-digest-for-in-band-recovery-fixtures"),
    },
    attempt_id: "in-band-recovery-attempt-1",
  }
}

fn recovery_fold_with(channel: InBandChannelObservation, controller: BmcAccessObservation) -> InBandRecoveryStanding {
  converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: recovery_route_with(
      channel: channel,
      controller: controller,
      reading: recovery_measured_identity(),
    ),
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
}

fn recovery_control_standing() -> InBandRecoveryStanding {
  recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel(),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_observation(),
  )
}

// THE UNIT IDENTITY IS PART OF THE BIND. The channel observation names a different chassis than the
// route drives: the fold must refuse the bind, and the refusal must SAY so -- naming both subjects, so
// the reader learns which reading is about which machine. The control half is the unmodified route
// reaching the proof arm, which is what makes the refusal side non-vacuous.
test fn the_bind_refuses_a_channel_observation_of_another_unit() -> Bool {
  let crossed = recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel_with(
      subject: recovery_subject_of_another_chassis(),
      reported: Present { value: recovery_controller_address() },
    ),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_observation(),
  )
  (match crossed.outcome {
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: _,
      cause: Present {
        value: RecoveryBindSubjectsDisagree { route_subject: rs, channel_subject: cs },
      },
    } => rs == recovery_test_subject() && cs == recovery_subject_of_another_chassis()
    _ => false
  })
    && (match recovery_control_standing().outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

// THE ENDPOINT MUST BE THE ADDRESS THE BMC NAMED FOR ITSELF. The BMC, asked over SSIF (a transport that
// cannot be pointed at another machine), said 192.168.1.250; the Redfish answer was knocked at .249.
// That is two machines in the room, and firmware agreement between them is what makes the mistake fatal:
// two controllers of the same OpenBMC build answer identically while being different boards.
test fn the_bind_refuses_an_endpoint_the_bmc_never_reported() -> Bool {
  let crossed = recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel(),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_with(endpoint_host: "192.168.1.249", firmware_release: recovery_test_release()),
  )
  (match crossed.outcome {
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: _,
      cause: Present {
        value: RecoveryBindEndpointDisagreesWithChannelReport { endpoint_host: h, reported_host: r },
      },
    } => h == "192.168.1.249" && r == "192.168.1.250"
    _ => false
  })
    && (match recovery_control_standing().outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

// A PROSE ENDPOINT IS REFUSED AT PARSE, WITH THE PARSER'S OWN LOCATED ERROR. The old fixture endpoint
// ("bmc-recovery-fixture-controller", no dots) now falls to the parser rather than being waved through:
// the refusal carries the ipv4 module's error -- which position, which value -- so the repair points at
// a field of a different module instead of blaming the comparison.
test fn the_bind_refuses_an_endpoint_that_is_not_an_address() -> Bool {
  let crossed = recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel(),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_with(
      endpoint_host: "bmc-recovery-fixture-controller",
      firmware_release: recovery_test_release(),
    ),
  )
  (match crossed.outcome {
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: _,
      cause: Present {
        value: RecoveryBindEndpointNotAnAddress { endpoint_host: h, error: e },
      },
    } => h == "bmc-recovery-fixture-controller"
      && (match e {
        // one field, not four dotted numbers -- the parser located exactly that
        Ipv4WrongOctetCount { observed_count: 1 } => true
        _ => false
      })
    _ => false
  })
    && (match recovery_control_standing().outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

// NO SELF-REPORT, NO BIND. A capture that never recorded the BMC's own address cannot vouch for which
// machine the endpoint belongs to, and filling the gap with the endpoint's own address -- the obvious
// shortcut -- would make the comparison a tautology and the check silently always pass. The absence is
// its OWN arm, with a re-capture as the repair.
test fn the_bind_refuses_a_channel_that_reported_no_address() -> Bool {
  let crossed = recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel_with(subject: recovery_test_subject(), reported: Absent),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_observation(),
  )
  (match crossed.outcome {
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: _,
      cause: Present { value: RecoveryBindChannelReportedNoAddress { endpoint_host: h } },
    } => h == "192.168.1.250"
    _ => false
  })
    && (match recovery_control_standing().outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

// THE FIRMWARE JOIN STILL BITES AFTER THE ADDRESS JOIN: the same route, endpoint and self-report
// agreeing, with only the Redfish release swapped to one the measured Get Device ID contradicts. If
// the address match made the firmware check redundant, this claim would stay green without it -- it
// does not, and the refusal must carry BOTH releases so the reader sees which answer disagrees.
test fn the_bind_refuses_release_answers_that_disagree() -> Bool {
  let crossed = recovery_fold_with(
    channel: recovery_test_channel(),
    controller: recovery_test_controller_with(
      endpoint_host: "192.168.1.250",
      firmware_release: BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity { family: OpenBmc, raw_version: "9.99", semantic_version: none },
    ),
  )
  (match crossed.outcome {
    RecoveryReadbackNotBoundToThisController {
      readback: _,
      cause: Present {
        value: RecoveryBindReadingsDisagreeOnRelease { redfish_release: rf, ipmi_release: ip },
      },
    } => rf == "9.99" && ip == "3.22"
    _ => false
  })
    && (match recovery_control_standing().outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

fn recovery_admitted_in_band_write() -> PasswordWriteAuthorization
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "a_reset_arm_that_keeps_the_user_table_is_never_a_recovery"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "a_route_on_a_controller_nobody_measured_refuses_the_destructive_reset"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "the_standing_refuses_to_claim_proof_without_the_readback"),
    // the bind claims all drive the fold through this one helper, which is the direct caller of
    // both mints; admitting the claims themselves would roster a caller that never calls
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_fold_with"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "an_unattempted_reset_write_leaves_the_route_unverified_rather_than_recovered"),
    // the ticket-binding claim needs an ADMITTED pair as its control: without it, the two refusals
    // above could be what every call returns
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "an_admission_bound_to_another_unit_or_another_operation_is_refused"),
    // three fixtures OUTSIDE this module stand on an admitted pair, and each is named here rather than
    // let through by a wildcard: two witness fixtures that build a standing to test a REFUSAL with (a
    // locked and a completed one), and the seal probe's sanctioned control. An unrostered caller of
    // either mint is a compile refusal, which is the property the seal witness measures.
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_route_witness", decl_name: "recovery_locked_standing_on_a_release_nobody_measured"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_route_witness", decl_name: "recovery_completed_standing"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_authorization_seal_witness", decl_name: "sanctioned_ticket_source"),
  ]
{
  PasswordWriteAdmitted {
    ticket: recovery_mint_password_admission_ticket(
      subject: recovery_test_subject(),
      operation: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
      authorized_by: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(),
    ),
  }
}

// Same confinement as the password fixture: the four claims that exercise the admitted arms, and no
// one else. This is the one whose mint the compiler already refuses elsewhere (UnitHoldProof is sealed
// at its source), so the fixture is the only path to the admitted reset arm -- which is exactly why it
// needs a roster.
fn recovery_admitted_reset_write() -> ResetWriteAuthorization
  admit_callers: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "a_reset_arm_that_keeps_the_user_table_is_never_a_recovery"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "a_route_on_a_controller_nobody_measured_refuses_the_destructive_reset"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "the_standing_refuses_to_claim_proof_without_the_readback"),
    // the bind claims all drive the fold through this one helper, which is the direct caller of
    // both mints; admitting the claims themselves would roster a caller that never calls
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "recovery_fold_with"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "an_unattempted_reset_write_leaves_the_route_unverified_rather_than_recovered"),
    // the ticket-binding claim needs an ADMITTED pair as its control: without it, the two refusals
    // above could be what every call returns
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route", decl_name: "an_admission_bound_to_another_unit_or_another_operation_is_refused"),
    // three fixtures OUTSIDE this module stand on an admitted pair, and each is named here rather than
    // let through by a wildcard: two witness fixtures that build a standing to test a REFUSAL with (a
    // locked and a completed one), and the seal probe's sanctioned control. An unrostered caller of
    // either mint is a compile refusal, which is the property the seal witness measures.
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_route_witness", decl_name: "recovery_locked_standing_on_a_release_nobody_measured"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_route_witness", decl_name: "recovery_completed_standing"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.machine_intake.machine_intake_bmc_recovery_authorization_seal_witness", decl_name: "sanctioned_ticket_source"),
  ]
{
  ResetWriteAdmitted {
    ticket: recovery_mint_reset_admission_ticket(
      subject: recovery_test_subject(),
      operation: recovery_reset_actuation(),
      reset_type: ResetAll,
      authorized_by: recovery_manager_reset_site(),
      operator_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
    ),
  }
}

fn recovery_in_band_write_record() -> RecoveryWriteRecord {
  ExecutedWithoutByteCapture {
    kind: InBandTemporaryAdministratorPassword,
    observed_exit: 0,
    detail: "operator-observed only; wiped by the reset before it could be re-read",
  }
}

fn recovery_reset_write_record() -> RecoveryWriteRecord {
  ExecutedWithoutByteCapture {
    kind: recovery_reset_actuation(),
    observed_exit: 0,
    detail: "observed in session; request and response bodies were not captured",
  }
}

// THE BODY THE CAPTURE HOLDS, AND NOTHING ELSE. The readback below is produced by handing these bytes to
// recovery_readback_of, so the MessageId in the proving arm is READ OUT OF THEM by extdeps.bmc.redfish --
// there is no fixture that types the id by hand, and therefore no fixture that can be green because the
// author knew what a 403 from this firmware looks like. The bytes are the 403 body recorded in
// 04_post_reset_readback.txt; the digest of that file is what the evidence ref below cites, and the same
// digest is the one the post-reset readback record in gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_inband_recovery_receipts is
// keyed by -- which is what lets the bind succeed for this specimen and refuse every other one.
data recovery_post_reset_readback_body: String = "{\"@Message.ExtendedInfo\":[{\"@odata.type\":\"#Message.v1_1_1.Message\",\"Message\":\"The password provided for this account must be changed before access is granted.  PATCH the Password property for this account located at the target URI '/redfish/v1/AccountService/Accounts/root' to complete this process.\",\"MessageArgs\":[\"/redfish/v1/AccountService/Accounts/root\"],\"MessageId\":\"Base.1.16.0.PasswordChangeRequired\",\"MessageSeverity\":\"Critical\",\"Resolution\":\"Change the password for this account using a PATCH to the Password property at the URI provided.\"}]}"

fn recovery_factory_credential_readback() -> RecoveryFactoryCredentialReadback {
  recovery_readback_of(
    body: recovery_post_reset_readback_body,
    evidence: recovery_post_reset_readback_capture_ref,
    observed_at: recovery_post_reset_instant(),
  )
}

// THE GATES ARE CALLED, NOT JUST NAMED. Until now nothing in this file CALLed either admission gate --
// the census references them only as decl_ref STRINGS, which cannot catch a wrong field or parameter
// name. That gap is how `AdmittedUnitHoldSubject { unit: }` (the record's field is `key`) and a fold
// writing `proven:` into an arm declaring `readback:` survived: the compiler never saw those call sites
// because there were none. Both gates are now called, on every arm they can be called on from here.
//
// WHICH HOLD ARMS ARE NOT EXERCISED HERE, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A HOLE. UnitHoldProof is sole_constructor
// in gunbc.host.managed_host_unit_hold, minted only by the live compare-and-set acquire, so this module
// cannot build one and no claim here asserts the admitted-hold arm: forging one would be the exact attack
// that module's own forged-probe witness checks is refused at the literal. What IS exercised is every arm
// reachable without a proof -- hold never taken, hold refused by the store -- plus the key decision that
// separates "this chassis" from "somebody else's chassis", which was pulled out of the inline comparison
// precisely so it could be tested (see recovery_hold_key_verdict).
test fn the_reset_gate_refuses_by_naming_the_hold_mistake() -> Bool {
  let route = recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity())
  // NO HOLD TAKEN: refused, and the cause says WHICH mistake, rather than the generic sentence a Bool
  // produced for all three.
  (match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
    route: route,
    under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
    unit_hold: optional_absent(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
  ) {
    ResetWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
      starts_with(s: cause as String, prefix: "the durable unit hold was never acquired")
    ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
    ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
  })
    // REFUSED BY THE STORE: the store's own words travel into the cause -- the half the Bool discarded.
    && (match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
      route: route,
      under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
      unit_hold: optional_present(
        value: UnitHoldRefused { reason: "unit is held by boot-run-41; no BMC actuation until it is released" },
      ),
      reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    ) {
      ResetWriteRefused { cause: cause } =>
        starts_with(s: cause as String, prefix: "the durable unit hold was refused by the store: ")
          && string_contains(s: cause as String, pattern: "boot-run-41")
      ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
      ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
    })
    // THE REFUSALS ARE NOT THE SAME STRING, which is what the Bool made all three of them.
    && (match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
      route: route,
      under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
      unit_hold: optional_present(value: UnitHoldRefused { reason: "store unavailable" }),
      reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    ) {
      ResetWriteRefused { cause: refused_by_store } =>
        match admit_recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults(
          route: route,
          under_ruling: recovery_reset_operator_ruling,
          unit_hold: optional_absent(),
          reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
        ) {
          ResetWriteRefused { cause: never_taken } => (refused_by_store as String) != (never_taken as String)
          ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
          ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
        }
      ResetWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
      ResetWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
    })
    // THE KEY DECISION, BOTH WAYS. Two units whose keys share a prefix are two chassis; the refusal keeps
    // BOTH names so an operator can see which two, instead of being told "mismatch".
    && (match recovery_hold_key_verdict(held_key: "altrad8ud-1l2t-6", this_unit: "altrad8ud-1l2t-6") {
      HoldKeyMatchesThisUnit => true
      HoldKeyNamesAnotherUnit { held_for: _, this_unit: _ } => false
    })
    && (match recovery_hold_key_verdict(held_key: "altrad8ud-1l2t-7", this_unit: "altrad8ud-1l2t-6") {
      HoldKeyMatchesThisUnit => false
      HoldKeyNamesAnotherUnit { held_for: held_for, this_unit: this_unit } =>
        held_for == "altrad8ud-1l2t-7" && this_unit == "altrad8ud-1l2t-6"
    })
}

// THE CHANNEL EACH WRITE TRAVELS, CHECKED AGAINST WHAT THE CAPTURE RECORDED. The two writes do not
// travel the same channel: the capture's own summary puts the temporary password on the in-band SSIF
// interface and the reset at the BMC's LAN address over Redfish, so the in-band interface is how the
// route READS and how the password is WRITTEN, and the management interface is where the wipe is issued.
// That distinction is not decoration -- the ruling's text is what a per-effect operator approval is filed
// against, so a ruling that scoped the reset to the in-band channel would have an operator approve a
// different actuation from the one being admitted (§4d: "do not assert as deduced what is only inferred";
// §3: a name may not silently change obligations). This asks the ruling and the effect row it governs to
// agree, rather than trusting either sentence in isolation: two declarations that drift apart is how a
// meaning fork starts.
test fn the_reset_ruling_names_the_channel_the_capture_recorded_and_both_rows_agree() -> Bool {
  let ruling = recovery_reset_operator_ruling
  let governed = recovery_manager_reset_to_defaults_effect().subject
  // the wipe goes out-of-band, in BOTH places that describe it
  string_contains(s: ruling.ruling_text as String, pattern: "out-of-band management interface")
    && string_contains(s: ruling.effect_subject as String, pattern: "out-of-band management interface")
    // the in-band interface is named for ONE write, and the ruling has to say which rather than leave it
    // to be inferred from the module's prose
    && string_contains(
      s: ruling.ruling_text as String,
      pattern: "in-band system interface is used only to write the temporary Administrator password",
    )
    // and neither description may call the reset itself an in-band actuation
    && string_contains(s: ruling.effect_subject as String, pattern: "in-band recovery reset") == false
    && string_contains(s: governed as String, pattern: "in-band") == false
    // the row the ruling governs still names the transport it actually uses
    && string_contains(s: governed as String, pattern: "over Redfish")
}

// THE LEDGER, CALLED THROUGH -- BOTH WAYS ROUND, INCLUDING THE ARMS THE FIXTURES NEVER PRODUCE. The
// fixtures in this module are the census's own rows, so the conforming path was the only one ever exercised
// and the ledger's refusals were decoration. A site the census has never filed is a real configuration (the
// row is a declaration in the tree; delete it and every write becomes unclassified), and the refusal has to
// arrive as THAT: naming the site, so "add the row" is actionable, and not as one of the four channel/record
// sentences that would send someone to check a cable.
test fn a_write_the_census_never_classified_is_refused_naming_the_site() -> Bool {
  let unfiled = decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
    decl_name: "a_declaration_the_census_has_never_filed",
  )
  match recovery_ledger_admits_password_write(site_ref: unfiled) {
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => false
    LedgerRefusal { cause: CensusRowMissing { site: site } } => {
      // the refusal names WHICH site it looked for, rather than "the ledger declined"
      let said = ledger_refusal_text(site_ref: unfiled, cause: CensusRowMissing { site: site })
      declaration_ref_eq(a: site, b: unfiled)
        && string_contains(s: said as String, pattern: "a_declaration_the_census_has_never_filed")
        // and it does NOT blame the channel, which is what a Bool collapsed this into
        && string_contains(s: said as String, pattern: "credential-free") == false
    }
    LedgerRefusal { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE TWO LEDGER QUESTIONS AT THE SITES THEY ARE ASKED AT. Both write gates consume one of these, so this
// also proves the gates' own admissions are emitted from a verdict the census really returned rather than
// from a value this module asserts about itself.
test fn both_recovery_writes_are_admitted_by_their_own_filed_rows() -> Bool {
  (match recovery_ledger_admits_password_write(site_ref: recovery_in_band_password_write_site()) {
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => true
    LedgerRefusal { cause: _ } => false
  })
    && (match recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: recovery_manager_reset_site()) {
      LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => true
      LedgerRefusal { cause: _ } => false
    })
    // THE TWO GATES ARE ASKED AT DIFFERENT SITES, which is what keeps the reset's gate from being the
    // password gate under another name: each is answered by its own filed row, and a single shared site
    // would mean one classification vouching for two writes of different kinds.
    && declaration_ref_eq(a: recovery_in_band_password_write_site(), b: recovery_manager_reset_site()) == false
  // WHAT IS *NOT* CLAIMED, so the absence is on the record rather than looking like an oversight. Both
  // rows select the operator-approved capability -- the census says so, and each row states the force that
  // brings it about (the reset by irreversibility, the password write by the credential it mints) -- so no
  // claim can be made here about the reset gate refusing a revocable grant: the fixture data does not
  // contain one, and asserting a refusal the data does not produce would be fabricating an expectation to
  // match a comment. The strength difference is instead pinned where it exists: the reset's walk names
  // OperatorApprovedCapability and the password's does not, and the mutation that makes the reset accept
  // ANY selected pattern turns a claim red.
}

// A CENSUS REFUSAL SURVIVES THE WHOLE ROUTE, AND IS NOT RE-RENDERED AS A CHANNEL PROBLEM ON THE WAY OUT.
// The fold hands the gate's ledger arm to the standing, the standing hands it to the unavailability sum, and
// the next action derives its sentence from the carrier. If any layer dropped it into the generic sentence,
// an operator whose census row was deleted would be told to go find a rescue USB.
test fn a_census_refusal_reaches_the_next_action_with_the_census_words_intact() -> Bool {
  let unfiled = decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route",
    decl_name: "a_declaration_the_census_has_never_filed",
  )
  match recovery_ledger_admits_password_write(site_ref: unfiled) {
    LedgerRefusal { cause: cause } => {
      let unavailable = RecoveryCensusDoesNotAdmit {
        kind: recovery_in_band_password_actuation(),
        site: unfiled,
        cause: cause,
      }
      let advice = recovery_route_next_action(unavailable: unavailable)
      // the operator is sent to the ledger, not to the hardware
      string_contains(s: advice as String, pattern: "fix the ledger")
        && string_contains(s: advice as String, pattern: "a_declaration_the_census_has_never_filed")
        // and the advice is not the one a missing rescue medium gets
        && advice != recovery_route_next_action(unavailable: NoReachableRescueHost {
             cause: RescueMediumNotExecuted,
           })
    }
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE PASSWORD GATE, CALLED. The admitted arm is the one the fold consumes, so this also proves the fold's
// input is what the gate actually emits. The refusal exercised here is a write of the WRONG KIND: the
// password gate is handed the reset's write record and must refuse, naming the arm it arrived in rather
// than emitting a generic sentence -- which is the mix-up the two separate authorization types make
// unwritable, checked from the outside.
test fn the_password_gate_admits_the_captured_channel_and_refuses_the_wrong_kind() -> Bool {
  let route = recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity())
  (match admit_recovery_in_band_password_write(route: route, write: recovery_in_band_write_record()) {
    PasswordWriteAdmitted { ticket: t } =>
      declaration_ref_eq(a: t.authorized_by, b: recovery_in_band_password_write_site())
    PasswordWriteRefused { cause: _ } => false
    PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
  })
    && (match admit_recovery_in_band_password_write(route: route, write: recovery_reset_write_record()) {
      // cause is NonEmptyStr, so a length test could never go red; what this conjunct rules out is the
      // OTHER two arms -- the wrong write handed to the gate must be refused, not admitted and not
      // bounced to the ledger
      PasswordWriteRefused { cause: _ } => true
      PasswordWriteAdmitted { ticket: _ } => false
      PasswordWriteRefusedByLedger { site: _, cause: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE LEDGER SAYS NO, AND THE REFUSAL IS NOT A SENTENCE ABOUT THE HARDWARE. Every recovery row in the
// census selects OperatorApprovedCapability, so the arm that refuses a REVOCABLE pattern could only be
// reached by mutation until now -- which is how a gate that stopped consulting the selection at all kept
// passing. This calls the reset gate's ledger question on a row the census really files for a different
// effect: `gunbc.fleet.hetzner_cloud_server_control.read` is a recurring reversible bindable read, so the
// selection answers FederatedScopedGrant, and an irreversible actuation may not stand behind a grant that
// can be withdrawn. It must come back LedgerRefusal naming THAT pattern -- never LedgerConforms, and never
// the CensusRowMissing arm (the row exists; a gate that looked up the wrong site would land there and this
// claim would go red, which is the point).
test fn the_reset_ledger_refuses_a_row_whose_selection_is_a_revocable_grant() -> Bool {
  let non_recovery_site = decl_ref(
    module_path: "gunbc.fleet.hetzner_cloud_server_control",
    decl_name: "read",
  )
  match recovery_ledger_selects_operator_approval(site_ref: non_recovery_site) {
    LedgerRefusal { cause: PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: selected } } =>
      // the refusal names the pattern it refused, and it is not the operator-approved one
      (pattern_identity_text(p: selected) as String) != "OperatorApprovedCapability"
        && string_non_empty(value: ledger_refusal_text(site_ref: non_recovery_site, cause: PatternIsNotOperatorApproved { selected: selected }))
    LedgerRefusal { cause: _ } => false
    LedgerConforms { verdict: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE CAPTURE TIMES HAVE REFERENTS OUTSIDE THIS FILE, AND EACH ASSERTS ONLY THE PRECISION THE ARTIFACT
// RECORDS. Every capture-backed field carries a band minted in the observing layer
// (gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_inband_recovery_receipts), so if a bound were a plausible-looking number for
// the wrong day every fixture here would still be self-consistent and nothing in this module would
// notice. The referents are 00_README.txt ("Fresh re-capture taken 2026-10-16 ~06:5xZ, AFTER the reset")
// for the reading and 02_inband_channel.txt's own `Oct 16 06:27 /dev/ipmi0` for the device node. Bounds
// are read back through the observing layer's captured_bound_utc_text, which goes through
// extdeps.clock's epoch_secs_of_millis and extdeps.units.iso8601_calendar's iso8601_utc_text -- functions
// other than the ones that built the bounds, so the two have to agree rather than one restating the
// other. The day is asserted exactly; the time only as the band the artifact records, because a tighter
// claim would itself be the fabrication this replaced: the value these bounds replaced decoded to
// 06:53:00.000, a reading nothing ever measured.
test fn the_capture_bands_read_back_as_the_times_the_artifact_records() -> Bool {
  // the reversed pair, bound once so the carrier and the mint are asked about THE SAME endpoints
  let reversed_low = captured_epoch_millisecond(
    day: recovery_capture_day(),
    hour: 6,
    minute: 59,
    second: 59,
  )
  let reversed_high = captured_epoch_millisecond(day: recovery_capture_day(), hour: 6, minute: 50, second: 0)
  let channel = captured_instant_band(instant: recovery_inband_channel_instant())
  let device_node = captured_instant_band(instant: recovery_device_node_creation_instant())
  let post_reset = captured_instant_band(instant: recovery_post_reset_instant())
  // both mints produced bands; neither folded to the inverted arm
  match channel {
    Present { value: b } =>
      // the README's ~06:5x band, whole: 06:50:00Z through 06:59:59Z -- and neither bound claims a
      // second it has
      captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_low(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:50:00Z"
        && captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_high(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:59:59Z"
    Absent => false
  }
    && (match device_node {
      Present { value: b } =>
        // 02's own `06:27`, to the minute it shows
        captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_low(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:27:00Z"
          && captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_high(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:27:59Z"
      Absent => false
    })
    && (match post_reset {
      Present { value: b } =>
        captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_low(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:50:00Z"
          && captured_bound_utc_text(bound: interval_high(interval: b)) == "2026-10-16T06:59:59Z"
      Absent => false
    })
    // A REVERSED PAIR REFUSES, IT IS NOT SWAPPED. The observing layer's fold is the only route to a
    // band, so this is what that fold does with an out-of-order pair: std.interval's own words, no band,
    // and -- by the type -- no absorbing plausible value. Asked of the real mint, not of a copy.
    && (match captured_band(
      low: captured_epoch_millisecond(
        day: recovery_capture_day(),
        hour: 6,
        minute: 59,
        second: 59,
      ),
      high: captured_epoch_millisecond(day: recovery_capture_day(), hour: 6, minute: 50, second: 0),
    ) {
      // NOT MERELY NON-EMPTY: equal to what the mint itself says about those endpoints. A check that
      // only asked for a non-empty string passed while a fold swapped the mint's sentence for a fixed
      // word, which is the same defect this claim's own comment forbids -- the carrier has to carry the
      // mint's words, so the claim asks the mint directly and compares. The mint's sentence is never
      // retyped here; a re-typed copy could go stale against std.interval and still pass.
      CaptureTimeBandInverted { reason: r } =>
        match closed_interval(low: reversed_low, high: reversed_high, le: (earlier, later) => earlier <= later) {
          IntervalRefused { reason: m } => r == m
          IntervalReady { interval: _ } => false
        }
      CaptureTimeBand { band: _ } => false
    })
}

// THE READING FOLLOWS THE RESET, AND THE DEVICE NODE PRECEDES IT. The one timestamp these
// facts used to share said the channel was read at 06:27, which put an observation of the post-reset
// factory state BEFORE the destructive write it is evidence about. Each fact now carries its own band,
// and what is asserted is the ORDER the artifact records: node created, then the reset, then the
// re-capture. Both halves are asked through captured_band_precedes -- the same fold the admission gate
// uses -- so this claim checks the gate's rule rather than a copy of it, and a band that failed to
// construct refuses instead of being rescued into a plausible number. With the old single shared stamp
// this claim was unfalsifiable, because a band is never earlier than itself.
test fn the_channel_reading_follows_the_reset_the_device_node_precedes_it() -> Bool {
  captured_band_precedes(
    earlier: recovery_device_node_creation_instant(),
    later: recovery_reset_instant(),
  )
    && captured_band_precedes(
      earlier: recovery_reset_instant(),
      later: recovery_inband_channel_instant(),
    )
}

// AN ADMISSION FOR ANOTHER CHASSIS, OR FOR ANOTHER WRITE, IS REFUSED BY THE FOLD (side-chat HOLD blocker
// 1). The sealed ticket is what makes this checkable at all: it carries the subject the gate read and the
// actuation it verified, so the fold can ask whether the authorization in hand is about THIS route and
// THIS write instead of assuming it. Both refusals are asked for by name, with the two mismatch fixtures,
// and each is checked on three things: the typed arm arrives, it names the write it is about, and it
// survives the projection as its own outgoing refusal rather than collapsing into "readback inconclusive"
// -- which is blocker 5's requirement applied to blocker 1's new arms. If either binding check were
// deleted, the mismatched admission would be accepted and this claim goes red; that is what the fixtures
// are for, and why a check with no fixture would not count as one.
test fn an_admission_bound_to_another_unit_or_another_operation_is_refused() -> Bool {
  let route = recovery_test_route(reading: recovery_measured_identity())
  let for_another_unit = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_password_admission_for_another_unit(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  let for_another_operation = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_password_admission_for_another_operation(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  // the admitted pair, as the control: the same fold with a ticket bound to THIS route hands off, so the
  // two refusals above cannot be what every call returns
  let control = converge_in_band_recovery(
    route: route,
    in_band_write: recovery_in_band_write_record(),
    reset_write: recovery_reset_write_record(),
    readback: recovery_factory_credential_readback(),
    in_band_admission: recovery_admitted_in_band_write(),
    reset_admission: recovery_admitted_reset_write(),
  )
  match for_another_unit.outcome {
    RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: k, cause: c } =>
      k == recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
        && string_non_empty(value: c)
        && (match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: for_another_unit) {
             // the outgoing refusal is the withheld-write one, carrying the same kind, and it is NOT the
             // generic readback answer
             Present { value: RecoveryWriteWithheldByAdmission { kind: pk, cause: _ } } =>
               pk == recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
             Present { value: _ } => false
             none => false
           })
        && (match for_another_unit.outcome {
             RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => false
             _ => true
           })
    _ => false
  }
    && (match for_another_operation.outcome {
      RecoveryWriteNotAdmitted { kind: k2, cause: c2 } =>
        k2 == recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
          && string_non_empty(value: c2)
          && (match recovery_route_unavailability(standing: for_another_operation) {
               Present { value: RecoveryWriteWithheldByAdmission { kind: pk2, cause: _ } } =>
                 pk2 == recovery_in_band_password_actuation()
               Present { value: _ } => false
               none => false
             })
      _ => false
    })
    && (match control.outcome {
      RecoveryRouteProofComplete { bound: _, proven: _, writes: _ } => true
      _ => false
    })
}

// THE DEVICE NODE IS COMPARED AS AN ARM, AND THE TEXT A CAPTURE SHOWS IS DERIVED FROM THE ARM (review
// 81285). Before this, the route held a NonEmptyStr and checked it against a copy of itself, so "same
// controller's queue" was a string fact. Now a second controller's queue is a DIFFERENT ARM OR A DIFFERENT
// NUMBER and nothing about it is textual. The two canonical texts are asserted against what the capture
// shows (02_inband_channel.txt: `crw------- 1 root root 235, 0 ... /dev/ipmi0`), so the derivation has an
// outside referent rather than being the definition of itself.
test fn a_device_node_is_the_same_device_only_when_the_arm_and_the_number_match() -> Bool {
  IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 0 } == IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 0 }
    // /dev/ipmi1 is a different controller's queue on a multi-controller host
    && (IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 0 } == IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 1 }) == false
    // and the IMB device is not an IPMI queue at all
    && (IpmiDevIpmiNode { device_number: 0 } == IpmiImbDeviceNode) == false
    && ipmi_device_node_canonical_text(node: recovery_ipmi_device_node) == "/dev/ipmi0"
    && ipmi_device_node_canonical_text(node: IpmiImbDeviceNode) == "/dev/imb"
}
